window.GC2=[["e533-will-ai-agents-replace-devs-or-just-10x-them-w-peter-bell","episode 533 - will ai agents replace devs or just 10x them w/ peter bell peter bell founder of gather.dev build proof of work with ai tools since tokens are cheap, even 20 bucks a month can show what you can do junior and senior engineers both benefit from pairing together while using ai agents, so dont skip hiring juniors engineering leaders now screen for agency, curiosity, proof of work and a passion for solving business problems over past credentials to stand out in a job search, build a small project for a specific hiring manager and reach out directly instead of only applying online pick a narrow niche at the intersection of two topics you care about so you become known for something specific rather than a generic developer ep 533"],["e532-get-promoted-w-o-being-the-loudest-in-the-room","episode 532 - get promoted w/o being the loudest in the room jennifer marcou leadership coach for introverts, author of lead with quiet confidence speaking up in meetings does not require being the loudest, asking one strategic question like what does success look like here can move things forward introverts often undersell wins so prepare stronger verbs for interviews, say led instead of managed and include specific outcomes your boss cannot reward work they do not know about so share a short weekly update on what you accomplished even if its not required at networking events set a small goal like talking to three people and prepare a few genuine questions ahead of time instead of winging it keep a running list of your wins, sometimes called a dig list, so you have real examples ready for reviews and promotion conversations ep 532"],["e531-ai-yap-ai-assistants-bots","episode 531 - ai & yap - ai assistants & bots   use ai assistants like instinct or muse to automatically search linkedin and x for people posting about layoffs so you can reach out and help them set up watches with your ai assistant to search job boards daily and surface roles that match what you are looking for try assistantbenchmark.com to compare different ai assistants before picking one to commit to start small with any ai bot setup and add a monitoring or spend tracking bot early so costs do not spiral have your ai assistant track your paychecks to confirm taxes and 401k are actually being withheld correctly ep 531"],["e530-dont-walk-away-from-ai-w-mark-hinkle","episode 530 - don't walk away from ai w/ mark hinkle mark hinkle founder of peripety labs and organizer of all things ai treat ai like a tool you actually drive, not a ferrari parked in the garage you never take out look for the easy wins first, like cutting down time spent in email or copy pasting between tools use one model to plan and a different model to check its work so mistakes get caught earlier ask yourself not just what ai can do but what you should actually hand off to it keep showing up to in person events since trust and empathy still get built face to face, not through a screen ep 530"],["e529-state-of-the-market-from-a-veteran-recruiter","episode 529 - state of the market from a veteran recruiter tony payne veteran recruiter and ai hiring systems builder tailor every single application since generic cvs no longer cut it with job volumes this high if you are not in the first 250 applicants your odds drop sharply so apply fast ask for feedback politely every time, even after just an application, not only after interviews take a contract role if it helps you grow, since permanent jobs are not more secure anyway put must-have skills like specific tools on your cv in context so ai screening tools match you correctly ep 529"],["e528-building-your-ai-job-search-system","episode 528 - building your ai job search system subhash data scientist use the company's own career site instead of job boards like glassdoor when applying match the exact wording from the job description including phrases like python framework not just python put unpaid projects under a dedicated projects section rather than work experience so it scrapes correctly test how an ats parses your resume by applying to a random job on that same system before applying for real give ai a clean yaml file of your experience instead of your formatted resume when generating tailored versions ep 528"],["e527-all-things-open-2026-the-current-state-of-the-tech-market","episode 527 - all things open 2026 - the current state of the tech market -   treat ai fluency as a baseline skill now, not a nice-to-have, across tech and non-tech roles alike stop reading from chatgpt scripts in interviews, interviewers can tell and it kills trust and credibility prepare with the star method as a thinking framework, not a memorized script, to keep answers clear under pressure network constantly since a large share of hires trace back to referrals, so build real human connections at events contribute to open source and write about what you build, visible work and thought leadership gets you noticed by hiring managers ep 527"],["e526-how-to-use-ai-to-navigate-offers","episode 526 - how to use ai to navigate offers   use ai to research market rate before accepting an offer by asking it what data sources and factors affect pay for your role ask ai for bullet points to guide a negotiation call instead of a full script so you dont sound robotic if the conversation shifts practice negotiation out loud using ai voice chat and ask it to critique you afterward on what you missed when comparing multiple offers ask ai to evaluate total value beyond base salary against your own career goals if equity is not mentioned in an offer ask specific questions about vesting shares and exercise windows before you negotiate further ep 526"],["e525-using-ai-for-mock-interviews","episode 525 - using ai for mock interviews   upload your resume into an ai project so it has context before you ask it questions ask ai what skills or tools are missing from your resume based on your actual day to day work use voice chat with ai to brain dump your background when you dont know how to start your resume run mock interviews with ai as the hiring manager and have it critique what landed and what was weak after each answer use the star method with ai by answering situation task action and result one at a time to get a polished answer ep 525"],["e524-using-ai-in-your-job-search-dms-cover-letters","episode 524 - using ai in your job search - dms & cover letters   use ai to build a company hit list by giving it your target role and the type of companies you want, like community-first or saas companies when reaching out to someone on linkedin, feed ai their profile and your background so it can find real commonalities for a dm, not generic flattery short dm's beat long ones, ai tends to write wordy messages so trim them down to something that doesn't feel salesy paste a job description into ai and ask honestly if it's a good fit for you, including salary, location and scope red flags for cover letters tell ai to skip the cliche opener, write three short paragraphs on why the company, why you're a fit, and what you'd do in the first 90 days, then edit it to sound like you before sending ep 524"],["e523-from-blockbusters-to-burnout-reinventing-work-with-steven","episode 523 - from blockbusters to burnout - reinventing work with steven puri steven puri founder of suka, former studio executive at fox and dreamworks find the best person in your field and get close to them, even if it means starting at the bottom just to watch and learn in interviews, research the company's actual gap and show how you fill it instead of just talking about your background don't trust free signals like waitlists or praise from friends, the only real proof is whether someone will pay fall in love with the problem you are solving, not with your first solution to it build a daily habit of picking one thing before you touch your phone that moves your life forward, and do it before bed for 30 days ep 523"],["e522-week-4-on-using-ai-to-make-over-your-linkedin","episode 522 - week 4 on using ai to make over your linkedin   use the prompt pressure test this as a hiring manager for your headline, about section, and experience bullets to find gaps ask ai for the top 15 to 20 keywords and phrases recruiters and linkedin algorithms search for in your target role and where to place them quantify your experience bullets with community or team size, growth rate, engagement, and retention numbers instead of vague claims give ai your full career history across roles and ask how to position yourself if your path zigzags between functions like recruiting and marketing upgrade from a free ai plan to a paid one only once you consistently hit your daily usage limit ep 522"],["e521-week-3-using-ai-to-write-your-resume","episode 521 - week 3 + using ai to write your resume   start your resume summary broad then get specific, leading with years of experience before diving into scope numbers are the biggest thing missing from most professional summaries so push ai to pressure test vague claims into real metrics ask ai to interview you with questions about scope, budget, team size, and business impact before writing bullet points use a project or dedicated workspace when job searching so ai keeps context and memory across your resume work run an ats pressure check prompt on your resume to catch empty skills sections and missing keywords before you apply ep 521"],["e520-build-your-target-company-hit-list-with-claude","episode 520 - build your target company hit list with claude   ask claude to act as a career coach and build a list of fifteen to twenty company types that fit your target role, including size, industry, and value turn that list into a tracked target list with tiers and priority status so you know where to focus your energy first before messaging anyone, ask claude to research the specific company so you understand their business, customers, and possible red flags use claude to draft an outreach dm, then punch it up with your own personality and details instead of copying it as is paste a real job description into claude to pressure test your fit, surface gaps, and generate interview questions to prep with ep 520"],["e519-use-claude-to-figure-out-what-job-you-actually-want","episode 519 - use claude to figure out what job you actually want   ask claude to figure out what role you actually want instead of just what you are qualified for start by asking claude to assume it knows nothing about you, then have it ask what it needs to build your job search background have claude ask you five to seven questions about your values, priorities and non-negotiables one at a time before moving on once you have a target role profile, paste in real job descriptions and ask claude to score the fit one to ten and flag yellow flags ask claude what job boards or niche communities fit your background since most people do not know where to start looking ep 519"],["e518-the-inner-workings-of-job-search-algorithms-w-subhash-jaini","episode 518 - the inner workings of job search algorithms w/ subhash jaini subhash jaini data scientist focus on real company career pages for applying, not job board listings like glassdoor match the exact keywords and phrasing from the job description, since synonyms like spring boot wonâ€™t count as java put unpaid work under a projects section rather than work experience so the ats handles overlapping dates better test how an ats parses your resume by applying to a random job on that same system before applying to ones you actually want track every application you submit so you can spot patterns like instant rejections tied to specific ats systems ep 518"],["e517-navigating-ai-and-your-career-all-things-ai-2026","episode 517 - navigating ai and your career - all things ai 2026   treat ai like the react shift and start learning it now instead of resisting the change use ai to tailor your resume to a job description instead of letting ai write it for you use ai to prep for interviews by researching the company and building out your star stories document what you are building and learning publicly so people start to recognize your name get into niche communities and discords since many hiring managers share jobs there instead of posting publicly ep 517"],["e516-latam-community-recap","episode 516 - latam community recap   show up in person at engineering conferences instead of only staffing events if you want to actually meet talent face to face free or low cost conferences tend to draw bigger and more engaged crowds than pricier ones small thoughtful swag or gestures can mean a lot to people, so don't underestimate the impact of showing up prepared consistent email and newsletter outreach compounds over time and helps grow an engaged community booking private dinners around conferences is a simple way to build deeper connections with your community ep 516"],["e515-community-always-wins-w-chris-demars-sr-dev-advocate-tuxcare","episode 515 - community always wins w/ chris demars, sr. dev advocate @ tuxcare chris demars sr. dev advocate at tuxcare when you get laid off, treat the job search as your full time job and start applying and telling your network immediately before interviewing, research whether a company actually understands the role they are hiring for or is just copying a competitor use interview conversations to disqualify roles that dont match what you need, dont just chase any offer after a good interview, follow up with a thank you plus extra materials like a portfolio, talks, and work samples so they can pass you along spread yourself across multiple communities, not just your own niche, since those connections can lead to referrals and job leads ep 515"],["e514-literally-writing-the-book-on-technical-interviews-w-david","episode 514 - literally writing the book on technical interviews w/ david mccarter david mccarter software engineer, author, and microsoft mvp prepare for interviews beyond reading the job description, since most candidates barely glance at it before showing up ask the interviewer questions too, since interviews are a two way conversation and you need to know if you actually want the job before accepting an offer, try to poke holes in it and even use ai to pressure test the details use ai to generate likely interview questions from a job description, but don't let it write your resume or code from scratch for you get into the habit of writing and releasing your own small projects regularly, since that builds the practical skills that separate you from ai ep 514"],["e513-everythings-broken-w-mike-peditto","episode 513 - everything's broken w/ mike peditto mike peditto recruiter, director of talent, and creator known online as realistic recruiting focus applications on roles where you are a true top match instead of mass applying to everything you are loosely qualified for skip the generic resume summary and use a clear headline at the top that instantly shows your role, skills, and industry fit make sure your most important skills and achievements appear in the first third of your resume since recruiters scan fast the ats is not an ai robot auto rejecting people, its a filing system where a human still searches and decides when reaching out to a recruiter or hiring manager, be specific about why you match that exact role instead of sending a generic message ep 513"],["e512-ai-resumes-ghost-jobs-the-state-of-the-market-w-sr-recruiter","episode 512 - ai resumes, ghost jobs, & the state of the market w/ sr. recruiter steven evans steven evans sr. recruiter, marathon consulting ghost jobs often aren't fake, they just stay open because companies keep hiring the same type of role on an ongoing basis if you applied and heard nothing, send a short direct follow-up email instead of assuming the job is dead shrinking your geographic search and showing willingness to be in office can make you feel lower risk to employers research the recruiter and company before an interview so you can build a real connection instead of a purely transactional chat on your resume, state tangible skills and years of experience up top and avoid vague lines like used react or used aws without context ep 512"],["e511-how-to-level-up-your-career-w-ai-nader-dabit-growth-at","episode 511 - how to level up your career w/ ai + nader dabit, growth at cognition nader dabit growth at cognition set aside about a hundred dollars a month to try bleeding edge ai tools instead of boxing yourself into one stack stop thinking of yourself as only a front end or back end developer and build a broader skill set including devops and design if your company blocks these tools build side projects on your own and set aside daily time to experiment look for sub billion dollar valuation startups growing fast instead of joining large legacy companies facing layoffs in person networking helps you stand out now that digital applications are flooded with ai generated resumes and cover letters ep 511"],["e510-ai-and-your-career-w-aaron-francis","episode 510 - ai and your career w/ aaron francis aaron francis  spend a small amount on a paid plan for an ai coding tool and use it daily for a month before judging it you are not too late to learn ai assisted engineering but you need to start now, even an hour a day use planning mode or an interview style conversation with the ai before letting it build, then validate the plan build something real and put it online, a live project beats a resume for showing self starter skills and ability to learn new tools at in person conferences focus on the conversations between talks, that is where real opportunities and connections happen ep 510"],["e509-how-to-tackle-salary-negotiations-w-john-gates","episode 509 - how to tackle salary negotiations w/ john gates john gates salary coach, former head of global recruiting for fortune 500 companies when asked for salary expectations early on, answer with a range instead of a single number so you dont get locked in low ask what the budget is for the position before giving your own number, then stay silent for several seconds after asking if salary is capped, negotiate other things like signing bonus, title, level, incentive milestones, remote work, or even insurance premiums position yourself as talking to other companies to create competition, since recruiters move faster and offer more when they fear losing you even when an offer looks good take time to review it collaboratively before accepting, since there is often more left on the table than you realize ep 509"],["e508-finding-your-next-job-w-recruiter-richie-lampani","episode 508 - finding your next job w/ recruiter richie lampani richie lampani recruiter and head of talent, rebel talent react and node are so saturated that agencies rarely get hired to find react developers anymore, so consider building skills elsewhere stay in touch with a recruiter about once a month, engage with their posts, and keep follow ups factual instead of emotional don't let ai fully write your resume since reviewers can spot the same generic phrasing, write it yourself and use ai to check for blind spots add a short italic description under unfamiliar company names on your resume so recruiters understand the context of your experience after a rejection thank the recruiter and ask if they know anyone else hiring, since recruiters often pass good candidates to each other ep 508"],["e507-what-to-do-if-you-were-laid-off-today-w-will-king-design","episode 507 - what to do if you were laid off today w/ will king, design engineer will king design engineer at snowflake if you get laid off, pick a few target companies and build something that shows you can do the job day one instead of mass applying hiring is a trust exercise so your goal is to make yourself the least risky choice a company can make use ai to speed up research and prep, not to generate your resume or project for you since that is easy to spot contributing real fixes to open source tools you actually use heavily can be a stronger signal than a cold application say yes to ambitious side projects while still employed since those unexpected efforts tend to open the most career doors ep 507"],["e506-navigating-recruiters-solo-show","episode 506 - navigating recruiters - solo show   treat recruiters as one of five pillars in your job search alongside networking, cold dms, job boards, and applying directly with internal recruiters at your target companies, connect on linkedin, engage with their posts, and build rapport before ever asking for help when an agency recruiter has no immediate role, ask how your resume stacks up, what to fix, how your salary expectations compare, and how often to follow up before trusting a recruiter who messages you about a job, ask how long they have worked the role, if a contract is in place, who the hiring manager is, and if they have placed people there before if multiple recruiters pitch you the same role, getting double submitted can get you rejected, so pick the one who can answer your questions fastest and ask about a right to represent ep 506"],["e505-interview-prep-solo-show","episode 505 - interview prep - solo show   research the company and be ready to explain why you are genuinely excited about that specific company, not just what they do find out exactly who is interviewing you and research them, and tie in any shared background like schools or interests before the interview ask why the position is open, what will be discussed, and how quickly they are looking to hire bring a notebook to interviews, tell the interviewer you are taking notes, and do not feel pressure to answer instantly after the interview ask about next steps, follow up in about 48 hours if they agree, and send a personalized thank you note on linkedin ep 505"],["e504-feeling-demotivated-in-your-career-and-how-to-fix-it-w-emma","episode 504 - feeling demotivated in your career and how to fix it w/ emma bostian emma bostian engineering manager at spotify before chasing the next promotion ask yourself why you actually want it and what the next level job really involves consider lateral moves like switching companies at the same level, trying product or engineering management, or contracting if the ladder up feels stuck figure out if you are running toward something new or running away from a bad situation before you make a career move in interviews admit what you do not know and show a growth mindset instead of pretending to have every answer use ai to understand code history and improve your skills rather than having it generate code you do not review before shipping ep 504"],["e503-how-to-answer-the-tell-me-about-yourself-solo-show","episode 503 - how to answer the 'tell me about yourself' - solo show   keep your tell me about yourself answer to about forty five seconds instead of rambling into your life story end your answer by asking if there is anything else they would like you to expound on so you land the plane instead of trailing off build multiple about me statements tailored to networking, a specific job, or general introductions research the company and interviewer so you can tie in relevant details even if you don't know the exact role practice on video by recording yourself answering the question to catch filler words and nervous habits ep 503"],["e502-how-to-find-a-job-on-linkedin-solo-show","episode 502 - how to find a job on linkedin - solo show   when a job posting lists required skills, mirror that exact language in your resume's professional summary search linkedin for your target role, then go to that company's page and click into people to find who to contact message someone a level below the founder, like a director, since they are more likely to respond than top leadership always click connect instead of follow, since follow does not open a direct way to message someone craft dms that name a specific connection point, state your fit for the role, and end by asking if they want your resume or who else to contact ep 502"],["e501-how-to-network-on-linkedin-solo-show","episode 501 - how to network on linkedin - solo show   post once a day on linkedin and document your journey instead of trying to post multiple times daily use a simple weekly rhythm of posts covering what you are doing at work, what you are stuck on, a win, and a piece of content you liked with your thoughts engage with posts through likes and thoughtful comments since it works like shaking hands at a meetup and helps the algorithm show you to more people when sending a connection request or dm always add a short personalized note with admiration or a specific shared detail, keep it to about three sentences, and end with a clear ask search your school or bootcamp alumni on linkedin and filter by city to find warm contacts who are more likely to respond and help with your job search ep 501"],["e500-lets-talk-about-job-boards-solo-show","episode 500 - let's talk about job boards - solo show   keep your resume active on job boards even when you are not actively job hunting dice rewards frequent logins by pushing your profile higher in search results for recruiters use chatgpt to find niche job boards specific to your role, like ai jobs or remote-only sites create a job-search specific email so you can track applications and filter out spam use job boards like dice to get found by recruiters, but apply to open roles directly on the company's career page ep 500"],["e499-recapping-wesbos-tweet-about-the-job-search-solo-show","episode 499 - recapping @wesbos tweet about the job search - solo show   referrals are now the single biggest way to cut through the noise in hiring, more than mass applying if a hiring manager sees you don't use ai tools at all, more companies are starting to skip you fundamentals still matter most, but pairing solid fundamentals with ai skills closes the gap fast auto-apply tools are flooding companies with applications, which is a big reason you may not hear back building a niche personal brand online can let you skip early interview rounds and go straight to senior decision makers ep 499"],["e498-how-to-create-your-linkedin-profile-solo-show","episode 498 - how to create your linkedin profile - solo show   use your linkedin banner to show a personal detail like a hobby so you look human and memorable, not just another ai-style profile pick a profile picture that is current, happy, and professional looking so you don't catfish someone who later meets you on a call put your specific skills and target job title in the words under your name since that section is what recruiters actually search write the first few sentences of your about section before the see more cutoff with real impact and keywords, since many people never click to expand it ask for linkedin recommendations directly from people you worked with since multiple recent recommendations help build trust the same way restaurant reviews do ep 498"],["e497-how-to-write-an-impactful-resume-solo-show","episode 497 - how to write an impactful resume - solo show   keep your resume header small, skip the address, and use a dedicated job search email instead of your personal inbox write a bullet pointed professional summary matching the job description's must have and nice to have skills so recruiters call you faster start every bullet with an action verb and make it quantitative, showing what you did, what tools you used, and the result add a technologies used bullet to each role so recruiters can keyword search and find you in applicant tracking systems keep formatting simple and boring with no colors or double columns, since content and results are what set a resume apart ep 497"],["e496-how-to-build-healthy-developer-culture-w-matt-stauffer-ceo","episode 496 - how to build healthy developer culture w/ matt stauffer, ceo of tighten matt stauffer ceo of tighten build healthy dev culture by applying empathy first, treating employees the way you would want to be treated watch for red flags when a company dismisses questions about work-life balance during the interview process try to talk to someone other than the hiring manager before accepting an offer to get a real sense of the culture when job hunting, research the hiring manager and company deeply since a generic resume sent to hundreds of openings gets deleted immediately if you want a promotion, ask your employer to clearly define the next role and the specific steps needed to get there ep 496"],["e495-how-to-land-your-next-promotion-w-kelly-vaughn-sr-eng-mgr","episode 495 - how to land your next promotion w/ kelly vaughn, sr. eng mgr kelly vaughn senior engineering manager at zapier understand that a promotion needs both personal readiness and an open business case, and you can only control the first one advocate for yourself by emailing or updating your manager regularly on what you accomplished that month, not just your job description tasks if your company has a formal promotion document, start filling it out a year ahead to spot gaps and build a record of your work when giving suggestions to leadership, present multiple options and clearly state which one you recommend and why to show ownership if you get rejected for a role or promotion, ask why, work on the gaps, and reapply later since a no now is not a no forever ep 495"],["e494-soft-skills-over-technical-skills-w-alex-berk-lead-engineer","episode 494 - soft skills over technical skills w/ alex berk, lead engineer alex berk lead engineer at shipium when you don't have much experience yet, lead with your network and your willingness to ask for help research the person interviewing you ahead of time and look for a real connection like hometown, school, or shared interests you cannot skip technical skills entirely, you still need enough to get past the first screening round keep building your own small projects between jobs, like an app or tool, so you always have something recent to show and talk about cold dming people on linkedin or twitter about roles or questions can lead directly to interviews, even without a warm intro ep 494"],["e493-pretending-to-have-the-job-before-you-get-it-w-josh-cirre","episode 493 - pretending to have the job before you get it w/ josh cirre, devrel @ laravel josh cirre devrel at laravel start doing the job you want before you have the title by making content or solving problems nobody asked you to solve build small side projects in different tools to find the stack that lets you ship fastest, then lean into that specialty going above and beyond usually means working on your own time outside your actual job description, not during work hours find out where your target companies and future coworkers actually hang out online instead of assuming everyone is on linkedin treat creating content as first round interviewing at scale rather than only applying to job listings ep 493"],["e492-the-state-of-ai-and-your-career-w-mark-thompson-sr-devrel","episode 492 - the state of ai and your career w/ mark thompson, sr. devrel @ google mark thompson sr. devrel @ google position yourself as an ai-empowered engineer, someone who uses ai tools to boost your work, not replace it fundamentals still matter because not every company allows ai tools and you need to know when the output is wrong build a portfolio project that looks polished and enterprise-level, modeled after a real app, then add an ai feature to stand out find a mentor and come prepared with specific questions, a focused fifteen minute ask gets better results than searching online alone show up to conferences and meetups and work in public since a strong network often leads to unexpected job opportunities ep 492"],["e491-a-very-analytical-approach-to-the-job-search-w-jerome","episode 491 - a very analytical approach to the job search w/ jerome hardaway jerome hardaway software engineer and founder at vetswhocode if you are senior keep your resume to the last ten years of experience since most strict background checks and ats systems only look back that far break a broad resume into focused versions by skill area so ats and recruiters can clearly see your expertise instead of a confusing mix use linkedin to talk publicly about what you know so your activity signals expertise and draws the right recruiters and opportunities to you keep your resume active on niche job boards and log in daily since staying visible can get you found by recruiters for roles you never applied to build real working projects you can fully explain in detail since being able to talk through decisions matters more than having a finished template ep 491"],["e490-finding-a-job-in-this-tough-job-market-w-barrett-blake-ms","episode 490 - finding a job in this tough job market w/ barrett blake ms mvp & architect barrett blake microsoft mvp and architect at nimble pros connect with two or three new people every time you watch a live show or attend a meetup to slowly build your network treat linkedin strictly as a professional space and skip posts about family or hot takes if you want it to work for you ignore recruiter messages that skip salary range and job details since real opportunities will include that upfront create content and document what you learn even if you think no one cares, since views and interest often build slowly over years getting any foot in the door, like support or qa roles, can lead to a dev job faster than waiting for your first dev offer ep 490"],["e489-how-to-navigate-internal-recruiters-w-principal-recruiter","episode 489 - how to navigate internal recruiters w/ principal recruiter aaron harmon aaron harmon principal recruiter at pdw performance drone works dont apply to roles where you clearly dont meet the stated requirements unless youre explaining the gap in a cover letter put must have keywords like specific languages or tools near the top of your resume so they are easy to find if youve done contract work or were laid off just say so directly on the resume instead of leaving recruiters to guess when following up with a recruiter stay short and polite and mention your name and the role so they can find you fast engage with a companys linkedin posts and follow their page since recruiters often check who is interacting before deciding who to reach out to ep 489"],["e488-finding-a-job-through-a-friend-and-a-dm-w-andrew-gilliland","episode 488 - finding a job through a friend and a dm w/ andrew gilliland andrew gilliland full stack engineer at elevate when job hunting, look at small agencies and niche shops since they take chances on people without a formal track record get in front of people face to face instead of only submitting applications online, it builds real connections faster starting a local meetup with no marketing can grow steadily and even lead directly to a job offer through a referral when juggling two interview processes, be upfront with the company you prefer instead of using offers just to negotiate use ai tools to learn unfamiliar tech by asking questions back and forth, but always validate what they tell you ep 488"],["e487-the-art-of-networking-internally-w-dania-luc-cyber-security","episode 487 - the art of networking...internally w/ dania luc, cyber security program manager dania luc cyber security program manager at digital realty keep a brag sheet of your projects so you can quickly explain impact when a manager or interviewer asks for specifics when interviewing, talk about what you personally did on a project, not just what the team accomplished research a company's financials and strategic focus before interviews so you can connect your skills to where they are headed build real relationships at internships and conferences since those connections can lead to referrals and support years later when networking for help, ask specific questions like requesting feedback on a resume or about particular job openings instead of a vague ask ep 487"],["e486-why-is-everyone-hiring-a-social-media-person","episode 486 - why is everyone hiring a social media person julia tech marketing and personal branding agency founder, taylor's friend more companies are hiring social media leads and chief content roles because brand perception is built on social, not traditional marketing if you hire someone to run your personal brand, treat it like hiring a clone of you, so screen carefully and build trust over time through freelance or trial work first give content creators a clear brief like make good videos instead of forcing scripted product pitches, then trust them to know the audience don't be scared of direct hot takes on linkedin, buzzwords and fluff make it harder for people to understand what you actually do a real community means people engaging with each other, not just a support channel or sales funnel disguised as community ep 486"],["e485-adhd-anxiety-to-public-speaking-w-will-klein","episode 485 - adhd & anxiety to public speaking w/ will klein will klein developer experience team at north start small by volunteering to speak at a local meetup instead of waiting until you feel ready if there is no meetup in your area start one with just a handful of people at a coffee shop or library rehearse your opening few minutes heavily since the first moments of a talk are the hardest part to get through use physical movement like pushups or a short walk to work through pre-talk adrenaline and nervous energy apply for roles that feel out of reach since getting rejected still teaches you what you need to learn next ep 485"],["e484-navigating-a-20-year-career-in-tech-w-sunil-pai","episode 484 - navigating a 20 year career in tech w/ sunil pai sunil pai principal engineer at cloudflare social media is adversarial so don't panic when it says everyone else has it figured out and you're behind the ai tech is solid but unfamiliar, so your job is to build daily habits of testing new tools, not chase hype being an absolutist about refusing to use ai tools can make you unemployable in a fast changing industry write about what you're learning in public, even badly, since showing curiosity builds your reputation over time before interviewing somewhere, try the product and send feedback or a quick demo directly to the hiring manager ep 484"],["e483-in-demand-skills-in-todays-market-w-founder-ken-schumacher","episode 483 - in demand skills in today's market w/ founder ken schumacher ken schumacher founder and ceo of ropes assessments built around real time hiring requests give a clearer read on in demand skills than lagging reports like dice or indeed some companies now want ai open assessments where candidates use tools like claude openly so hiring managers can see how fast they drive them junior engineers should lean fully into ai tools to learn faster, but still dig into why something works instead of only accepting the output list ai tools like claude or github copilot as a bullet point if central to a project, otherwise include them in a technologies used line standing out in todays market means doing visible work like finishing side projects, trying assessments, and sharing feedback instead of just polishing a resume ep 483"],["e482-how-you-should-hire-in-todays-market-w-engineering-manager","episode 482 - how you should hire in today's market w/ engineering manager nate taylor nate taylor engineering manager at skillable start job descriptions by figuring out what your team actually needs, not just a generic wish list pare down job descriptions to the few skills that truly matter instead of listing thirty requirements write and read your own job descriptions and resumes as the hiring manager since you know the team best write interview questions and example good and bad answers ahead of time so every candidate is judged consistently reject candidates quickly instead of dragging out rounds since candidates value a fast respectful no ep 482"],["e481-finding-a-job-in-this-terrible-market-w-principal-engineer","episode 481 - finding a job in this terrible market w/ principal engineer, jeff blankenburg jeff blankenburg principal developer advocate at dynatrace expect a real senior-level job search to take around six months, even with a strong network and reputation skip the apply button and go straight to leadership or peers on linkedin with a short customized note before applying when reaching out, ask for a specific small amount of time like fifteen or thirty minutes so busy people can say yes in interviews ask what success looks like a year from now and get the interviewer talking about themselves to build real connection do not take rejection personally, it is usually business and fit, not a judgment on your worth or skill ep 481"],["e480-tech-hiring-is-not-dead-no-matter-what-the-headlines-say","episode 480 - tech hiring is not dead, no matter what the headlines say   tech hiring is not dead despite headlines, with tech job postings up 16% year over year and it unemployment down to 2.8% big tech is no longer the standard for the market so look seriously at the startup ecosystem for opportunities ai job postings are up 7x so learn to use ai and research how to fold it into your workflow now instead of waiting join or help revive local tech meetups and online communities since that is where referrals and jobs actually come from use x to watch and learn, build in public, and get known for something specific so recruiters think of you first ep 480"],["e479-aaron-presley-on-leaving-nike-freelancing-finding-purpose","episode 479 - aaron presley on leaving nike, freelancing & finding purpose beyond code aaron presley independent consultant, localization and globalization expert, former nike localization architect before going independent get your budgeting in order since income will be inconsistent for a while consider starting small and local instead of chasing a unicorn startup idea networking is a muscle that gets easier the more you choose to show up and be social coding skill alone wont be your edge as ai makes writing code easier, so find what else sets you apart try contract to hire arrangements since short interview processes rarely reveal how someone will really work out ep 479"],["e478-from-yoga-teacher-to-director-of-engineering-harjis-deksnis","episode 478 - from yoga teacher to director of engineering — harjis deksnis’ nonlinear tech journey harjis deksnis director of engineering your career path does not need to be a straight line from school to a tech job to succeed genuine curiosity about how things work matters more than years of experience when applying for roles being able to learn fast and keep up with new tools matters more than already knowing everything for the role when interviewing, turn short answers into a real conversation and ask the interviewer what kind of depth they want go to industry conferences even if you don't learn much on the spot, since the inspiration and connections pay off later ep 478"],["e477-how-gilad-shoham-cracked-52-interviews-in-30-days","episode 477 - how gilad shoham cracked 52 interviews in 30 days gilad shoham leads the development team at bit.dev (bit.cloud) when running many interview processes at once, keep a daily log of questions asked, how it went, and how the company felt to you build a weighted scoring spreadsheet of what matters to you (tech stack, company size, product, fundraising) before you start job hunting, not during space out interviews with each company even if you have time, so you dont get a contract deadline forcing you to rush everything else before an interview, explore the product, find bugs, and prepare specific questions to show you did your homework when hiring top talent, screen for ego during interviews since even one ego-driven hire can hurt team productivity ep 477"],["e476-the-power-of-community-at-and-career-guidance-w-david","episode 476 - the power of community at torc and career guidance w/ david messinger, cto of torc david messinger  build your professional network intentionally, since strong connections open doors throughout your career pay attention to community involvement and contributions, as they can boost your visibility to recruiters showcase personal projects even without professional experience to prove skills in new areas find mentors and schedule recurring check-ins, even if meetings get rescheduled or spaced out ask the so-called dumb questions and put yourself out there, since most people are more willing to help than you expect ep 476"],["e475-career-and-community-talks-w-domitrius-clark","episode 475 - career and community talks w/ domitrius clark domitrius clark head of devrel at netlify take real time off between jobs to decide what you want next before you start applying ask interviewers directly why the role exists and what success looks like before you commit reaching back out to former coworkers for help is just as valuable as building brand new connections in person events help people remember you in a way that online presence alone cannot be upfront about how you work and what you expect, even if it costs you some offers ep 475"],["e474-from-layoff-to-job-in-19-days-w-sr-engineer-michael-eaton","episode 474 - from layoff to job in 19 days w/ sr. engineer michael eaton michael eaton senior engineer turn on the open to work signal immediately after a layoff instead of waiting around keep a simple tracker of every lead, referral source, recruiter, and interview stage so nothing slips through the cracks always follow up with recruiters and interviewers, even the ones who did not move forward, to leave a good impression build your network over years by staying in touch with former coworkers, speaking at events, and being active in online communities share what you know publicly so people come to you, rather than just consuming content and staying invisible ep 474"],["e473-how-to-stand-out-in-your-search-to-a-founder-w-nizar-zaher","episode 473 - how to stand out in your search to a founder w/ nizar zaher nizar zaher founder of mail zero skip the ai-generated resume and instead do real research on the company you're applying to find a way to dm the founder directly or meet them at a meetup instead of just submitting an application build something people actually use since that shows you can act like a founder, not just follow instructions post what you're building on x consistently so companies can see your work before you ever apply practice explaining your work out loud since communicating clearly is as valuable as the coding itself ep 473"],["e472-how-to-crush-the-application-and-interview-process-w-brad","episode 472 - how to crush the application and interview process w/ brad garropy brad garropy front end developer at cloudflare practice talking through your code out loud while solving problems, not just solving them silently record yourself coding and watch it back to catch nervous habits before real interviews ask interviewers about team rituals, planning, and performance reviews to spot real culture apply directly on company career pages instead of linkedin easy apply to stand out from the flood of applicants keep a tracker of every interview contact including shared background details to build rapport fast ep 472"],["e471-mindset-in-the-job-search-w-jasmine-vo","episode 471 - mindset in the job search w/ jasmine vo jasmine vo  after getting laid off, take time to actually process it instead of jumping straight back into work the next week treat a layoff as a transition, not a personal failure, since company funding and budget issues are rarely about you if you like structure, college can help, but self-paced routes like 100devs can get you into tech in a few months block off set hours each day to network and learn, just like you would treat a 9 to 5 job find a mentor early so you learn about career paths you don't know exist instead of just following the path others expect ep 471"],["e470-leaving-corporate-america-w-cto-david-fannin","episode 470 - leaving corporate america w/ cto david fannin david fannin cto and co-founder of kino look for product engineers who understand intent and solve problems, not just feature engineers who check boxes against acceptance criteria when hiring, test for competency plus humility, someone who can sit with a more junior person and still listen and learn make it a habit to interview elsewhere periodically so you know you are staying by choice, not because you are afraid to leave use ai to speed up busy work like summarizing user calls or stubbing out code, but still review and own the final work line by line career moves do not have to be a straight line up, sometimes a step that looks smaller gives you more room to grow and experiment ep 470"],["e469-community-building-from-a-dtc-brand-w-carl-maynard","episode 469 - community building from a dtc brand w/ carl maynard carl maynard lead of creative and community at freaks of nature join communities before you try to build one so you understand how to show up for people the best community leaders stay willing to be the least important person in the room and skip the credit consistency with the same people over time matters more than showing up once and moving on community cant be measured by a clean roi so dont force a data point onto every event let the brand or product sit in the background of the story instead of making it the main focus ep 469"],["e468-ai-the-job-search-w-engineering-leader-anna-mcdougall","episode 468 - ai & the job search w/ engineering leader anna mcdougall anna mcdougall engineering manager at hello better treat ai tools like any other part of your developer tooling that you need to keep learning and updating pick one ai agent or tool and get comfortable with it instead of jumping between many nobody needs to panic about falling behind on ai right now since most teams expect this to matter more in a year or two in technical interviews let candidates use whatever tools they normally would, including ai, to mimic a real first week on the job the real signal in interviews is whether a candidate can explain and justify what the ai helped them build, not just whether they finished the task ep 468"],["e467-talent-land-guadalajara-exposing-the-job-search","episode 467 - talent land - guadalajara - exposing the job search   write resume bullet points instead of long paragraphs since recruiters skim the first third of your resume include specific technical keywords in your resume because hiring managers often search for them directly quantify your work with real numbers like users supported or revenue impact instead of listing tasks before an interview find commonalities with the interviewer and research what problem the company is trying to solve always follow up after an interview since checking back in can turn a soft no into a yes ep 467"],["e466-how-to-land-a-cool-job-w-jordan-rogers-former-director-of","episode 466 - how to land a cool job w/ jordan rogers, former director of marketing at nike jordan rogers former director of marketing at nike focus on what you are, not what you are not, then build skills around your actual strengths volunteer and show up consistently at events for years if that is what it takes to break into a dream company learn emerging skills early like photography or video, since those extras often set you apart more than your resume when trying to land a role, ask what the team needs and how you can help instead of only talking about yourself build a passion project outside your day job, since initiative and effort often matter more than a polished resume ep 466"],["e465-beyond-the-match-how-is-redefining-the-talent-industry","episode 465 - beyond the match: how torc is redefining the talent industry mike morris ceo and co-founder identify your mentors early and set up regular check-ins with them so the relationship lasts for years build a profile that pulls live data from places like linkedin and github instead of relying on a static resume ai should give recruiters a sidekick for manual work, not replace the human relationship with candidates specializing in a specific skill set makes it easier to get matched to the right opportunities than staying a generalist look for places built around a curated community of vetted talent instead of just sending resumes into a pile ep 465"],["e464-hiring-trends-and-the-power-of-community-w-andy-hutsell","episode 464 - hiring trends and the power of community w/ andy hutsell andy hutsell leads permanent placement sales at ronstad digital core tech skills like system engineering, network engineering, and devops are still in demand despite ai buzzwords dominating headlines job seekers should expect different companies to want different things, from cover letters to ai-showcasing, so stay flexible and true to your style get your resume reviewed often since agency recruiters see macro market trends and can help you avoid overpricing yourself when reaching out to a recruiter for the first time, ask how they prefer to interact with talent instead of just asking for a job build a community of people to do life with professionally, since human connection and trust can't be replaced by any process or technology ep 464"],["e463-the-grueling-job-search-w-bradley-stokes-vp-of-it","episode 463 - the grueling job search w/ bradley stokes, vp of it bradley stokes vp of it, triple point avoid easy apply on linkedin and instead apply directly on the company career site to reduce the competition pool after making a connection, suggest coffee or lunch instead of just networking small talk at events build a two column document mapping job requirements against your own experience and send it proactively stay active and transparent about your job search on linkedin so your network can support and advocate for you always send a genuine thank you or close out note even after rejection since the industry community is small ep 463"],["e462-ai-and-the-job-search-w-vp-of-eng-andrew-moore","episode 462 - ai and the job search w/ vp of eng, andrew moore andrew moore vp of software engineering at a nationwide mortgage lender dont blame ai for hiring slowdowns that are really just a tough economy and fewer openings junior devs should still learn core fundamentals so they can vet and explain whatever ai generates for them in big regulated companies ai coding tools are often banned or far from mainstream so dont assume everyone uses them in fast moving startups speed and revenue matter more so being hands on with ai tools can help you move quicker make your resume and cover letter sound like you since generic ai written content is obvious and frustrating to read ep 462"],["e461-how-to-get-a-job-off-of-x-twitter-w-staff-eng-jacob-evans","episode 461 - how to get a job off of x/twitter w/ staff eng, jacob evans jacob evans staff engineer at wop.com treat tech twitter as a place for genuine connection, not just memes or drama find mentors by engaging publicly with their posts instead of dming asking for mentorship build your network by finding shared interests outside of tech like hobbies or background when a post gets traction, be ready to say yes to opportunities like flying out to meet a team job boards and easy apply can have a very low response rate, so build real connections elsewhere ep 461"],["e460-generalists-vs-specialists-where-do-we-go-from-here-w-pat","episode 460 - generalists vs. specialists where do we go from here w/ pat clarke, dir of eng pat clarke director of engineering at lumen learning having a specific specialty gets you in the door faster than being a broad generalist right now learn the underlying language, not just the framework, so you can handle problems outside your usual tools ask your manager for small backend tasks even if you are a front-end dev so you can ramp up before a big need hits build a secondary learning community of people whose content matches how you learn, not just courses keep networking even after you reach a leadership role since relationships often matter more than a perfect skill match ep 460"],["e459-starting-over-w-clark-sell","episode 459 - starting over w/ clark sell clark sell software engineer and entrepreneur, founder of unspecified after running a conference for 12 years, clark sell had to shut it down when ticket sales and sponsorships dropped post pandemic don't let your personal brand disappear while you pour everything into building something for everyone else your career will ebb and flow, not just go up and to the right, so know what work you actually enjoy stop waiting for your company to invest in you and start doing the work on yourself instead action creates momentum, so take a small step forward instead of waiting until you feel ready ep 459"],["e458-navigating-your-career-w-ai-as-a-junior-dev-w-will-king","episode 458 - navigating your career w/ ai as a junior dev w/ will king will king design engineer at crunchy data use ai as rocket fuel to level yourself up, not as a crutch that writes all your code for you pick specific companies you want to work for and build something that shows you understand their product, instead of mass applying when learning a new skill like ai tools, be able to explain how it makes you more valuable to a business before you invest time in it use ai to practice decision making by asking for three variations of a solution plus an argument for the best one build relationships early by saying hey to people you find interesting and sharing your work publicly instead of waiting until you need something ep 458"],["e457-staying-positive-in-your-job-search-w-bree-hall-developer","episode 457 - staying positive in your job search w/ bree hall, developer advocate bree hall developer advocate when job searching keep a running list of questions you missed in interviews and study those gaps before the next one a non-dev first job like client support can still build skills like empathy and real examples that help you land a dev role later mindset matters in a tough search, focus on doing one small improvement a day instead of trying to do everything at once don't be afraid to ask interviewers questions about the process or to negotiate things beyond salary like remote days or tools don't search alone, surround yourself with a positive community since comparing yourself to others online can hurt your confidence ep 457"],["e456-power-of-engaging-on-social-and-how-to-interview-with-a","episode 456 - power of engaging on social and how to interview with a startup w/ founder, rae lambert rae lambert founder, river treat posting on social media like a long game, small consistent posts compound more than chasing viral hits engineers benefit from learning go to market basics like understanding funnels, tracking, and why marketing work matters when interviewing with a startup, show you understand the founder's problem using a jobs to be done style framing build a small, curated community around shared interests or struggles instead of chasing huge follower counts when applying for jobs, record a short loom video introducing yourself and show side projects you built out of curiosity ep 456"],["e455-why-james-disappeared-from-the-internet-w-james-q-quick-head","episode 455 - why james disappeared from the internet w/ james q quick, head of dev exp james q quick head of developer experience at makeswift (bigcommerce) expect burnout from full time content creation even when you control your own schedule and income treat networking as showing up and investing in your community instead of cold introductions at a booth job leads can start from casual conversations in a lunch line rather than formal pitches at a booth volunteering at a conference can get you a free or discounted ticket while still letting you attend talks bring a friend to a conference so sitting down at a random table feels less intimidating ep 455"],["e454-devworld-2025-conference-talk-exposing-the-job-search","episode 454 - devworld 2025 conference talk - exposing the job search   put specific skills and years of experience at the top third of your resume so recruiters doing a keyword search actually find you ditch multi column resume formats since recruiters only spend seconds scanning and get confused by them network with one new person a day since job searches move through human connections more than mass applying use linkedin daily by documenting what you learn, liking and commenting on posts, and tagging people to build visibility follow up every few days during an interview process since staying top of mind matters more than worrying about annoying someone ep 454"],["e453-why-you-should-just-post-things-w-francesco-ciulla-sr-dev","episode 453 - why you should just post things w/ francesco ciulla, sr. dev rel francesco ciulla senior developer advocate at daily dev treat content creation like a long term commitment, try it for six months or a year before deciding it is not for you post consistently even when nobody is watching, the feedback and connections often come later than expected prioritize physical health and energy first, tired or stressed creators struggle to make good content people will judge you whether or not you post, so you might as well put your work out there build a backlog of content ideas so you can keep producing even during busy or low motivation periods ep 453"],["e452-that-conference-keynote-you-are-not-alone","episode 452 - that conference keynote - you are not alone   remember that feeling alone in the job search is common, even for people you admire on social media build your network before you need it by attending meetups, live streams, and conferences seek out a mentor and take ownership of driving that relationship instead of waiting to be guided push for regular one-on-ones with your manager since feeling heard reduces isolation at work as a leader, give feedback during interviews and onboarding since candidates remember how they were treated even after rejection ep 452"],["e451-the-blocking-tackling-of-finding-a-job-in-90-days-w-lawrence","episode 451 - the blocking & tackling of finding a job in 90 days w/ lawrence lockhart lawrence lockhart developer advocate at befree structure job search days around four buckets: body, spirit, brain and family, then block them on a calendar use a visual system like google calendar or a whiteboard with reminders since relying on memory alone does not work target applications instead of mass applying, researching each company and role before sending anything track every application, rejection and interview in a spreadsheet so you can learn from the data over time use ai tools like gemini to research companies and tailor a condensed resume from a longer master version ep 451"],["e450-jsconfmx-conference-talk-how-content-community-can-impact","episode 450 - jsconfmx conference talk - how content & community can impact your career   invite a couple of friends to something non technical like sports or a favorite restaurant instead of waiting for someone else to start a community post on linkedin once a day or every other day simply documenting what you are learning right now treat creating content as an ongoing first interview since hiring managers may follow your journey before ever talking to you after a conference block time to follow up with everyone you met since the follow up matters more than the event itself reach out to old contacts on linkedin even after many years since one message can lead directly to your next job ep 450"],["e449-being-nice-and-how-to-network-w-salma-alam-naylor","episode 449 - being nice and how to network w/ salma alam-naylor salma alam-naylor head of developer education at nordcraft treat networking as making friends, not a transaction, and build relationships slowly over time admitting you dont know something builds trust faster than pretending to know everything share your real life and vulnerabilities online, not just work content, to connect with people avoid chasing follower counts or engagement, since real opportunities come from genuine connections invest time in learning fundamentals instead of rushing to land a job in a few weeks ep 449"],["e448-all-things-open-conference-talk-exposing-the-job-search","episode 448 - all things open conference talk (exposing the job search process)   skip the one-page resume myth and let your resume breathe, two to three pages is fine remove outdated skills from your resume if you dont want to be contacted about them send a quick thank you message right after an interview instead of overthinking the timing research the company and the people interviewing you before the interview, most candidates skip this focus on depth not width in your job search, fewer intentional applications beat mass applying ep 448"],["e447-layoff-to-offer-letter-in-89-days-w-sr-dev-rel-lawrence","episode 447 - layoff to offer letter in 89 days w/ sr. dev rel, lawrence lockhart lawrence lockhart senior developer advocate at be free give yourself time after a layoff before applying so you make decisions from a calm place, not fight or flight write a smart goal for your job search with a clear timeline instead of a vague target like just get hired figure out your strengths and where you show up most competitively before you start applying anywhere share your impact publicly, not just daily coding reps, so companies can verify your skills before they even talk to you turn on open to work and build a strong linkedin profile so recruiters and hiring managers find you first ep 447"],["e446-how-to-hire-and-navigate-startup-culture-w-ceo-kevin-van","episode 446 - how to hire and navigate startup culture w/ ceo, kevin van gundy kevin van gundy ceo and founder of hypermode startups often hire from their open source communities, so small contributions like fixing a typo or improving a how-to guide can put you on their radar look at integration layers and how-to guides for open source contributions since core project areas usually require deep insider context de-risking a hire matters more for leadership roles since a bad vp can take months to replace and months more to undo the damage show genuine interest in a company by using their product or asking how the team works instead of only focusing on technical questions startups tend to favor people comfortable with ambiguity who can set their own goals and manage up rather than wait for direction ep 446"],["e445-career-switching-and-job-search-101-w-account-manager-jordyn","episode 445 - career switching and job search 101 w/ account manager, jordyn wallace jordyn wallace account manager internal recruiters hire only for their own company, while agency recruiters work across many clients, so their net is cast much wider talk to at least one agency recruiter and one internal recruiter a week, and come at a company you want from every angle you can when a recruiter posts a job on linkedin you can usually see who posted it, so go to their profile and reach out directly if a recruiter can't help right now, ask for one resume tip, one linkedin tip and how your salary ask compares to the market, then stay in touch switching careers is scary: commit fully, learn the basics of the roles around you, and let people tell you about their work ep 445"],["e444-how-ai-is-effecting-the-interviewing-w-head-of-dev-relations","episode 444 - how ai is effecting the interviewing w/ head of dev relations, angie jones angie jones head of developer relations, block treat ai use in interviews the same way you would on the job, since candidates will use it at work anyway when reviewing a coding exercise done with ai help, ask the candidate to explain what the ai generated and what they would fix or improve trying to secretly have ai answer interview questions for you is the real disqualifier, not ai use itself posting publicly about tools you use and problems you solve helps opportunities find you, even without likes or comments ask someone directly to refer you before applying, since a referral can help your resume get noticed among many applicants ep 444"],["e443-advice-from-a-hiring-manager-w-eng-manager-dan-digangi","episode 443 - advice from a hiring manager w/ eng manager dan digangi dan digangi engineering manager at active campaign treat freelance work like a real job on your resume, with specific projects and details, not vague summaries going from leadership back to an ic role is a lateral move, not a step down, if it fits your skills and goals long employment gaps matter less if you can explain them clearly, like taking time off intentionally use ai tools to research companies and competitors before interviews, not just to write for you read your resume or important writing out loud before sending it, it helps you catch what doesn't flow ep 443"],["e442-building-a-network-betting-on-yourself-w-co-founder-dee","episode 442 - building a network & betting on yourself w/ co-founder dee murthy dee murthy co-founder of ghost build your network slowly over years by consistently showing up, not by expecting instant payoff from one meeting at conferences introduce yourself, make a quick impression, and move on instead of over-pitching someone when you lose out on a role, how you respond matters since companies may circle back when timing is better do your homework before interviews and be easy to work with during scheduling, since friction signals how you will be to manage treat failure as a learning tool by taking accountability instead of blaming others for what went wrong ep 442"],["e441-how-to-hire-properly-w-brian-holt-staff-pm-neon-tech","episode 441 - how to hire properly w/ brian holt, staff pm @ neon.tech brian holt staff pm at neon.tech making a costly bad hire is worse than missing out on a good candidate, so slow down and be cautious negative signal is hard to spot in interviews because nervousness can look like a bad fit, so create a comfortable climate for candidates give candidates multiple prompts to choose from so they can show off the skills they feel strongest in let candidates use a real code editor and google things during interviews since that mirrors how engineers actually work research the company before an interview and come with real opinions or critiques about the product, not just praise ep 441"],["e440-solo-show","episode 440 - solo show   treat posting on linkedin like showing up to a meetup, do it consistently to build your network liking and commenting on posts is the digital version of shaking hands and introducing yourself keep linkedin posts short, three to five sentences, since most people have short attention spans when networking in person, ask simple questions like what do you do and what do you love about it, then move on connect on linkedin right after meeting someone and follow up afterward to make the relationship stick ep 440"],["e439-from-sales-to-first-engineering-job-at-netflix-w-shaundai","episode 439 - from sales to first engineering job at netflix w/ shaundai person shaundai person senior software engineer, ui engineer at netflix on the developer productivity team build internal relationships before you need them by genuinely connecting with people outside your immediate team post what you are learning publicly so a community forms around your progress and vouches for you later when messaging someone senior for networking, keep it short, specific, and ask for a clear small thing like advice on skills to build come to a networking call fully prepared with your own questions since you are asking for someone else's time use short personalized video in applications and thank you notes to stand out as a real person in a remote hiring process ep 439"],["e438-finding-a-job-through-community-w-appwrites-nishant-jain","episode 438 - finding a job through community w/ appwrites nishant jain nishant jain integration engineer at appwrite join a community early since it can expose you to opportunities and knowledge you did not know existed share your work in public through blogs and videos because small projects can catch a founder or hiring manager's attention hackathons help you bond with a team fast and simulate real work pressure in a short timeframe hackathons do not have to be complex or competitive, a simple idea with passion behind it can win and make an impact dont be afraid to dm people for one on one conversations since most people in tech communities genuinely want to help ep 438"],["e437-navigating-your-career-and-job-search-w-leon-noel","episode 437 - navigating your career and job search w/ leon noel leon noel founder of 100devs and resilient coders get an outside perspective on your resume and linkedin because you cannot smell your own weak spots put the most important info in the top third of your resume since most readers never get past it name the size and type of companies you worked for so non-technical recruiters understand your experience join a community so you can see peers getting interviews and jobs instead of sitting in a negativity bubble build real relationships through small consistent actions like commenting online or showing up to the same meetup repeatedly ep 437"],["e436-do-what-you-love-and-it-just-works-out-w-kevin-blanco-sr","episode 436 - do what you love and it just works out w/ kevin blanco, sr. devrel kevin blanco sr. developer relations advocate at appsmith build your career on community involvement, not just applying through hr or job boards show up and contribute to communities to help others first, not to immediately get a job out of it skip a formal degree in tech and instead spend that time and money building real projects and a portfolio invest in communication and soft skills since connecting with people can matter as much as technical ability in interviews keep applying to speak or contribute even after rejection, since consistency and showing up is what separates people over time ep 436"],["e435-the-power-of-putting-yourself-out-there-w-aaron-francis","episode 435 - the power of putting yourself out there w/ aaron francis aaron francis co-founder at tryhard studios lower the bar for what you share by posting a novel to you insight instead of waiting for a universal insight frame your posts as i did this instead of you should always do this so you avoid defending a position you cannot back up build content brain by writing down clever moments while you work so you never run out of things to share ask for the opportunity you want since the worst case is the same no but the best case opens a new door do your best work even when the audience is tiny because you never know who is watching or who they will tell later ep 435"],["e434-how-to-navigate-large-conferences-w-nick-taylor-dev-advocate","episode 434 - how to navigate large conferences w/ nick taylor, dev advocate nick taylor developer advocate at pomerium set a clear personal goal before a big conference or you will feel like you accomplished nothing smaller settings like dinners and intimate conversations build stronger connections than trying to meet everyone aim for a handful of intentional conversations rather than spreading yourself thin across thousands of attendees if company swag is cheap or scratchy skip it, people only wear high quality items that double as free promotion create content from talks you attend and tag the speaker, most speakers will repost or appreciate the shoutout ep 434"],["e433-the-winding-road-w-leslie-greenwood-co-founder-of-women","episode 433 - the winding road w/ leslie greenwood, co-founder of women wednesday leslie greenwood co-founder of women wednesday start networking two years before you need a new job, not when you're already searching before quitting a steady role, write an ideal life profile and build work around it instead of the reverse pick one channel where your actual audience spends time instead of spreading yourself across every social app people do business with people they trust, not with a company page, so build rapport and show up as yourself your career path can zigzag through layoffs and side steps and still lead somewhere good, so walk through open doors when you see them ep 433"],["e432-10-month-job-search-w-jeremy-barger-director-of-engineering","episode 432 - 10 month job search w/ jeremy barger, director of engineering jeremy barger director of engineering rewrite a resume that has not changed in years, since old formats stop getting callbacks even with strong experience do not be afraid to let a resume run two or three pages if it is tailored and highlights specific tech and accomplishments clean up your linkedin headline and attach skills to each job so recruiter searches for your title actually surface your profile get involved in online communities and groups even when not job hunting, since those relationships can lead to referrals and tips later follow up after every interview and rejection with a thank you, since hiring managers keep track of who to call first for the next opening ep 432"],["e431-what-to-do-when-you-are-laid-off-w-eng-leader-colin-lord","episode 431 - what to do when you are laid off w/ eng leader, colin lord colin lord senior manager of e-commerce engineering at carter's when you get laid off, take a day or two to grieve before diving into the job search handle practical items fast after a layoff, like filing for unemployment and signing up for health insurance through the marketplace before coverage gaps hit focus on networking and direct outreach through dms and texts rather than mass applying to job postings don't get emotionally attached to a role until you have a signed offer, since interviews can fall through for reasons outside your control be kind to yourself during a job search since rejection is not a reflection of your worth or skill as a person ep 431"],["e430-recruiter-turned-software-developer-w-brooke-sweedar","episode 430 - recruiter turned software developer w/ brooke sweedar brooke sweedar software engineer and bootcamp graduate, former executive recruiter start networking before you finish your bootcamp so you already have connections by graduation send personalized dms to local software engineers and bootcamp alumni to build common ground fast posting your learning journey publicly, even small projects, can attract support and opportunities from experienced professionals prepare a few genuine questions before coffee chats since most people enjoy talking about their own experience pick one programming language and build real projects before spreading yourself across many frameworks or tools ep 430"],["e429-hello-darkness-my-old-friend-w-devrel-expert-tessa-kriesel","episode 429 - hello darkness my old friend w/ devrel expert, tessa kriesel tessa kriesel developer relations expert and ceo at build4dev after a layoff, isolation made things worse, so make time to be around peers who get you split work into offensive tasks you love and defensive tasks like emails, then protect blocks of time for the offensive ones if you find yourself skipping workouts, that is a sign you are too busy and not prioritizing yourself a scheduling tool can fill your calendar with meetings fast, so set limits on when people can book you in person events build trust faster than virtual ones because people put themselves out there more face to face ep 429"],["e428-advancing-your-career-from-senior-to-leadership-w-principal","episode 428 - advancing your career from senior to leadership w/ principal engineer, stephen shary stephen shary principal software engineer at acv auctions use levels.fyi to compare technical career ladders across companies since titles like staff, principal, and fellow mean different things everywhere read the book staff engineer by will larson if youre a senior engineer wondering what comes next without going into management when moving into leadership roles aim to write reference implementations, the correct example others can copy, not just working code refresh your resume every january regardless of whether youre job searching so youre always ready to move as a leader get behind decisions once theyre made even if you disagreed, then fully learn why the choice was made ep 428"],["e427-the-tactical-part-of-community-w-pauline-narvas-community","episode 427 - the tactical part of community w/ pauline narvas, community @ vercel pauline narvas staff community engineer at vercel build one central hub for community conversations instead of scattering updates across every channel run regular live sessions or office hours so customers and internal people can show their faces and share what they are building consistency beats a single big push, post and engage on a repeating schedule across multiple channels when picking conferences, go where your actual users already gather, and ask customers directly where they spend their development budget meetups often beat large conferences for roi because they create direct relationships at a much smaller, cheaper scale ep 427"],["e426-community-chats-and-meetup-com-alternatives-w-founder-taz","episode 426 - community chats and meetup.com alternatives w/ founder, taz singh taz singh founder of guild treat community building like the creator economy, where the ecosystem pays through sponsors, not the organizers or members founders misalign incentives when they start a community just to hire developers, then let it die after one event mix event formats like tech talks, workshops, socials, and hack nights, each on its own natural schedule surround yourself with strong performers in a community since who you spend time with shapes how much you improve push any project past the easy first 90 percent, since the final 10 percent is where the real learning and growth happens ep 426"],["e425-start-showing-up-and-get-off-your-couch-w-kent-dodds","episode 425 - start showing up and get off your couch w/ kent dodds kent c. dodds teaches web development through epic web and epic react, runs epic web conf and epic web camp treat conferences as a tool to build real relationships, not just a place to pick up education you can get online if you want your company to send you to an event, frame it around solving a specific problem or finding talent, not just networking for yourself sponsors get more value from creative booth activities like giveaways than from passive setups where employees just sit at a laptop going to an event and paying your own way often makes you more engaged than when your company foots the bill build your professional bridge before you need it by showing up to meetups and conferences while things are still going well ep 425"],["e424-building-your-audience-and-going-independent-w-josh-comeau","episode 424 - building your audience and going independent w/ josh comeau josh comeau software developer and creator of online courses at joshwcomeau.com treat your time like a limited resource and spend it on work you actually care about start a blog just to document how you solved problems so you do not relearn them later build an audience and teaching experience before you try to create a paid course when reviewing feedback look for patterns across many people rather than reacting to one comment play to your natural strengths, like building and sharing work online, instead of forcing networking styles that do not fit you ep 424"],["e423-management-talks-w-jem-young-eng-leader-netflix","episode 423 - management talks w/ jem young, eng leader @ netflix jem young engineering manager at netflix believe in the power of the individual and invest in empowering people to grow hire and look for curiosity, since curious people proactively find and solve problems instead of just closing tickets make it safe to fail on your team, since failures teach more than successes ever do culture beats execution, but execution beats vision, so build trust and then get things done as a manager your job is to repeat the goal and priorities over and over until it feels boring ep 423"],["e422-how-content-can-land-you-a-job-w-chantastic","episode 422 - how content can land you a job w/ chantastic chantastic developer educator at workos document a layoff publicly if it feels right, since being open and helpful can resonate well beyond your immediate circle treat content as a trust-building exercise, not a highlight reel, since people hire who they trust making a specific video for a company you want to work for can get you noticed faster than a standard application it is okay to tell an interviewer a role is not the right fit for you, even at a company like netflix if you have any interest in video editing, pursue it, since developer content and storytelling skills are increasingly valuable in tech careers ep 422"],["e421-the-importance-of-value-in-your-career-w-staff-dev-advocate","episode 421 - the importance of value in your career w/ staff dev advocate, adewale abati adewale abati staff developer advocate at block focus on the value you create in a role, not just the code you write or tools you use document what you're doing for yourself first, then sharing it becomes less scary over time sharing your struggles and lessons publicly often resonates more than sharing polished wins getting involved in open source or community slacks solving problems can open doors you never expected in interviews, frame your projects around the value and impact they created, not just the tech stack used ep 421"],["e420-finding-a-job-as-a-director-of-engineering-w-brian-walter","episode 420 - finding a job as a director of engineering w/ brian walter, republic.com brian walter director of software engineering at republic expect a long search if you are a senior leader, one guest spent about 18 to 19 months finding his next role a spray and pray approach of mass applying on linkedin produced almost no responses, so pivot after a couple months if its not working joining a structured peer group that meets regularly can multiply your network and keep you accountable during a long search cold outreach rarely gets a reply, but a warm introduction from someone who knows and trusts you changes the odds completely always send a specific thank you note after interviews and keep following up with recruiters and old contacts over many months ep 420"],["e419-building-teams-exactly-how-you-want-w-hank-taylor-marketing","episode 419 - building teams exactly how you want w/ hank taylor, marketing at laravel hank taylor head of marketing at laravel ask whether equity is valued at fair market value or preferred price before accepting an offer understand that exercising options means paying the strike price to actually own your shares when negotiating an offer, treat it as a conversation between two people on the same side, not a battle look for teammates with the ability to learn new skills quickly rather than only specific past experience keep relationships alive by actually calling people instead of only messaging, since that effort compounds over time ep 419"],["e418-how-to-land-freelance-opportunities-w-hank-washington","episode 418 - how to land freelance opportunities w/ hank washington, founder hank washington founder, hank design studios build a visible body of work so clients can quickly see what you can do and want to hire you when selling yourself focus on diagnosing a client's real problem before pitching your service post consistently on linkedin since business decision makers live there, not on instagram or tiktok be easy to reach with fast dm response since opportunities can disappear as quickly as they appear sell your time and communication skills, not just your craft, to build long term client trust ep 418"],["e417-career-transition-into-software-engineering-w-chris-nowicki","episode 417 - career transition into software engineering w/ chris nowicki, eng lead chris nowicki engineering lead at this dot labs treat a coding bootcamp as the start, not the finish, since landing a job takes extra skills beyond the technical training pick one stack like react and next.js and go deep instead of spreading thin across many frameworks attend conferences and talk to people in person since real connections there can lead directly to job opportunities offer to help someone on a real project for free to build experience that goes beyond side projects build in public by writing about what you are learning, since those articles can stand in for job experience on a resume ep 417"],["e416-why-community-matters-w-staff-comm-mgr-at-vercel-pauline","episode 416 - why community matters w/ staff comm mgr at vercel, pauline narvas pauline narvas staff community manager at vercel treat community as its own discipline that touches growth, product, and engineering, not just marketing build community before you get the job by volunteering, teaching, or running your own online groups use a personal blog or portfolio site to document talks, posts, and projects so people can see your work over time put yourself out there in dms, comments, and events the same way you would advocate for a community, because no one else will do it for you stop hiding your authentic self at work, since showing up as you are opens doors instead of closing them ep 416"],["e415-doing-the-job-before-you-get-the-job-w-jason-torres","episode 415 - doing the job before you get the job w/ jason torres jason torres host of the tech commute, former filmmaker now in developer advocacy and job search earn the job by doing the work before you get hired, just like apprenticeships in film or trade industries build genuine relationships early so people are willing to help you when you actually need it lead interviews by asking questions and building rapport instead of waiting to be asked things if you keep losing out on roles for the same reason, change your approach instead of repeating it find a personal reason to keep going since that will outweigh the frustration of rejection ep 415"],["e414-promotion-by-layoff-w-ai-staff-eng-john-vandivier","episode 414 - promotion by layoff w/ ai staff eng, john vandivier john vandivier staff software engineer treat your job search like a full time job and track every application in a spreadsheet with a real human point of contact follow a rule of three, don't apply to more than three roles at one company so recruiters and referrers aren't confused use the snowball method, start interviewing at smaller companies to build confidence before tackling your dream tier one companies reach out for a referral before you apply, not after, and message multiple contacts at a company for better odds split your job search day evenly between applying, networking, and interview prep, then adjust weekly based on what results you're seeing ep 414"],["e413-xs-and-os-of-networking-solo-show","episode 413 - x's and o's of networking (solo show)   never message someone just because they posted a job, build rapport first around a shared topic use linkedin job search filtered to the past day, then check the company for shared school connections message people adjacent to a role about the skill or tool it mentions, not about the opening itself show up consistently in slack and discord communities and comment before you ever need something post on linkedin consistently and consider going live to interview people on topics you want to learn ep 413"],["e412-you-are-enough-w-motivational-speaker-reggie-ford","episode 412 - you are enough w/ motivational speaker reggie ford reggie ford motivational speaker notice when you chase awards or titles just to prove your worth to others instead of for real growth get honest feedback from people you trust, like a partner, close friend, or therapist, to build real self awareness practice sitting in silence for a few minutes without your phone, music, or tv to reconnect with your own thoughts if you get rejected after a good interview, remember rejection is often about timing or budget, not your worth give yourself permission to feel emotions instead of numbing out with social media scrolling after a tough day ep 412"],["e411-updates-on-the-job-market-w-managing-director-dan-thompson","episode 411 - updates on the job market w/ managing director, dan thompson dan thompson managing director expect software development hiring to pick back up over the next few months as companies still have real work piling up on your resume explain the problem you solved and how you built the solution instead of just listing tech jargon do not stuff your resume with every skill in the job description or interviewers will probe and catch inflated experience before an interview research the company's business and products and know your own resume well enough to discuss any bullet point test your tech, limit distractions, and show up as the person you would actually be in the office to make a strong impression ep 411"],["e410-thoughts-around-hiring-senior-staff-engineers-w-dir-of-eng","episode 410 - thoughts around hiring senior & staff engineers w/ dir of eng, harry wolff harry wolff director of engineering, identity and access management at mongodb get as clear as possible on what you want from a role before you start a senior or staff search treat job requirements as two buckets, technical skills and non technical traits like independent thinking and collaboration use pragmatic interview questions tied to real day to day work instead of memorization style puzzles always leave time at the end of interviews for candidate questions since that signals real curiosity and interest cold emailing a hiring manager rarely works, a referral or just applying through the recruiting team is the better path ep 410"],["e409-the-grass-isnt-always-greener-w-ad-slaton","episode 409 - the grass isn't always greener w/ ad slaton ad slaton senior director of software engineering, wbd staying long term at one company can come down to people, meaningful work, and real work-life balance keep an internal checklist to separate industry-wide problems from company-specific problems before deciding to leave have frank honest conversations with your manager about being unhappy before you decide to quit treat documentation as a core skill, writing things down helps new hires and global remote teams ramp up faster keep building on github and showing real work, not just talking about skills, since hiring managers actually look at it ep 409"],["e408-helping-people-w-kelly-vaughn-director-of-eng","episode 408 - helping people w/ kelly vaughn, director of eng kelly vaughn director of engineering stop using chatgpt to write your whole resume because recruiters can spot copy and pasted text word for word when following up after applying keep it short and genuine instead of sending a generic auto filled linkedin message write down bullet points before an interview so you dont blank, you only need to hit the key points not a perfect script its okay to pause for a second before answering an interview question instead of rushing into a response to get noticed reach out to the hiring manager or someone in your second level network instead of just applying and waiting ep 408"],["e407-how-to-send-a-cold-reach-out-solo-show","episode 407 - how to send a cold reach out (solo show)   check your current network first before cold messaging strangers since most jobs come through people you know always submit your resume to the job before dming the hiring manager so you can reference it keep cold dms short and specific, state who you are, your fit, and the clear ask research the person's profile for shared schools, interests, or posts to make a genuine connection before messaging follow up multiple times since people are busy and rarely respond on the first try ep 407"],["e406-why-everyone-in-tech-is-burnt-out-w-eng-leader-ryan-burgess","episode 406 - why everyone in tech is burnt out w/ eng leader, ryan burgess ryan burgess engineering leader, formerly at netflix, host of front end happy hour podcast companies running lean after layoffs means fewer people absorb more work without anyone officially increasing their load remote and hybrid work removed natural boundaries like commute time, making it harder to fully disconnect at the end of the day block off time on your calendar for lunch and short walks or it will get filled with meetings and messages if you are burnt out in your current job, try setting real boundaries for 30 days before deciding to quit journaling can help you realize you may not be as stressed as you think once you write down the reasons why ep 406"],["e405-the-importance-of-the-follow-up-w-liz-schwerd-sr-recruiter","episode 405 - the importance of the follow up w/ liz schwerd, sr. recruiter liz schwerd sr. recruiter, founder of a recruiting consulting firm apply to fewer jobs but follow up on each one, aiming for a higher hit rate instead of just blasting applications after applying, message the recruiter directly and include the job or rec id number so they can route you to the right person fast if you get no response after a cold application, give it a week or two before reaching out to the likely hiring manager, not before once a recruiter has actually contacted you, never jump over their head to the hiring manager, that move backfires when networking feels awkward, pick learning or industry events over pure networking mixers so conversation happens naturally around shared interest ep 405"],["e404-career-conversations-w-chris-gardner-eng-manger-accenture","episode 404 - career conversations w/ chris gardner, platform eng manger @ accenture chris gardner  blind applying can work but response rates are low so pair it with warm intros through friends and community show up and do the job you want before asking for the promotion since titles usually follow proven work when hiring, look past the resume and watch how a candidate talks through an unfamiliar problem out loud treating teammates well and asking good questions builds a reputation that gets you referred into new roles moving into management means stepping back from hands-on coding to focus on unblocking and developing your team ep 404"],["e403-landing-an-interview-solo-show","episode 403 - landing an interview (solo show)   mass applying to jobs right now rarely works because recruiters get flooded with applicants for a single role treat every linkedin visit like walking into a networking event and actually engage with people's posts rewrite your resume's professional summary to match the specific skills listed in each job posting when linkedin limits your dms, send a connection request with a short note instead to reach the top of their inbox if you're a new developer with no experience, offer to build something for a small business or creator to prove your skills ep 403"],["e402-leaving-meta-and-figuring-out-whats-next-w-keiyana-arnold","episode 402 - leaving meta and figuring out what's next w/ keiyana arnold deib strategist keiyana arnold deib strategist treat your personal brand as real security instead of relying only on a steady salary before quitting a job, reflect on your values and whether your day to day still aligns with them stand out in applications by creating content that shows your expertise instead of just applying and praying use a calendar to structure your day when job searching, including blocking time for basics like movement and rest lean on your network, a coach, or a therapist for support when making a big career transition ep 402"],["e401-your-strengths-in-the-job-search-w-vp-of-eng-jesse-gray","episode 401 - leveraging your strengths in the job search w/ vp of eng jesse gray jesse gray vp of engineering stop blasting applications everywhere and focus your energy on roles you actually want double down on your strengths instead of only trying to fix your weaknesses in interviews talk about what you personally did on a project, not just what the team did build a small real project, even something silly, to show practical skills beyond bootcamp coursework be easy to work with and stay engaged with your team, since that often matters more than raw output ep 401"],["e400-the-power-of-just-getting-started-w-torrance-evans","episode 400 - the power of just getting started w/ torrance evans torrance evans software engineer at jp morgan and chapter leader of black boys code in atlanta start small and let it grow, one networking newsletter for 10 people can turn into something much bigger when going to events it is fine to just listen and take notes instead of forcing an elevator pitch connect with people on linkedin right after meeting them so your activity keeps showing up in their feed without extra follow up messages narrow down what direction you want in tech early so networking conversations are more focused and useful look for ways to attend expensive conferences for free by volunteering or working the event instead of skipping it ep 400"],["e399-from-sr-ic-to-sr-leader-w-vp-of-eng-matt-burch","episode 399 - from sr. ic to sr. leader w/ vp of eng, matt burch matt burch vp of engineering be proactive with mentors instead of reactive, meet regularly so you go into new situations warned, not surprised practice concise communication so you are not forcing busy founders or executives to read through a wall of text give feedback early and often even when it feels risky, since avoiding it to protect morale is a bigger risk as a new leader avoid spending more than half your time on ic work, your job is empowering the team not just building do not assume your new boss needs the same things past managers did, ask questions and get expectations explicit early ep 399"],["e398-the-art-of-interviewing-company-edition-w-matt-walsh-co","episode 398 - the art of interviewing (company edition) w/ matt walsh co-founder of talgo.io matt walsh co-founder of talgo.io before interviewing anyone, write a document defining the five or six key results the role must achieve and the cultural traits needed to thrive as a candidate expect interviewers to let you talk for minutes without interrupting, so prepare a tight 90 second to three minute story and check in with them network your way into roles instead of relying on mass applications, since a trusted referral lets both sides be honest and saves time treat most requirements in job descriptions as a kitchen sink wishlist and apply even if you only meet about half of them if you want to move teams internally, outperform in your current role and ask both your manager and the target team what results they need to see from you over the next six months ep 398"],["e397-tactics-around-the-job-search-solo-show","episode 397 - tactics around the job search (solo show)   if you cannot land a developer role, consider qa, analyst, or customer support to get into a company and build business knowledge practice explaining what you do in under 90 seconds so interviewers get a clear, confident answer right away follow up quickly with recruiters, since slow responses can cost you the role to someone faster stay positive and energetic in interviews, since hiring managers can sense negativity and it hurts your chances join a slack or discord group with others in your field to share job leads and crowdsource the search ep 397"],["e396-balancing-life-with-kids-mobile-development-w-mazen-chami-sr","episode 396 - balancing life with kids & mobile development w/ mazen chami sr. mobile dev mazen chami senior software engineer at infinite red, co-host of react native radio switching to react native can make ios and android teams move faster by converging into one codebase expect close to high code reuse with react native but not 100 percent when features are os specific like apple health kit attending a conference talk directly helped solve a technical bug and build relationships that led to a job use a weekly and monthly standup with your partner to divide family responsibilities around work meetings feeling impostor syndrome after being given more responsibility is normal and usually means you are actually growing ep 396"],["e395-building-community-and-your-career-w-director-of-marketing","episode 395 - building community and your career w/director of marketing of renderatl brianna holmes marketing director at renderatl build community before you chase revenue, people can tell when a brand only wants their money right now your most authentic, unpolished content often converts better than polished ads because it reminds people you are human apply to jobs even if you do not feel qualified, sometimes a rejection turns into a role created just for you stay in touch with recruiters or hiring managers after a rejection, continued connection can lead to an opportunity later pick passion over profit early in your career, chasing security over what excites you can cost you time and impact ep 395"],["e394-how-to-write-a-resume-that-doesnt-suck-solo-show","episode 394 - how to write a resume that doesn't suck (solo show)   keep your name and contact info in the header, not buried further down the page use a 14 to 16 point font for your name, not an oversized font that wastes space list only email instead of phone number so you can track and manage responses in one place tailor your professional summary to match the required skills and years of experience in the job post start every bullet point with an action verb and make it quantitative instead of just listing tasks ep 394"],["e393-how-to-tackle-the-job-search-with-content-w-dan-digangi-eng","episode 393 - how to tackle the job search with content w/ dan digangi, eng manager dan digangi engineering manager at postmark (active campaign) during a layoff, double or triple your usual job search effort since timelines are running much longer than before inmail messages tend to feel spammy, so add a short direct note when sending a connection request instead when networking goes cold, try reaching out to people outside your immediate function like sales or product, not just engineering peers build a personal story bank of specific project details and accomplishments so you aren't caught off guard in interviews posting honest content about your layoff and job search can create unexpected connections, so put yourself out there even imperfectly ep 393"],["e392-trauma-in-the-workplace-w-christie-hoffman-founder-of","episode 392 - trauma in the workplace w/ christie hoffman, founder of engagement academy christie hoffman founder of engagement academy figure out your personal values so you have a framework for decisions instead of relying on a company that may not have any generic recognition like saying good job means little, teach managers to explain the specific impact an employee's work actually had burnout often comes from feeling you have no control over your goals, so set personal goals outside of work to regain a sense of progress pay attention to how you react when stressed or angry and ask if it mirrors a parent or caretaker, that awareness can change bad leadership habits small daily habits like walking, joining a gym even without a plan, and watching nutrition can meaningfully reduce anxiety and burnout ep 392"],["e391-gamecocks-recruiting-content-and-the-job-search-w-corey","episode 391 - gamecocks, recruiting, content, and the job search w/ corey jones corey jones  if you're stuck finding a developer role try adjacent paths like qa, support, or account management to build business knowledge first follow up on interviews and recruiter conversations since timing and responsiveness often beat being the most qualified candidate try the bcc hack by emailing a small group of recruiters you trust once a month with updates on your search and resume add a short hobby line to your resume since shared interests can create real connections with hiring managers stay pleasant and curious in interviews, even on tough questions, since energy and attitude are read closely right now ep 391"],["e390-ai-women-in-tech-and-your-career-isnt-linear-w-apurva-misra","episode 390 - ai, women in tech, and your career isn't linear w/ apurva misra, sr. ml eng apurva misra senior machine learning engineer and ai strategy consultant before investing in ai, get your data organized, cleaned up, and well maintained first remember generative ai is probabilistic not deterministic, so design interfaces that set expectations and collect user feedback like thumbs up or down dont force ai into every problem just because its popular, use it where it actually improves your day to day workflow a step back early in your career, like an unpaid internship exploring new fields, can set up the path that leads to your real passion dont take job rejections personally, most of the time it has nothing to do with you and expecting rejection can make it hurt less ep 390"],["e389-how-to-use-linkedin-to-find-a-job-solo-show","episode 389 - how to use linkedin to find a job (solo show)   make your linkedin title specific like \"senior .net developer\" since recruiters search and filter by exact job titles search linkedin jobs filtered to the past 24 hours daily to catch new postings before the pile of applicants grows before messaging a hiring manager, check your connections and school alumni at that company for a natural way to reach out personalize every connection request with a short human note naming the role and your top matching skills instead of a generic pitch like and comment on posts consistently so your name becomes recognizable to people who might later hire or refer you ep 389"],["e388-creating-a-job-that-does-not-exist-w-aaron-harmon-sr","episode 388 - creating a job that does not exist w/aaron harmon, sr. recruiter aaron harmon sr. recruiter file for unemployment benefits immediately after a layoff since the process can take weeks to kick in after panic applying for a few days, switch to a more strategic approach like targeting specific people instead of mass applying reach out to peers and individual contributors at target companies, not just talent acquisition managers, when networking for referrals personalize your outreach messages based on the person's role and how they communicate on linkedin, matching their tone accept help when people offer it during a job search, even small gestures like an introduction can open doors ep 388"],["e387-how-to-hire-and-get-hired-w-donnie-skalak-vp-of-engineering","episode 387 - how to hire and get hired w/ donnie skalak, vp of engineering donnie skalak vp of engineering target roles you actually want and tailor your resume and cover letter instead of mass applying to everything reach out directly to a recruiter or hiring manager on linkedin since a thoughtful dm can get your resume a second look local onsite candidates often compete against smaller applicant pools than fully remote roles so going onsite can set you apart if a company gives you a canned rejection or no feedback at all after an onsite interview thats a red flag about their culture write your own job descriptions as the hiring manager instead of leaving vague generic language to hr ep 387"],["e386-innovation-in-community-building-w-kham-ward-ceo-of-blk-men","episode 386 - innovation in community building w/kham ward, ceo of blk men in tech kham ward ceo of blk men in tech start small when building community, kham ward grew black men in tech from a single zoom call with friends checking in on each other use panels instead of solo talks so attendees hear multiple perspectives on the same topic like resumes or job searching build community outside of work topics first, trust and connection often matter more than technical conversation to improve diversity hiring, build a recruiting team that reflects who you want to hire and create representation at every level, not just top or bottom before asking someone for help or mentorship, show up consistently and add value first, then look for ways to collaborate over time ep 386"],["e385-capturing-attention-on-the-job-search-w-tom-redman-head-of","episode 385 - capturing attention on the job search w/tom redman, head of dev dx tom redman head of developer experience at convex before applying, dig deep into a company's founders, history, and culture so your outreach shows genuine understanding, not a generic pitch quality beats quantity in a job search, a handful of tailored, researched applications outperform mass applying when reaching out to a hiring manager, skip generic resumes and instead write specific bullet points on how you see the role and the opportunities if imposter syndrome hits in a new role, reach out directly to people you admire in that space and ask for their help, many will respond most people are self limiting in their job search, pushing past what you think is possible often reveals you can go much further ep 385"],["e384-are-you-tired-of-grinding-w-reid-evans-founder-consultant","episode 384 - are you tired of grinding? w/reid evans, founder/consultant reid evans founder/consultant treat grinding differently from doing deep work you actually enjoy, since nonstop busyness can mask burnout try getting genuinely bored on purpose, since stepping away from your phone can spark better ideas and conversations build financial runway and live below your means before leaving a full time job to go out on your own niche down into a specific skill set to stand out, since generalists get lost in a noisy hiring market use networking as a way to find people you genuinely want to work with, not everyone is your customer ep 384"],["e383-navigating-the-job-search-solo-show","episode 383 - navigating the job search (solo show)   build a savings runway since some folks are taking six to nine months to land their next job big tech layoffs are happening while startups and high growth companies are still hiring get plugged into your local startup ecosystem through entrepreneur centers and founder communities aim to have one to two real networking conversations a day during your job search check for common connections on linkedin before applying so someone can refer or vouch for you ep 383"],["e382-its-not-2019-anymore-w-anthony-mays-founder-of-morgan","episode 382 - it's not 2019 anymore....w/ anthony mays, founder of morgan latimer anthony mays founder of morgan latimer stop grinding leetcode alone and instead build real projects with one or two people so you have proof to show hiring managers self taught job seekers often explain their work better than bootcamp grads because they had to learn how to find and filter good information on their own when networking lead with how you solve a specific problem for someone instead of asking for a job if a hiring manager views your profile after you apply, ask specific questions rather than generic ones, and go through your recruiter if you already have one treat live streams, audio rooms and small communities as the new meetups and pick the format that fits how you like to communicate ep 382"],["e381-be-curious-and-be-urgent-w-sales-leader-chris-richardson","episode 381 - be curious and be urgent w/sales leader, chris richardson chris richardson former googler, cloud large deal pursuits team treat every job you've ever had as part of your story, even service industry roles teach empathy and reading people practice your elevator pitch on camera until you can vary it for different audiences and settings be authentic and use humor instead of sounding over rehearsed when speaking or networking before messaging someone on linkedin, research their background and reference something specific instead of a generic ask don't over focus on job description requirements, talk to people who have done the hiring to learn what really sets candidates apart ep 381"],["e380-lets-talk-about-finding-a-job-right-now-solo-show","episode 380 - let's talk about finding a job right now (solo show)   aim for two meaningful conversations a day during a job search, not just a handful per month when networking, ask people how they got into tech and request one piece of tangible advice if a call is going well, ask to be introduced to another person instead of cold messaging strangers treat job searching like marathon training by mixing networking, applications, social engagement, and outreach every day offer to build something free for a local business to gain portfolio work and real experience ep 380"],["e379-devrel-content-going-out-on-your-own-w-aaron-francis","episode 379 - devrel, content, going out on your own w/ aaron francis aaron francis software developer and content creator, formerly developer educator at planetscale publish your work consistently before you think it is time, since prior public work drives opportunities when you need them most after a layoff, look into health insurance marketplace tax credits with an independent agent before assuming coverage will be too expensive teaching an audience a useful skill is effective marketing because developers respond to helpful content, not traditional ads separate webinars and sales enablement videos from your main youtube channel content so they do not confuse the algorithm or audience when applying for a job, follow the stated instructions exactly and include links to public examples of your work to stand out ep 379"],["e378-dont-lose-yourself-invest-in-yourself-w-omri-buzi","episode 378 - don't lose yourself, invest in yourself w/ omri buzi omri buzi product manager start the transition to independent work by having conversations with no expectations, not waiting for opportunities to come to you treat going fractional or freelance as a trial, not a tattoo, since full time work will always be there if you go back stop merging your identity with your employer, what you do for a company is their business but what you do with your income is yours find affinity groups like alumni networks, scouting, or hobby communities since people respond more to shared connections if you worry about seeming too salesy when networking, that worry itself means you are unlikely to ever cross that line ep 378"],["e377-are-you-scared-of-programming-w-francesco-ciulla","episode 377 - are you scared of programming w/ francesco ciulla francesco ciulla developer advocate at daily.dev switching careers into tech later in life is doable, francesco started coding professionally around age 34 or 35 reset your mindset like a fresh casino bet, decide what you would do now if you were starting at 18 instead of being held back by past choices ai coding tools are powerful assists but still need a human expert to catch mistakes and handle unexpected issues like a major package problem rust is gaining attention for being as powerful as a low level language while simplifying developer experience, but its not recommended as a first programming language since tutorials often reference other languages stop worrying about what people think and get out of your comfort zone earlier, publishing work publicly matters more than waiting until you feel ready ep 377"],["e376-having-a-savings-account-and-being-healthy-in-2024-w-paul","episode 376 - having a savings account and being healthy in 2024 w/ paul sheriff paul sheriff consultant, mentor, and former software company owner build a simple savings split, cover living costs, put money into long-term investing, keep an emergency fund, and save some for fun spending use small tricks like round-up savings on bank accounts or automatic 401k contributions to build savings without thinking about it schedule real breaks in your workday for walking or light exercise since consistent movement helps mental clarity and long-term health grow your network by stepping outside your comfort zone through user groups, volunteering, writing, or speaking since those build real relationships stand out in a job search by communicating clearly, answering calls professionally, and always including your full name and contact info in emails ep 376"],["e375-living-life-to-the-fullest-w-alex-friedman-investor","episode 375 - living life to the fullest w/ alex friedman, investor & entrepreneur alex friedman investor and entrepreneur build small, vetted events instead of big flashy ones if you want real connection over brand awareness start content by talking to the version of yourself from years ago, not by proving your authority own your audience through things like a newsletter instead of relying only on borrowed social reach set clear work boundaries like fixed start and stop times so family and health dont get sacrificed for output practice gratitude on purpose and remember failing early and often makes rejection easier to handle later ep 375"],["e374-busy-is-a-4-letter-word-w-gant-laborde-cio-of-infinite-red","episode 374 - busy is a 4 letter word w/ gant laborde, cio of infinite red gant laborde cio of infinite red treat ai as a new power source for solving real problems, not just a trend to memorize models from focus on raising quality of life with technology instead of forcing it into a spot just to cut costs guard your yeses because every yes you give means saying no to twenty other things avoid back to back meetings since you carry fatigue from one into the next and give people a worse version of you act like a leader before you get the title and the title will usually follow ep 374"],["e373-its-all-fun-and-games-until-w-will-johnson-sr-dev-advocate","episode 373 - it's all fun and games until... w/ will johnson, sr dev advocate at auth0 will johnson sr developer advocate at auth0 get into tech by showing up at meetups even before you have a job title, it signals initiative more than a resume line build community by doing the work before you have the title, like moderating a discord or answering questions for free giving back without expecting anything in return, like sharing links or translating content, can open doors you never planned on devrel is shifting from chasing big numbers like conference attendance to bringing back real product feedback from users bond with people over shared interests outside of tech like sports or hobbies, those connections often turn into career opportunities ep 373"],["e372-the-state-of-devrel-and-recent-job-search-thoughts-w-sam","episode 372 - the state of devrel and recent job search thoughts w/ sam julien sam julien developer relations leader, starting a new devrel role at an enterprise generative ai company treat your job search like a job but set clear work blocks instead of grinding nonstop all day split your search day into focused time blocks so you still have room for family and rest sort your job search tasks into green time like interviews and applying, yellow time like resume and linkedin upkeep, and red time like busywork, then prioritize green time try to book two meetings or interviews every day during a search to keep momentum and feel progress build your network and give back before you need a job, since that groundwork pays off if you're ever laid off ep 372"],["e371-fractional-work-and-ai-w-reed-tomlinson-cto","episode 371 - fractional work and ai w/ reed tomlinson, cto reed tomlinson fractional cto treat fractional work as crisis response as much as strategy, since most calls come when something has already gone wrong stay indispensable and easy to work with, since that is what keeps you employed whether you are full time or on contract if you start contracting, update linkedin, get active on it, try marketplaces, and network in person or at co-working spaces differentiate yourself with niche subject matter knowledge plus coding skill instead of trying to be the best coder in the room when using ai coding tools, treat code review and understanding why code works as the real skill, not just accepting autocomplete ep 371"],["e370-giving-yourself-permission-w-matt-dolson-manager-at-deloitte","episode 370 - giving yourself permission w/ matt dolson, manager at deloitte matt dolson manager, human capital at deloitte look for ways to apprentice others or learn from someone with different experience, regardless of title or tenure if your company lacks an apprenticeship or mentorship program, give yourself permission to start something small instead of waiting for approval build in regular moments to celebrate wins on your team, no win is too small to acknowledge leadership is about actions and skills anyone can show up with daily, not a title you wait to receive before chasing a new role, get clear on your own values and who you are so you can tell if a culture fits ep 370"],["e369-diaries-from-the-job-search-w-chantastic","episode 369 - diaries from the job search w/ chantastic chantastic  build a savings runway since some job searches are taking six to nine months right now startups and high growth companies are still hiring even as big tech continues layoffs follow startup focused sources like product hunt and angel list and plug into your local entrepreneur community aim to have one to two real conversations a day during your search instead of just applying online before you apply check linkedin for mutual connections at the company and ask them for insight, not a reference, unless they truly know you ep 369"],["e368-finding-a-job-in-todays-market-w-kelly-vaughn-dir-of-eng","episode 368 - finding a job in today's market w/ kelly vaughn, dir of eng @ spot ai kelly vaughn director of engineering at spot ai mention that you already applied in your outreach message, but skip asking for 20 minutes of a hiring manager's time to sell yourself expect it to take a few messages over a few weeks before a hiring manager connects your name with your resume getting the company name wrong in a cover letter or message is a fast way to get passed over keep a senior resume to about two or three pages and focus on showing impact and results, not just duties always update your resume every quarter or during performance review season so you are ready if you lose your job unexpectedly ep 368"],["e367-the-power-of-content-w-ykdojo","episode 367 - the power of content w/ @ykdojo yk founder of cs dojo start building an online presence by sending thoughtful dms before worrying about full blown content post about what you did, like events or projects you built, rather than just sharing opinions when starting out keep your linkedin profile complete and active since recruiters favor accounts that post consistently tag people you meet at events in your posts to get engagement even with zero followers treat posting content like giving a small talk to hundreds of people instead of a one on one conversation ep 367"],["e366-professional-development-101-w-rhia-dixon-assoc-director-vml","episode 366 - professional development 101 w/ rhia dixon, assoc director, vml rhia dixon associate director, vml write your accomplishments in third person to turn feelings into facts you can actually share keep a brag book of screenshots from client praise and send them to your manager so they know your value get visible by writing internal blog posts, joining employee resource groups, and speaking up in open forums moving from mid level to senior is less about technical skill and more about aligning people to the why behind decisions use checklists and templates like pr templates so your successful process can be repeated and taught to others ep 366"],["e365-catching-up-on-brads-job-search-w-brad-garropy-sr-engineer","episode 365 - catching up on brad's job search w/ brad garropy sr engineer brad garropy sr engineer update your resume and linkedin on a regular schedule so you stay ready even before you start actively searching let your network know you are looking since people amplify your search more than they hand you job leads directly keep delivering at your current job while you search, then put in extra personal time in mornings and evenings build a document outlining your compensation history and benefits so negotiation conversations go smoother do not stay too long at a first job, aim for roughly two to five years to grow salary and experience faster ep 365"],["e364-band-to-bartender-to-developer-w-chance-strickland-sr","episode 364 - band to bartender to developer w/ chance strickland sr engineer at replo chance strickland sr engineer at replo treat early career moves like gigs you dont know how a show will go but you learn by talking to people and showing up when recruiter outreach dries up or picks back up thats a real signal for gauging how the job market is shifting layoffs hit mostly giant tech companies and unprofitable venture backed startups while smaller sustainable companies kept hiring quietly if youre early career expect to take opportunities that pop up instead of being picky since experience is what lets you get systematic later know your specific reason for leaving a role before you jump so you dont make a move without a clear why ep 364"],["e363-tips-on-how-to-sell-yourself-w-michael-hale","episode 363 - tips on how to sell yourself w/ michael hale michael hale project manager, office of mobility innovation, city of detroit figure out what you are genuinely good at and lead with that instead of copying someone else's style set a clear goal before walking into a networking event so you know who you are trying to meet let your personal style show up consistently since people remember details like shoes or glasses and it starts conversations find common ground with people by asking about something small they have, like an item or interest, to disarm them after a layoff, take a moment to ground yourself, then reach out to your existing network before searching for new contacts ep 363"],["e362-future-of-ai-and-transitioning-into-tech-w-rishab-kumar","episode 362 - future of ai and transitioning into tech w/ rishab kumar staff eng twilio rishab kumar staff engineer, twilio treat unpaid internships as a strategic move if they clearly lead toward a full time job offer go beyond assigned tasks during an internship to build trust and get included in bigger projects nail interview fundamentals like networking and system design since big tech cares more about core knowledge than specific tools practice soft skills by recording yourself talking through technical topics and reviewing the tapes over time when reaching out for a job, skip the fluff and directly explain how your skills solve the company's specific problem ep 362"],["e361-10-months-on-the-job-search-w-dev-advocate-valarie-regas","episode 361 - 10 months on the job search w/ dev advocate valarie regas valarie regas devrel enthusiast, former devops engineer waiting for a new job to bring happiness is a trap, decide to find joy now instead of putting life on hold building genuine self esteem matters more than just having thick skin when facing job search rejection submitting a mix of technical, hobby, and human impact talks to conferences shows what audiences are actually hungry for referrals alone will not get you an interview in this market, resume optimization only goes so far against every ats do not tie your self worth to your job title, salary, or what other people think of your work ep 361"],["e360-tactics-for-the-job-search-from-conversations-solo-show","episode 360 - tactics for the job search from conversations - solo show   stop mass applying and instead dm hiring managers directly with why you are a good fit for the specific role build a short mission statement that explains what you bring to the table so you can answer what do you do clearly give yourself time to breathe after a layoff since its a traumatic event and deciding things on pure emotion rarely goes well use a monthly bcc email to your favorite recruiters reminding them who you are and what you are looking for when a recruiter cant place you still ask for a resume tip, how you stack up on skills and salary, and other recruiter contacts ep 360"],["e359-job-search-q-a-w-dev-advocate-lawrence-lockhart","episode 359 - job search q&a w/ dev advocate lawrence lockhart lawrence lockhart dev advocate apply in high volume right now since competition is fierce and referrals help bump your application up in the stack consider adjacent roles like sys admin, qa, or business analyst as a strategic step toward the job you actually want build a portfolio with varied projects, including one full stack build with real backend logic, not three similar clones make at least one project personal and meaningful since passion for your own work signals you'll care about the employer's work get out of your head and network in small chunks, even as an introvert, since people often make the difference in landing a job ep 359"],["e358-from-college-student-to-speaking-at-conferences-w-dev","episode 358 - from college student to speaking at conferences w/ dev agrawal dev advocate dev agrawal dev advocate look for student organizations and university funding to get to conferences you otherwise could not afford volunteering at conferences gets you access to speaker dinners and parties that regular attendees never see post your projects publicly and tell people about them instead of just building quietly in the shadows submitting a conference talk proposal before the talk is finished can be treated as a chance to learn the topic reach out directly when a company shows a product demo you are genuinely excited about, it can lead to a job ep 358"],["e357-a-tough-last-year-so-lets-talk-about-it-w-david-neal-sr-dev","episode 357 - a tough last year so let's talk about it w/ david neal sr dev advocate david neal sr dev advocate expect interview requirements like unpaid take-home projects to sometimes be unreasonable and it is ok to decline them take real time off to sit down and figure out what actually brings you joy before jumping back into a job search if you are struggling after a layoff or tough year, consider talking to a counselor instead of pushing through alone make a list of companies you would actually love to work for and reach out directly, even if they have no posted openings getting involved in public speaking or community work builds skills and connections that can directly lead to future jobs ep 357"],["e356-the-possibility-of-leaving-tech-all-together-w-sr-dev","episode 356 - the possibility of leaving tech all together w/ sr dev advocate chris demars chris demars senior developer advocate, digitalocean expect job searching to feel like a second job, applying 10-15 roles a day when unemployed track every application in a notion or spreadsheet with role type, stage, and date applied be direct and specific in interviews about what you do and how you will benefit the company build a clear personal brand so people know what to expect from you at conferences and in interviews timing matters as much as skill, since hiring speed depends on company budgets, fiscal calendars and holidays ep 356"],["e355-why-you-should-consider-freelance-contracting-w-founder-dan","episode 355 - why you should consider freelance & contracting w/ founder dan mall dan mall founder, designer, and creative director build cash savings and in-demand skills before a layoff hits so you have options instead of desperation decide in advance what job or income you would accept if your main career path dried up, so you're not deciding under stress when freelancing, learn your clients buying cycles and plan slow months around them instead of fighting them get your first freelance clients through friends of friends and overflow work, then trade a great testimonial for future higher paying projects frame your freelance or contract work as crucial to a client's business, not a nice to have, so it survives budget cuts ep 355"],["e354-how-to-contribute-to-open-source-the-right-way-w-bekah","episode 354 - how to contribute to open source the right way w/ bekah weigel bekah weigel developer experience lead at opensauce start contributing to open source by checking the project's github and looking for a contributing.md file before asking questions read the docs and readme first, since answering questions you could have solved yourself burns out maintainers ask questions in the open issue or pr instead of dming maintainers directly, since transparency is the norm in open source pick open source issues that target skills missing from your resume, like documentation or a specific framework, to grow and get noticed leadership, clear communication, and mentoring others make people stand out and get recommended faster when job searching ep 354"],["e353-overcoming-fear-in-this-economy-w-shruti-kapoor-staff-eng-at","episode 353 - overcoming fear in this economy w/ shruti kapoor, staff eng at slack shruti kapoor staff engineer at slack start building your network inside your company before layoffs hit, then move those connections to linkedin ask coworkers for written or verbal recommendations now so you have them ready for future interviews prepare for front end interviews by studying state management, css, and interactivity instead of pure leetcode style questions ask recruiters whether an interview will focus on data structures and cs algorithms or practical javascript so you know how to prepare update your resume and collect project details now so you are not writing it emotionally after a layoff ep 353"],["e352-how-to-ace-an-interview-solo-show","episode 352 - how to ace an interview - solo show   bring printed resume and pen and paper to interviews so you are not distracted by typing on screen research the company and the people interviewing you so you can turn the interview into a conversation prepare three to four clear bullet points on your background tied directly to the company's specific needs quantify your accomplishments with real numbers and be ready to explain details, since vague claims get challenged after each interview keep a journal of what went well and what to improve to build your own study guide ep 352"],["e351-the-mastermind-of-networking-community-w-christiaan-heijmen","episode 351 - the mastermind of networking & community w/ christiaan heijmen christiaan heijmen executive recruiter check your zoom link and show up early, dont let tech issues undercut a strong candidate profile ask your interviewer or recruiter if your career transitions are making sense instead of guessing be open about compensation with your recruiter so they can advocate for the best offer on your behalf build networking habits by connecting people to each other instead of staying the only link between them bring your authentic energy to interviews since most of the impression comes from nonverbal communication ep 351"],["e350-the-benefits-of-changing-companies-w-james-q-quick-content","episode 350 - the benefits of changing companies w/ james q quick, content creator james q quick content creator when someone offers to help or says reach out, actually follow up since most people never do invest in your community through meetups, livestreams, and conferences instead of thinking of it as networking research salary ranges, required skills, and experience levels for target roles, then work to fill the gaps staying at one company can cap your salary and growth, so compare offers and be open to moving after a layoff give yourself grace and a day to breathe before starting to research and apply again ep 350"],["e349-climbing-the-career-ladder-w-tim-cheadle-vp-of-engineering","episode 349 - climbing the career ladder w/ tim cheadle, vp of engineering tim cheadle vp of engineering at binti a career ladder only works if both manager and employee agree on what success looks like at each level vague job descriptions are usually a sign leadership does not know what they actually want from the role promotion speed should match impact and risk, not just time served in a title for hiring, pattern recognition and real world experience matter more than which school issued the degree familiarity with the exact tech stack is a plus but companies with room to train will look past it for strong problem solvers ep 349"],["e348-people-and-values-w-ben-pyle-cto-of-curantis","episode 348 - people and values w/ ben pyle, cto of curantis ben pyle cto of curantis hire on attitude and aptitude, not just technical skill, since both can be taught but good character is harder to find reaching out directly to someone with hiring authority can pay off, even casually, if you show genuine skill and care executive coaching can create real change if you find a coach you connect with and you do the introspective work learn what makes your people's eyes light up, then put them in roles and projects that match those interests invest in yourself consistently through content and networking, since showing growth in public makes hiring easier ep 348"],["e347-talent-marketplaces-what-are-they-solo-show","episode 347 - talent marketplaces - what are they? - solo show   a talent marketplace gives you access to a pool of already vetted talent you can pick from instead of sourcing from scratch like a staffing agency does talent marketplaces mainly serve senior level roles while staffing agencies are more likely to place junior developers too ask any staffing recruiter if they are showing you every open role or only the ones that are fastest and easiest for them to fill when picking an agency focus on finding a good individual recruiter you vibe with rather than judging the company as a whole fix your resume formatting first since font choices bold text and font size issues cause more rejections than the actual content ep 347"],["e346-black-tech-pipeline-ai-and-learning-to-code-again-w-pariss","episode 346 - black tech pipeline ai and learning to code again w/ pariss athena pariss athena founder and ceo of black tech pipeline avoid resume designs with colors, logos, tables, or charts since ats systems can rank them low or skip them entirely use chatgpt to outline a resume draft but always review and personalize before submitting it anywhere bootcamps can offer a paid, equitable path into tech so research free or low cost options before taking on big student debt look for tech roles at small companies and consumer brands you already use by checking their careers pages directly once you land your first tech job focus on getting your foot in the door rather than chasing the biggest salary right away ep 346"],["e345-how-to-lead-without-being-in-management-w-kelly-vaughn","episode 345 - how to lead without being in management w/ kelly vaughn, director of eng kelly vaughn director of engineering think beyond your assigned task and consider the impact your work has on the wider organization amplify and support your teammates instead of only promoting your own work to build real influence ask for feedback often and be willing to accept it even when it is delivered poorly or bluntly work on genuine weaknesses like being defensive or abrasive instead of writing them off as just your personality build emotional intelligence by managing your own reactions and stepping away before responding in heated moments ep 345"],["e344-new-responsibilities-w-taylor-poindexter-eng-manager-at","episode 344 - new responsibilities w/ taylor poindexter, eng manager at spotify taylor poindexter engineering manager at spotify use your network to get introductions since that can push you to the front of the line in a crowded job market look beyond fang companies since plenty of non tech companies are hiring software engineers and tech roles research products you love and check sites like g2.com to find competitors and uncover roles you hadn't considered be gentle with yourself during a job search and take breaks since rest can help you interview better when reaching out to a hiring manager point to the specific role and explain why you fit instead of a generic cold message ep 344"],["e343-how-to-navigate-an-interview-solo-show","episode 343 - how to navigate an interview (solo show)   research the company and tie your own experiences or personal connection into why you care about their mission research your interviewers on linkedin and google so you can find common ground and shift the conversation bring a one page sizzle sheet with three to four bullet points connecting your background to the role keep a pen and paper in front of you to take notes instead of typing, since hiring managers find typing more distracting and suspicious print your resume, close extra tabs, and silence your phone so you stay focused and avoid blanking during the interview ep 343"],["e342-practicing-what-we-preach-w-eng-manager-julian-dasilva","episode 342 - practicing what we preach w/ eng manager julian dasilva julian dasilva engineering manager at misfits market reach out to your network first when starting a job search, including old coworkers and recruiters you know cold applying can still work but pair it with referrals and direct outreach to hiring managers on linkedin time block your job search, spend focused hours each evening on quality applications instead of mass applying research the actual people interviewing you, not just the job post, to tailor your examples to what they value in your first 90 days at a new job spend real time learning how people actually work before changing processes ep 342"],["e341-navigating-layoffs-side-hustles-portfolios-w-tyler-clark","episode 341 - navigating layoffs, side hustles, & portfolios w/ tyler clark, devrel at okta tyler clark devrel at okta build a portfolio by picking companies you want to work for and matching your projects to the tech stack in their job descriptions if you lack experience, get close to a dev team at your current job and ask them to vouch for you when a junior role opens in a small town with no local tech jobs, offer to build a website for a local business to get real portfolio experience ask your employer to fund conferences or learning subscriptions and use conference time to network with recruiters, not just attend keep interviewing even when you're happy in your job so you stay sharp and aren't caught off guard if you're laid off ep 341"],["e340-making-yourself-invaluable-w-annyce-davis-vp-of-eng-at","episode 340 - making yourself invaluable w/ annyce davis vp of eng at meetup annyce davis vp of engineering at meetup connect your resume bullets to business impact instead of just listing what tech you used if you cannot find direct revenue numbers, estimate using team salary and headcount as a rough guess become the go to person for a specific topic so leadership knows your name and what you handle give busy stakeholders a direct yes or no answer first, then offer more detail if they need it ask your manager how your work moved the needle so you have concrete impact to put on your resume ep 340"],["e339-the-art-of-relationship-building-w-jeff-torbeck-vp-of","episode 339 - the art of relationship building w/jeff torbeck, vp of revenue at gun.io jeff torbeck vp of revenue at gun.io treat networking as being of service to others rather than asking everyone for help small groups matter more than big ones, even 6 or 7 people at a meetup can build real relationships if there's no local community for your field, start one yourself with just a few people talk to people already in the role you want before chasing a title, then ask yourself why you actually want it post on linkedin about real interests and personal life, authenticity matters more than chasing likes or followers ep 339"],["e338-navigating-this-job-market-w-dan-thompson-managing-dir-at","episode 338 - navigating this job market w/ dan thompson, managing dir at vaco dan thompson managing director at vaco expect salary growth to stay flat for a while since the huge jumps of 2021 and 2022 were not sustainable if you negotiate near the top of a salary range be ready to explain why you are worth it, not just point to your old pay junior developers should build real experience through startups, hackathons, meetups, or helping small businesses with projects reach out to other juniors, mids, and seniors for advice since they get asked less often than managers and directors be purposeful in your job search by researching a company's actual products and services before interviewing, not just their website ep 338"],["e337-laid-off-twice-in-a-year-w-joe-previte-sr-engineer","episode 337 - laid off twice in a year w/ joe previte, sr. engineer joe previte senior full stack engineer at mercury after a layoff take time to let your head stop spinning before jumping back into the job search searching publicly after a layoff can open doors because people start coming to you with opportunities build a flexible daily routine that mixes interview calls with prep time and personal time so you do not burn out think about non-negotiable parameters like benefits, stability, and family leave before picking your next company size be careful about posting too much detail on failures or exact interview stages since companies can use that against you ep 337"],["e336-ai-coffee-and-leveling-up-your-career-w-robin-gupta-vp-of","episode 336 - ai, coffee, and leveling up your career w/robin gupta, vp of eng robin gupta vp of engineering treat prompt engineering like talking to a seven or eight year old, give clear but not overly detailed instructions on scripting languages aim copilot at small chunks of about five to seven lines of code for best results don't be a naysayer on ai tools, pro coder or no coder, this is the time to learn and experiment build a mentor mentee habit, be both a mentor and a mentee, and keep catch ups to roughly once every two weeks to start when stepping into a new or undefined role, spend the first 90 days having conversations to understand pain points before building solutions ep 336"],["e335-q-a-episode-solo-show","episode 335 - q&a episode (solo show)   if you are a front end heavy developer, start learning back end skills since back end talent is harder to find right now languages like python and java are in high demand for data processing, and messaging tools like kafka and rabbit mq are hot skills to pick up if someone does not reply to your first message, follow up again since recruiters often need two or three messages before responding networking does not require meeting everyone at an event, picking one or two people to intentionally connect with is enough record a short selfie style video introducing yourself and add it to the featured section on linkedin to stand out to recruiters ep 335"],["e334-tackling-interviews-w-vitor-oliveira-talent-success-manager","episode 334 - tackling interviews w/ vitor oliveira, talent success manager at gun.io vitor oliveira talent success manager at gun.io treat interviewing as a learning journey where you apply to many jobs, fail sometimes, and adjust rather than expecting one interview to land the role study three to five job descriptions for similar roles and mirror that language in your resume so recruiters see a fast match build a company research doc for your top target companies so you can explain what they do and why you want in answer tell me about yourself in under two minutes, tie it directly to the role, and find a personal connection with the interviewer use the star method, situation task action result, and always close with a measurable outcome when describing projects or challenges ep 334"],["e333-redefining-success-w-michael-hale-jr-project-manager-city-of","episode 333 - redefining success w/ michael hale jr, project manager city of detroit michael hale jr project manager, city of detroit figure out who you are and what you actually like before chasing a job someone else seems to love slow down and ask what makes you angry or excited since that points to real passion redefine success by asking who told you that version of success in the first place treat setbacks like at bats in baseball since just getting close counts and builds you toward wins before big decisions ask yourself a short list of personal questions to see if the opportunity truly fits your life ep 333"],["e332-importance-of-social-collaboration-w-ashley-narcisse-staff","episode 332 - importance of social collaboration w/ashley narcisse, staff swe @ apollo ashley narcisse staff software engineer at apollo hackathons are a fast way to build real skills because the time pressure forces you to learn and apply new tools quickly the most successful hackathon teams mix skill sets like design, business, and engineering instead of only technical people going to the same hackathons and conferences repeatedly builds a network effect that pays off later in referrals and job leads many companies hire based on relationships and referrals, so being visible in community spaces lowers the barrier to getting in wearing or doing something distinctive at events gives people an easy reason to start a conversation with you ep 332"],["e331-job-search-101-solo-show","episode 331 - job search 101 + solo show   if you have to take a customer service job to pay the bills, keep coding or building on the side so you have something to show later attention to detail matters more than ever, a typo or small mistake can get you rejected right now if you cannot land a technical role, look at customer success, marketing, or support roles inside tech companies as a way in before an interview, actually use the company's product or tool so you can speak to it instead of just talking about your own background follow up matters a lot, checking back in a week instead of waiting a month helped taylor turn a stalled offer into a job ep 331"],["e330-restaurant-management-to-coding-w-lawrence-lockhart-dev","episode 330 - restaurant management to coding w/lawrence lockhart, dev advocate at vaadin lawrence lockhart dev advocate at vaadin switching careers at 46 is not too late if you put in consistent study time outside work hours find a human mentor early instead of relying only on googling or self-study to avoid wasted years attend meetups even as a beginner to learn, observe, and build your personal brand before you have deep technical skills show up to events with curiosity and humility, ask open-ended questions, and let people talk about themselves post content consistently and mean what you say, since putting yourself out there can lead to unexpected speaking invites and career jumps ep 330"],["e329-back-to-the-basics-finding-a-job-solo-show","episode 329 - back to the basics (finding a job) + solo show   treat your resume and linkedin as the foundation everything else in your job search depends on log into job board sites once or twice a week so your profile resurfaces at the top of recruiter searches aim for two networking conversations a day, one brand new contact and one existing connection use the three pillars of a good dm: admiration, a specific time ask, and a clear question follow up consistently on applications since staying top of mind can change a hiring outcome ep 329"],["e328-are-you-content-w-mark-techson-sr-devrel-engineer-google","episode 328 - are you content w/ mark techson, sr. devrel engineer @ google mark techson sr. devrel engineer @ google wanting more money or a bigger title is fine as long as you want it for yourself, not because someone else has it success chased for its own sake can leave you unfulfilled since things like layoffs and market shifts are out of your control devrel work is mostly behind the scenes, with stage time and videos making up only a small fraction of the job to break into devrel, write blogs, contribute on github, help with docs and issues, and focus on serving the developer community you do not need a big twitter following to get into devrel, since other channels and ways of connecting matter just as much ep 328"],["e327-building-courses-and-reputation-w-josh-comeau","episode 327 - building courses and reputation w/ josh comeau josh comeau software developer and educator build an audience before you build a course so people already trust your teaching style expect a course to take far longer than planned, often years, not months start blogging or posting just to build your own reference and skills, not for an audience at first teach at a local coding bootcamp to learn where beginners actually get stuck treat your time and effort like a limited resource and spend it on work that serves your real goals ep 327"],["e326-how-to-excel-as-a-human-outside-of-coding-w-michael-eaton","episode 326 - how to excel as a human outside of coding w/ michael eaton michael eaton principal consultant at improving write things down daily, from quick notes to journaling, to externalize your memory and track decisions when you join a project without onboarding docs, create them so you leave the team better than you found it treat continuous learning as a daily habit, even in small 10 minute chunks between meetings share what you learn through blogging or quick team talks, since teaching something helps you learn it better use ai tools like github copilot as a helper similar to an intern, but double check outputs since they can be wrong ep 326"],["e325-state-of-the-economy-w-the-jobfather-jermaine-murray","episode 325 - state of the economy w/ the jobfather, jermaine murray jermaine murray career coach and technical recruiter known as the jobfather treat your goals as abstract but firm, stay flexible on the path you take to reach them a resume that is too long can hurt you, cut it down so a busy hiring manager will actually read it if you cannot land your target role right away, take an adjacent job like qa or business analyst that builds skills toward it look beyond typical tech companies and consider startups and older established companies that still rely heavily on tech stay flexible on relocation and contracting, and keep networking and building community to create opportunities ep 325"],["e324-triathalons-and-tech-w-vp-of-eng-brian-walter","episode 324 - triathalons and tech w/ vp of eng, brian walter brian walter vp of engineering use the equation stress plus rest equals growth to avoid burnout on high performing teams when a teammate is stressed, encourage them to take real time off so they return ready to perform job titles vary wildly between companies so tailor your resume title to match the role you are applying for networking beats mass applying, cold outreach works better when you ask for an introduction instead of a job in interviews prioritize team fit and communication over pure technical skill since technical ability can be taught ep 324"],["e323-the-balance-of-hustle-culture-w-bria-sullivan-cto-founder","episode 323 - the balance of hustle culture w/ bria sullivan, cto & founder bria sullivan cto and founder, honey bee games network at college events by waiting in line to talk to recruiters and staying in touch long term practice tech interviews out loud with a friend since explaining your thinking is a separate skill from coding watch for yes but responses in your job search as a sign it might be time to move on measure wealth by time with family and peace of mind, not just salary increases keep a mentor who is only one or two steps ahead of you, not five ep 323"],["e322-engineering-leader-on-the-job-search-w-dan-digangi","episode 322 - engineering leader on the job search w/ dan digangi, engineering manager dan digangi senior engineering manager expect the application process across different job sites and logins to be frustrating, and plan extra time for it if your usual network isn't producing leads right now, consider expanding it or approaching it differently instead of just mass applying avoid blasting your resume out hundreds of times, focus instead on real conversations with connections and connections of connections when emailing people to schedule time, give two to three specific days with clear time blocks so they can pick easily in interviews, ask what success looks like in the first 30, 60, and 90 days to show you're focused on outcomes ep 322"],["e321-navigating-change-w-frank-bach-frmr-principal-designer-of","episode 321 - navigating change w/ frank bach, frmr principal designer of headspace frank bach former principal designer of headspace keep a folder of screenshots showing praise or wins so you can fight imposter syndrome when you feel a dip if you stay camera off and hidden in remote work no one knows your impact when promotion time comes ask your manager for a trade off take on a less exciting project now in exchange for a more meaningful one later after a layoff take a beat before jumping into action mode then reach out to your network once you feel clearer headed being public about a layoff on social media can open up interviews, coffee chats and part time opportunities you would not expect ep 321"],["e320-the-importance-of-written-content-w-brian-morrison-educator","episode 320 - the importance of written content w/ brian morrison, educator at planetscale brian morrison educator at planetscale written content is often quicker to produce than video since you skip scripting, filming, and editing steps pick a content format based on how you want to consume it, reading is faster for learning while video works well as background absorption you dont have to start your tech career as a developer, networking and internal relationships can bridge you from it or other roles into software look for tasks you can automate or problems you can solve at any job, that extra effort builds reputation with management over time at conferences focus on meeting people over attending every session, keep introductions short and let the other person talk more than you do ep 320"],["e319-why-do-people-keep-doing-this-w-kelley-griggs-founder-of","episode 319 - why do people keep doing this w/ kelley griggs, founder of britepeer kelley griggs founder of britepeer and startup nash start a community around a shared interest even if your first goal is personal, like finding a job give a community a clear purpose and set expectations early so members know why they joined when networking on linkedin, accept connections based on shared interest or local ties rather than just tech vs non-tech pick meetup locations that feel different from an office and make sure parking is easy or people will not show up if you feel stuck in your career, try writing or creating content about an industry you care about to build connections ep 319"],["e318-the-power-of-being-present-w-teja-yenamandra-ceo-of-gun-io","episode 318 - the power of being present w/ teja yenamandra, ceo of gun.io teja yenamandra ceo of gun.io treat small mistakes as part of growth, the bigger risk is not pursuing an opportunity at all build trust with your manager through regular casual check ins, not just work talk if your workplace punishes every mistake harshly, treat that as a sign to look elsewhere when hiring, skills and resume are baseline, the real differentiator is the energy a person brings to the team in your career take risks big enough that a failure would really sting, smaller risk usually means you are underplaying it ep 318"],["e317-solo-show","episode 317 - solo show   treat cold applying as one tool in your job search, not the whole strategy, since hundreds of people often apply to the same posting if you have applied to 500 jobs with no results, change your approach instead of repeating the same thing follow up the day after a conference or meetup by connecting on linkedin and messaging people you met if networking feels uncomfortable, bring a friend to events and follow them around until you warm up for interviews, research the company and interviewer, review your resume against the job description, and prepare your own questions ep 317"],["e316-the-importance-of-taking-care-of-devs-w-vitor-oliveira","episode 316 - the importance of taking care of devs w/ vitor oliveira, talent @ gun.io vitor oliveira talent success manager at gun.io when giving feedback or bad news as a freelancer, show up honest and transparent instead of avoiding the conversation managing up means proactively updating your manager with your progress instead of waiting to be checked on in coaching sessions, let the other person talk most of the time so you can find the real root cause of a problem practice soft skills like active listening and clear communication in everyday life, not just at work build genuine rapport with clients since strong relationships are often what turn freelance work into full time offers ep 316"],["e315-what-is-out-there-for-free-w-julian-dasilva-software-mgr","episode 315 - what is out there for free w/ julian dasilva, software mgr, stem advocate julian dasilva senior software engineering manager, agtech, stem advocate tie your first coding project to something you already care about so the lessons stick better than a generic tutorial clone cold applying still works but spend most of your job search time networking instead of only sending applications reach out to strangers on linkedin since it is one of the few places that kind of outreach is considered normal in interviews set your ego aside and focus on being coachable since peer interviewers weigh heavily on hiring decisions bring notes into an interview and tell the interviewer ahead of time so it doesn't look like you're distracted ep 315"],["e314-level-up-your-career-w-david-pine-content-developer-at","episode 314 - level up your career w/ david pine, content developer at microsoft david pine content developer at microsoft start open source contributions small by browsing github.com and watching repositories before jumping into code check a repository's contributing markdown file first to learn exactly what maintainers expect from new contributors treat github activity as a networking tool since recruiters can use it to spot engaged developers at conferences spend real time in the hallway track asking questions and talking to people between sessions big tech roles often come from letting your passion show through speaking, blogging, and community involvement rather than chasing buzzwords ep 314"],["e313-the-importance-of-trust-w-ben-kettle-vp-of-revenue-at-gun-io","episode 313 - the importance of trust w/ ben kettle, vp of revenue at gun.io ben kettle vp of revenue at gun.io strong vetting builds trust, which shows up as rising client satisfaction scores and clients returning without reposting jobs companies often stick with the same hiring sources out of habit, so looking beyond the usual job boards can surface senior talent who are not actively job hunting years of experience matters less than being able to talk clearly and confidently about what you have actually built when interviewing, aim for a peer to peer conversation instead of a formal interrogation, the best interviews feel like two experts talking shop early career professionals should get good at articulating business problems and reasoning through tradeoffs, not just citing technical skills ep 313"],["e312-is-devrel-going-away-w-tracy-lee-ceo-of-this-dot-labs","episode 312 - is devrel going away w/ tracy lee, ceo of this dot labs tracy lee ceo of this dot labs a good engineer cares about the work and understands how it connects to business impact, not just collecting a paycheck communication is the most repeated trait of strong engineers, from explaining your work to building empathy with your team devrel is not going away but it needs time, sometimes a year or more, to prove value which clashes with short term business metrics before applying to a company, engage with people who work there on social media so you are not a stranger when you reach out follow up more than once when job searching, most busy people do not respond until the second or third message ep 312"],["e311-managing-your-strengths-w-engineering-manager-tiffany-jachja","episode 311 - managing your strengths w/engineering manager, tiffany jachja tiffany jachja engineering manager at autodesk know how you want to be remembered as a leader before you set goals, it shapes how you communicate and lead build confidence by finding people who will see your work for what it is, starting in spaces where you feel safe ask why you want more money or a bigger title, the real goal is usually what that unlocks for you, not the title itself get to know your natural strengths early and lean into them instead of spending energy fixing weaknesses as a junior person, lean into curiosity and show your learning process instead of trying to prove you already know everything ep 311"],["e310-too-much-powerpoint-not-enough-code-w-neil-chaudhuri","episode 310 - too much powerpoint not enough code w/neil chaudhuri, president of vidya software neil chaudhuri president of vidya software developer productivity is nearly impossible to measure fairly because work happens in teams, not in isolation leadership, not individual developers, is responsible for creating the conditions that let people focus and do good work you dont have to be passionate about your job to be good at it, you just need an environment that lets you get satisfaction from doing it well before going independent, make sure you have the skills, relationships, and safety net to cover healthcare and income if things dont work out when hiring globally, think through time zone overlap, language, culture, and security practices before assuming it will save money ep 310"],["e309-hiring-culture-and-breaking-into-tech-w-branden-thornton","episode 309 - hiring culture and breaking into tech w/ branden thornton branden thornton designer at uber, founder of project nine treat any job as tech adjacent since every industry has the same functions tech companies have, like marketing, finance, and engineering design is judged on what you can show, not just what you can do, so build a strong portfolio with real case studies instead of redesigning a popular app with no context, pick a real problem you notice and design a solution with a full case study networking and referrals matter more than blind applications when trying to break into big tech companies keep making genuine conversations and collecting contacts wherever you work now, since you never know where those relationships lead ep 309"],["e307-decentralization-interviewing-w-angie-jones-vp-of-developer","episode 307 - decentralization, interviewing w/ angie jones, vp of developer relations at block angie jones vp of developer relations at tbd referrals matter because they get your application a second look even in a market flooded with applicants use open source contributions like fixing a typo in docs to get your name noticed by hiring teams skip chatgpt for cover letters since hiring managers can tell and authenticity stands out more in interviews admit what you dont know rather than making something up, then ask to learn the answer invest in networking before you need it since who you know can open doors to roles and opportunities ep 307"],["e306-importance-of-public-speaking-w-staff-ui-eng-homer-gaines","episode 306 - importance of public speaking w/ staff ui eng, homer gaines homer gaines staff ui engineer, specializing in ui, ux and accessibility treat accessibility as part of ux, not a separate add-on, since every user still interacts with the same product learn accessibility fundamentals first, since tools and trends change but core principles stay the same pick a topic you can talk about for 10 to 15 minutes with zero prep as your entry point into public speaking expect burnout from the conference circuit and plan for the toll it takes on family and energy public speaking builds real relationships and opens doors, so start small with lightning talks and build up ep 306"],["e305-the-power-of-community-w-head-of-devrel-domitrius-clerk","episode 305 - the power of community w/head of devrel, domitrius clerk domitrius clerk head of developer relations at clerk stop building your community in slack since it is a business communication tool, not a community tool message history and real discussion threads are what make a community valuable, so pick tools that keep that searchable and indexable growth numbers mean nothing without engagement, so aim for a smaller group that actually talks to each other build trust by giving people honest answers even when it means pointing them to a different product or solution when starting out, prove your value to a community first through things like meetups or workshops before expecting anything back ep 305"],["e304-gun-io-devrel-team-w-vp-of-tech-deividi-silva","episode 304 - gun.io devrel team w/vp of tech, deividi silva deividi silva vp of technology at gun.io build your freelance profile with real impact details, not just a resume dump, since results-oriented info gets you noticed by clients expect a conversational approval interview focused on your career and technical judgment instead of coding tests or hackerrank style challenges strong communication matters most as a contractor since you are responsible for keeping client projects clear and on track from start to finish going freelance does not mean going alone, ongoing support from a team can help you navigate tough calls and trade offs on client projects stay curious and keep asking questions throughout your career, looking at the data yourself instead of being afraid to look uninformed ep 304"],["e303-the-funnel-of-networking-solo-show","episode 303 - the funnel of networking (solo show)   when you meet someone new, skip the long pitch and just ask their name and what they do find out which social app someone actually uses and connect with them there right away treat networking like a funnel: connect first, engage with their posts for months, then ask for something if you avoid social media, you need daily coffees or lunches instead to keep meeting people keep posting even when no one engages at first, it can take months before people start responding ep 303"],["e302-gun-io-mini-series-w-victoria-stahr-talent-growth","episode 302 - gun.io mini series w/ victoria stahr, talent growth victoria stahr talent growth, gun.io fill out your profile with real detail since good data in means better job matches out most rejections come down to years of experience or not having a focused, consistent tech background being a generalist is valuable in life but clients hiring for contract work want proven consistency in specific skill areas contract roles typically run three to six months or longer, with some engagements lasting well over a year start contributing to a 401k early, especially if your employer matches, even if it feels unnecessary when you're young ep 302"],["e301-networking-and-the-restaurant-industry-w-head-of-eng-seth","episode 301 - networking and the restaurant industry w/ head of eng, seth alexander seth alexander head of engineering skills from restaurant management like talking to upset customers and juggling priorities transfer directly into tech and project management long-term networking pays off years later so stay in touch with former coworkers even after you change jobs or industries joining a group chat with former coworkers after a layoff can provide job leads, salary info, and emotional support pick a few meetups or slack and discord communities and show up consistently instead of spreading yourself across many when you connect with someone after a podcast or meetup, include one line about why you want to connect so it is memorable ep 301"],["e300-big-tech-energy-this-morning-w-jarrett-albritton","episode 300 - big tech energy this morning w/ jarrett albritton jarrett albritton tech sales executive and host of big tech energy podcast when networking at events ask people what they are working on and how you can support them instead of pitching yourself first if you want someone's time offer to buy them a coffee or lunch over a virtual call to stand out from everyone else asking for favors developers moving into sales should target products built for development teams since they already understand the buyer's problems going after series a or b or c startups is an easier way to break into tech sales than competing for roles at the biggest companies referrals and tech conferences skip steps in the hiring process because a referred resume gets flagged and seen directly by the hiring manager ep 300"],["e299-fun-fri-tays-w-kelly-vaughn-director-of-eng-at-spotai","episode 299 - fun fri-tays w/kelly vaughn, director of eng at spotai kelly vaughn director of engineering at spotai every time you say yes to something you are saying no to something else, so protect your time with intention try the positive no by offering a smaller scope or a later date instead of a flat refusal figure out when you personally do your best thinking and push meetings to the other part of the day before taking a meeting ask what it is about so you can mentally prepare instead of walking in blind decide at the start of the week what you are simply not going to get to, since not everything can fit ep 299"],["e298-ai-side-hustles-and-the-future-of-sourcing-w-cyrus-harbin","episode 298 - ai, side hustles, and the future of sourcing w/ cyrus harbin cyrus harbin founder of tech is a new black, co-founder of optiply.ai you dont have to be a software engineer to work in tech, roles like sales engineering or solutions consulting can be a better fit if you struggle to break into coding fast, look at pre-sales or solutions engineering as a lane that still pays well ai tools can handle the administrative grind in job searching like tailoring resumes and tracking applications so you can focus on interviews when building new tools expect bugs and user confusion, build simple workarounds like templates while you fix the bigger issues post content consistently without chasing likes, value compounds over time just like results from consistent gym work ep 298"],["e297-accessibility-hobbies-and-the-job-search-w-chris-demars","episode 297 - accessibility, hobbies, and the job search w/ chris demars chris demars developer advocate in devrel focus on reaching the developer directly, not the company signing the checks track attribution with things like utm links so you can show which content or outreach drove engagement devrel results take time, impact from a single conversation may not show up for a year use erp (exposure and response therapy) style exposure and hobbies like fishing or paintball to manage work related anxiety and burnout when prepping for a tech interview find role specific question lists on github and bring your own questions since interviews go both ways ep 297"],["e296-remote-vs-onsite-where-are-we-heading-w-clark-sell-of-that","episode 296 - remote vs. onsite? where are we heading? w/clark sell of that conference clark sell that conference treat remote work like a skill, not a given, since covid exposed how many teams never built real remote processes invest in a decent webcam and mic for video calls, it is a cheap way to look professional without buying a suit go to conferences and meetups because in person connections often turn into real job leads faster than social media alone when networking or interviewing, give context beyond short answers so people have something to ask follow up questions about do not be afraid to change career paths, keep learning new skills since most careers last decades not just a few years ep 296"],["e295-hiring-interview-triathlons-and-the-braves-w-eng-mgr-colin","episode 295 - hiring, interview, triathlons, and the braves w/eng mgr colin lord colin lord engineering manager at smile direct club when hiring, look for people who stay calm and solve problems when production goes down instead of panicking or blaming others identifying the problem is the hardest part of any outage, fixing it is usually the easy part once you know what broke you do not need to match every bullet on a job posting, be honest about gaps and show willingness to learn good communication and humility set candidates apart more than technical skills, since technical skills are easier to teach during take home assessments, write comments explaining your thought process to show humility and communication even without live interaction ep 295"],["e294-all-things-devrel-w-rizel-scarlett-dev-advocate-at-github","episode 294 - all things devrel w/rizel scarlett, dev advocate at github rizel scarlett developer advocate at github devrel sits between the company and developers, showing how to use a product while bringing community feedback back to the product team developers trust real demos and live coding more than marketing claims, so show the product in action instead of just promoting it if you want to break into devrel, look for junior developer advocate roles and communities like devrel-focused discords or newsletters that post openings building in public, even posting basic learning notes like how you made a button in html, helps hiring managers see you understand the process think of brand as your online reputation, built by consistently sharing what you are learning rather than waiting until you feel like an expert ep 294"],["e293-slowing-down-w-aaron-nunley-frmr-director-of-engineering","episode 293 - slowing down w/aaron nunley frmr director of engineering aaron nunley former director of engineering treat a career break like a startup runway and know your burn rate before you stop working schedule leisure time on your calendar the same way you schedule workouts or meetings networking after you leave a job still counts, posting an update and reconnecting with old coworkers can surface opportunities build relationships with cross functional people like sales reps, not just engineers, since they often hear about openings first do not fall in love with a job opportunity before you finish interviewing or it can hurt your judgment and your negotiation ep 293"],["e292-state-of-the-market-how-to-stand-out","episode 292 - state of the market & how to stand out dan digangi software engineering manager, velocity team at activecampaign connect with every person you interview with, even after a rejection, since you could move up the list if other candidates fall through when job searching, reach out to second and third degree connections instead of only asking first degree contacts for referrals look beyond big name companies since startups often hire people with less experience and offer fast skill growth during interviews, turn the q&a into a conversation by tying your answers and questions back to your own relevant experience connect with recruiters at target companies with no note, then post content regularly so they see your work in their feed over time ep 292"],["e291-bad-advice-and-failed-dream-jobs-w-j-d-hillen","episode 291 - bad advice and failed dream jobs w/j.d. hillen j.d. hillen developer and speaker at garmin get good at explaining short job stints, especially in volatile industries like advertising where staffing up and down for clients is normal before quitting a job ask if the problem is industry-wide or specific to your company, since you will likely face industry problems again elsewhere landing your dream job does not guarantee happiness, so check in with yourself or a trusted person on whether you actually feel proud going to work build real connections by reaching out directly to people at conferences or companies you want to join, even if you start as a total stranger to them keep working toward a goal even after early rejection, since skills built over years can make you qualified for a role later ep 291"],["e290-mastering-the-technical-interviews-w-anthony-mays","episode 290 - mastering the technical interviews w/ anthony mays anthony mays software engineer and interview coach, creator of mastering technical interviews treat interviewing like a marathon by first figuring out exactly where the finish line is for each specific company and role remember the interview is not about you, it is about showing the company and interviewer what is in it for them stop focusing only on listing tools and technologies, instead show how you make money, save money, save time, or drive efficiency tailor your resume summary to each job by reflecting the specific skills and wording from the job description back at the top use chat gpt to reword your resume for a role, research a company's problems, and prep answers for behavioral interview questions ep 290"],["e289-the-power-of-social-media-and-notifications-w-jon-martini","episode 289 - the power of social media and notifications w/jon martini jon martini senior leader in tech, product, and marketing save searches on linkedin with specific titles, locations and onsite or remote filters so new postings trigger alerts right away skip checking experience level filters on linkedin since job posters rarely fill them out accurately message every single new linkedin connection with a genuine thank you to build a real network, not just a contact list post on linkedin regularly and track when your audience actually engages so your updates get seen build a daily routine with set work blocks and real breaks so a long job search does not wreck your mental health ep 289"],["e288-interviewing-and-recruiting-w-todd-libby","episode 288 - interviewing and recruiting w/todd libby todd libby web developer and accessibility specialist double check candidate names before reaching out since misspelling them kills credibility fast read the candidate's profile closely so your outreach matches their actual title and skills, not unrelated roles share interviewer names, interview format, and number of attendees ahead of time so candidates aren't surprised build accessibility into a project from the start rather than bolting it on at the end to save time and stress it is fine to say you don't know an answer in an interview as long as you explain how you would find it ep 288"],["e287-persevering-through-trauma-with-vulnerability-w-reggie-ford","episode 287 - persevering through trauma with vulnerability w/ reggie ford reggie ford author of perseverance through severe dysfunction, founder of rose creek wealth management dont compare your journey to someone elses because you never see their full backstory or the luck involved break big goals into daily habits like writing one page a day instead of trying to finish a whole book at once ambition means wanting to grow while desire is just wanting something, pair both to avoid complacency healthy growth sometimes means setting boundaries with people you love, even family, to protect your peace advocate for yourself early in your career instead of just accepting whatever offers come your way ep 287"],["e286-renderatl-ready-solo-show","episode 286 - renderatl ready (solo show)   before any conference decide your goal networking learning or just meeting people so you know how to spend your time pick your sessions ahead of time since you cant attend everything so be strategic about who you want to see live tweet or summarize talks you attend because speakers notice and remember people who create content for them schedule downtime at conferences since treating it like a marathon not a sprint helps you avoid burning out follow up with everyone you meet soon after the event because most people never do and it makes you stand out ep 286"],["e285-from-developer-to-product-w-jacob-herrington","episode 285 - from developer to product w/jacob herrington jacob herrington product manager, formerly engineer at startups including commsor, now at walmart switching from engineering to product doesn't require giving up strong compensation since product roles can pay just as well if you work closely with engineers, learn the basics of code so you can empathize and communicate about technical tradeoffs to validate an idea, show people a concept and watch their emotional reaction instead of directly asking if it has value moving from a startup to a large company means adjusting your sense of scale since small decisions can carry much bigger consequences learn to identify where value is created in your company and connect your own work directly to that value ep 285"],["e284-getting-to-where-your-going-w-michael-liendo","episode 284 - getting to where your going w/michael liendo michael liendo senior developer advocate at aws find your five people early because the influences you surround yourself with shape the direction your life and career take self-taught developers need community and mentorship to avoid falling through the cracks, whether through meetups or boot camps don't confuse education with a degree, boot camps and self-teaching can work depending on the field you want to enter job searching right now takes grit, since hiring is tougher than it was when tech was booming show up and let people know you're in the room, being memorable opens doors that staying quiet never will ep 284"],["e283-burnout-and-hanging-on-solo-show","episode 283 - burnout and hanging on (solo show)   set a hard stop time for your workday instead of letting work bleed into evenings get outside and move your body daily, even short walks or workouts help with burnout limit how many meetings you take in a day so you have mental energy left for family find people in your industry you can vent to since friends outside the field won't fully get it ask yourself honestly if you'd feel relieved or sad if you got laid off today, it tells you something ep 283"],["e282-grab-bag-episode-w-tessa-kriesel","episode 282 - grab bag episode w/tessa kriesel tessa kriesel  developers need about four to five touch points before they truly recognize and trust you plan social events early in a conference so people see you multiple times before the final days track every engagement in a tooling stack so you can show how devrel work contributed to a sale even if it was not the direct lead use birds of a feather sessions at conferences to find and connect with your specific niche audience build genuine relationships with developers instead of selling to them since they can tell when they are being pitched ep 282"],["e281-being-contentful-w-shauna-kimball","episode 281 - being contentful w/shauna kimball shauna kimball senior manager, diversity inclusion for recruiting programs and diversity initiatives at 2k, and founder of everybody eats separate whether a bad work situation is about the company, about you, or just the current moment before reacting keep a running list of what frustrates you at work alongside your wins so you can spot real patterns, not just one bad day when considering a new job, look at total offer like benefits, flexibility and stock, not just the cash bump weigh how much real influence and leadership buy-in a role offers before chasing a title or a bigger paycheck in a tough job market, remember rejections and delays are often about the industry, not a reflection of you personally ep 281"],["e280-laid-off-twice-in-a-year-now-what-gc-2-0-w-emmett-naughton","episode 280 - laid off twice in a year...now what? + gc 2.0 w/emmett naughton emmett naughton technical support engineer, creator of coder dads build genuine relationships on twitter by cheering on others and sharing what you find, not just self-promoting when job hunting, book coffee chats and interviews quickly so you stay top of mind with hiring managers expect some companies to pull job offers or close positions late in the process during tough markets read the room on salary negotiation, sometimes taking a job that keeps you in tech matters more than matching past pay a support or non-coding tech role can still keep you in the industry and valuable to a company ep 280"],["e279-building-vs-building-with-ceo-cofounder-todd-werth-of","episode 279 - building vs. building with ceo/cofounder todd werth of infinite red todd werth ceo/co-founder of infinite red get customers to commit to paying before you spend money building your product or business choose b2b over b2c since you need far fewer customers to build a sustainable business treat sales like a skill to practice, not a talent you either have or dont after every sales call do a retrospective with your team to improve what you say and do in interviews and at work just show up with something tangible done, thats more than most people do ep 279"],["e278-the-power-of-content-w-jason-lengstorf","episode 278 - the power of content w/jason lengstorf jason lengstorf freelance developer-focused media and marketing consultant, former vp of developer experience at netlify, host of learn with jason focus content on the story of who someone becomes by using a tool, not a list of features or specs pick one clear narrative instead of burying people in technical specs they do not care about watch out for building brand identity so tightly that changing your rules later feels like betraying your audience stop obsessing over siloed metrics like blog views and instead track awareness, signups, activation, and paid conversion together treat your career like an experiment and try many paths early instead of specializing too soon ep 278"],["e277-break-the-glass-solo-show","episode 277 - break the glass (solo show)   if you get laid off, take the next day off to process before you start job searching so you are not acting on emotion rewrite your resume to be achievement and quantitative based instead of just listing tasks like react or aws if you are open to both front end and back end roles, build two different resumes each focused on that specific track treat your job search like an eight to five job with a routine, including resume work, linkedin engagement, and messaging recruiters and companies daily use a tool like trello to track every application by stage so you know exactly when to follow up ep 277"],["e276-shashi-lo-out-here-helping-people","episode 276 - shashi lo - out here helping people shashi lo senior software engineer at microsoft treat mentorship as something you can get through tweets and groups, not just one on one relationships shoot your shot when asking for mentorship or help, since the worst answer you get is no stay intentional with your network by checking in on people even years after a rejection, since it can lead back to a job keep a running doc of every interview question you missed so you can study and improve for the next one if anxiety hits during an interview, ask for 30 seconds to collect your thoughts instead of rushing an answer ep 276"],["e275-colin-lord-pto-hobbies-and-taking-care-of-yourself","episode 275 - colin lord - pto, hobbies, and taking care of yourself colin lord engineering manager of front end teams at smile direct club learn to tell the difference between needing a 15 minute walk and needing a full week off to recharge uninstall work apps like outlook and teams during vacation so you are not tempted to check in build a real relationship with your manager so it feels safe to say you are struggling before it affects your work during a job search treat it like a full time job and aim for one solid intentional conversation a day instead of nonstop applying get comfortable asking for help early since most people do not take advantage of help that is already offered to them ep 275"],["e274-a-letter-to-recruiters-solo-show","episode 274 - a letter to recruiters (solo show)   recruiters should show up at local tech conferences in their own city, not just work job boards all day you do not need to travel the country, just attend events in your hometown to start building relationships if your company will not pay for a conference, ask to volunteer so you can still get in the door after meeting someone, connect on their most active social channel right away instead of relying on a business card keep networking simple, introduce yourself, ask what they do, trade contact info, and move on ep 274"],["e273-braydon-coyer-and-navigating-800-submissions-to-jobs","episode 273 - braydon coyer and navigating 800 submissions to jobs braydon coyer senior front-end developer at logic gate expect the job search to feel slow until networking kicks in, even after hundreds of applications talk to people for a couple minutes, not thirty, networking is just a short genuine conversation juniors can stand out by showing initiative and talking to people even without much experience yet seniors should be adaptable across frameworks and able to find solutions anywhere, not tied to one stack build networking as a skill by practicing in low stakes settings like coffee shops before conferences ep 273"],["e272-networking-strategy-solo-show","episode 272 - networking strategy (solo show)   treat networking like a meetup where showing up and saying hey is the first step, then engage further in comments find a common denominator with someone, like a shared interest, to start a real connection keep conference conversations short, five minutes covering what someone does and whether they like it is plenty recruiters focus on the body of your resume, not links to portfolios or github, so make that content count after meeting people at a conference the follow up afterward is where the real relationship and opportunity happens ep 272"],["e271-entrepreneurship-and-startup-life-w-sheena-allen","episode 271 - entrepreneurship and startup life w/sheena allen sheena allen founder, capway treat non-technical communication like leaving no holes explain every button and screen so nothing is left to guess if you work with technical people learn enough about the process so you know when a timeline estimate does not make sense creativity and an outsider background can be an advantage as a founder instead of something to hide figure out a schedule that protects sleep meals time with family and short walks instead of sitting for hours straight focus on proving yourself right instead of trying to prove someone else wrong in your work ep 271"],["e270-a-tough-conversation-w-ashten-fizer","episode 270 - a tough conversation w/ashten fizer ashten fizer talent brand go to market lead at dropbox during tech layoffs, spend less time applying and more time upskilling to build a more varied resume figure out what skills actually excite you before jumping into ai or machine learning so you dont burn out when reaching out for mentorship personalize your message and explain specifically why you chose that person when communicating upward be succinct, use bullet points, lead with data and always bring a solution not just a problem in interviews ask leaders when they last took time off to gauge if the company culture actually supports work life balance ep 270"],["e269-contract-contract-to-hire-solo-show","episode 269 - contract/contract to hire (solo show)   understand the difference between freelancing, where you run your own llc and find your own clients, and contracting, where you work w2 through a recruiter expect contracting benefits through a recruiting firm to be decent for single people or couples but not great overall before taking a contract to hire role, ask the recruiter how many people they have placed with that company and how well they know the hiring manager always ask upfront what the salary will be if you convert from contract to full time, since your hourly rate will be higher than that salary look into hourly contract roles right now since more companies are avoiding big salary commitments and keeping staffing flexible ep 269"],["e268-a-letter-to-recruiters-w-head-of-devrel-at-snap-tessa","episode 268 - a letter to recruiters w/head of devrel at snap, tessa kriesel tessa kriesel head of devrel at snap developers think differently, so recruiters should do a little research, offer some value, and only then bring up what they're looking for tessa's three steps for reaching developers: find where they hang out online, open with something of value to them, then make your ask clear if you don't know the tech, ask developers what they're building and learning, most want to share what they're passionate about if someone's social media is clearly personal, don't comment on it for a recruiting reason, use what you learn to shape your outreach instead devrel builds the trust and community developers need, so it should feed leads to sales but never be turned into the sales team ep 268"],["e267-leading-from-the-bottom-and-creating-a-safe-space-for","episode 267 - leading from the bottom and creating a safe space for feedback ben cto of a healthcare company building emr tech for hospice and home care let your team run their plan even if you think yours is better, since ownership and buy-in beat a forced plan treat bad news from your team as a sign of trust and safety, then ask what they need from you to fix it if employees might not be telling you the truth, get 360 feedback from their peers since someone usually has better visibility than you do when hiring, prioritize aptitude and attitude over exact tech stack experience since good people ramp up fast with solid onboarding move fast in the hiring process and give candidates feedback quickly instead of making them wait weeks to hear back ep 267"],["e266-building-impactful-teams-w-director-of-engineering-chris","episode 266 - building impactful teams w/director of engineering, chris holland chris holland director of engineering ask better questions and truly listen to your team before assuming you know their challenges adding scrum or agile ceremonies wont fix delivery problems if the root engineering practices are broken focus on patterns, clean code, and test driven development to reduce technical debt and move faster long term test driven development can speed up delivery by cutting out manual steps used to verify code works moving into leadership means your success depends on your team's success, not just your own technical skill ep 266"],["e265-q-a-on-the-job-search-solo-show","episode 265 - q&a on the job search (solo show)   offer free tangible work like design, photography or video to small businesses to build trust and get your foot in the door don't overload yourself with free work, pick and choose where you give your time so you can still make money look at your own life, your church, your pets, your community for small free projects you can use to build a portfolio be willing to take contract or part time work since it can turn into a full time job within a few months show up prepared to interviews, research the company and ask real questions since employers are being more selective now ep 265"],["e264-jem-young-engineering-manager-at-netflix-and-the-state-of","episode 264 - jem young, engineering manager at netflix and the state of tech jem young engineering manager at netflix track your wins in a journal, even five or ten minutes every week or two, so you have artifacts ready for reviews or resumes when things get uncertain, be visible, make sure managers and vps know your name and what you deliver put hard numbers behind your work like money saved, projects shipped, or people hired instead of just describing effort junior engineers stand out by showing initiative like fixing outdated docs or pulling threads on problems, not by listing fake metrics ai will likely take over boilerplate and repetitive coding tasks, so get ahead by learning how it fits into your workflow now ep 264"],["e263-let-me-help-you-solo-show","episode 263 - let me help you (solo show)   use twitter lists to narrow thousands of users down to the people you actually want to network with treat networking dms like an interview of the other person, not a pitch about your own value good cold dms include a specific time ask, a specific question, and genuine admiration for the company or person always ask for another introduction at the end of a good conversation, only if the chat is going well if social media feels overwhelming, pick one or two spots like linkedin and discord servers instead of trying to be everywhere ep 263"],["e262-dealing-with-this-downturn-solo-show","episode 262 - dealing with this downturn (solo show)   in a tough market, mix mass applying with one or two intentional networking calls or messages a day stand out to recruiters by communicating consistently, following up, and staying flexible when they ask you to do something text or email recruiters instead of calling since they are in meetings most of the day post content and engage publicly even as a junior developer so recruiters and hiring managers can find you if you keep getting rejected, ask interviewers directly for feedback and how many candidates are in the process ep 262"],["e261-scaling-an-engineering-organization-of-300-developers","episode 261 - scaling an engineering organization of 300+ developers ryan and yule staff software engineer and director of engineering at volvo cars use a rotating roster for technical assessments instead of asking for volunteers so reviews don't stall out give take-home assignments a time cap and ask candidates to prioritize what they solve so you see judgment not just free time submit interviewer feedback separately before comparing notes so the panel doesn't bias each other have recruiters lead candidate-to-team matching once they know the tech space well enough to spot the right fit set expectations early that candidates may not know their exact team until later in a fast-growing org ep 261"],["e260-handling-an-uncertain-market-w-james-quick","episode 260 - handling an uncertain market w/james quick james quick developer, speaker, and content creator when you get laid off, resist posting a generic update and instead lean on relationships you already built in your community share your work consistently on twitter, discord, or a blog so people can verify your skills before you ever apply treat content as proof for your resume claims, since anyone can watch your videos or read your posts and see if the skill is real if a recruiter or contact does not respond, follow up with empathy a couple more times instead of sending canned repeated messages ask your employer for time and resources to learn new things rather than assuming all growth has to happen on unpaid personal time ep 260"],["e259-how-to-get-into-tech-w-ashten-fizer","episode 259 - how to get into tech w/ashten fizer ashten fizer talent brand reframe past jobs in resume language that fits tech, even a fast food job can show quality assurance skills research bootcamps carefully since not all are equal, look for strong job placement rates and real company relationships internships matter a lot for landing a job after school, aim for several summers of experience if you go the college route if you dont know what you want to study dont feel pressured to enroll right away, explore trade school or free online courses first if you wake up hating your job treat that discomfort as a signal its time to make a change ep 259"],["e258-recruiters-are-hiring-managers-best-friends-solo-show","episode 258 - recruiters are hiring managers best friends (solo show)   hiring managers rarely have time to screen hundreds of resumes for one job posting so recruiters filter them down to the best few recruiters can handle scheduling and interview follow up so hiring managers just show up and have the conversation inviting recruiters into company culture and team outings helps them understand what kind of person actually fits the organization when you sign a contract with a recruiter they agree not to poach your employees but that protection goes away if you stop working with them ask recruiters how your company is coming across in the market since they can give you a pulse on your brand and help you fix retention issues ep 258"],["e257-tips-and-tricks-of-the-job-search-from-a-meta-engineer-w","episode 257 - tips and tricks of the job search from a meta engineer w/andronica klaas andronica klaas lead quality assurance engineer at meta you don't have to be a developer to work in tech: andronica moved from writing code into quality assurance because it fits her strength for finding what's wrong and thinking up edge cases internships teach you a company's culture and the skills a role really needs before you commit, and interns give companies a fresh view from the next generation of users know your intention before you network (a sponsor, a mentor or just being known), look people up ahead of time, and think about what you can offer them if networking makes you nervous, keep a list of go-to questions, listen with curiosity, and ask people afterward what you could have done better keep a relationship with at least one recruiter even when you're happy, and tailor your resume to the skills in the job description, translating what you've done into those skills ep 257"],["e256-salaries-and-ats-crm-solo-show","episode 256 - salaries and ats/crm (solo show)   give recruiters three salary numbers your absolute minimum, your current base, and your ideal no-brainer offer talk base salary, not total comp, since things like hsa or stipends muddy the comparison update recruiters throughout the process if your salary expectations change so they can recalibrate to break into a target company, find mutual connections on linkedin and ask for a warm intro to recruiting no algorithm or ats fully matches resumes to job descriptions, humans make the final hiring decisions ep 256"],["e255-how-do-you-sell-yourself-w-erik-anderson-sr-software","episode 255 - how do you sell yourself w/erik anderson, sr. software engineer at yum! brands erik anderson sr. software engineer at yum! brands build experience by volunteering for nonprofits or small shops instead of waiting for someone to hand you a job log hours honestly on your resume so part time volunteer work does not read like a full time role optimize your linkedin headline, about section and skills with the specific keywords recruiters actually search like react or node spend time daily posting, commenting and engaging on linkedin since activity signals to the algorithm that you are responsive be intentional about telling your company what you want to work on so you do not get stuck as the expert in old tech you dont enjoy ep 255"],["e254-how-do-you-do-a-recession-w-engineering-manager-andrew","episode 254 - how do you 'do' a recession w/engineering manager, andrew wyborski andrew wyborski engineering manager, kroger treat your career the same in a boom or a bust, keep building your network and skills regardless of the economy an intentional email signature and salutation make you more human in a remote first environment if you get laid off, give yourself a day or two to breathe before jumping into a job search job searching after a layoff should run like an eight to five job, mornings for outreach, afternoons for networking calls you dont need one perfect mentor, stitch together advice from a few trusted peers who each know different things ep 254"],["e253-new-beginnings-and-how-to-scale-w-ryan-burgess","episode 253 - new beginnings and how to scale w/ ryan burgess ryan burgess software engineering manager at netflix when building a new team from existing people, invest the first few months in understanding how they work and what problems they see before proposing solutions pitch new internal projects by writing up the problem and proposal in a doc and asking for input instead of presenting it as already decided demonstrate the value of an idea by doing the work on the side before asking for permission to lead it when a company reorganizes, ask your manager and even skip level leaders why it happened and how you can help, since context matters more than control to judge if a company is scaling responsibly, ask whether an open role is a backfill and why the previous person left ep 253"],["e252-advocating-for-yourself-w-amy-from-hack-your-hr","episode 252 - advocating for yourself w/ amy from hack your hr amy hr leader at a global company, hack your hr frame tough conversations around what is reasonable versus what is realistic to keep the discussion productive remember pushback on a raise is often self protection from your boss, not a personal rejection before taking on more work, get paid for what you are already doing and audit your job description lead with candor by talking to your boss the way you would talk to a respected friend, honestly and directly when recruiting or interviewing someone, ask how they are doing in their job search before jumping into the role details ep 252"],["e251-employee-centered-management-systems-w-dmitriy-neganov-who","episode 251 - employee centered management systems w/dmitriy neganov who heads up vaco's m&a practice dmitriy neganov heads up vaco's m&a practice put employees first as the foundation, then build decision processes and priorities from that starting point stop treating people as resources and instead ask what value you personally add if you were not there three core employee needs to address are purpose, direction, and engagement, each requiring its own tool align individual employee goals with company goals rather than making profit the primary stated goal be sincere with recognition instead of treating it as a quick bonus or band aid fix ep 251"],["e250-interviewing-as-a-principal-engineer-as-a-first-timer-w-joe","episode 250 - interviewing as a principal engineer as a first timer w/joe flateau, technical lead/staff engineer at density joe flateau technical lead/staff engineer at density when moving to a new job after many years in one place, practice talking about what you personally did, not just what your team did trim resume debt by cutting skills that aren't front and center or relevant to the job you're applying for message a referral contact before you submit your application so they can actually advocate for you with hr keep cover letters short, around four to five sentences, each tied directly to something in the job description treat yourself like a business you run, not just a dependent of your employer, and keep learning and selling your skills ep 250"],["e249-how-to-be-better-w-my-colleagues-nate-walker-and-joseph","episode 249 - how to be better w/my colleagues nate walker and joseph jewell nate walker and joseph jewell colleagues on taylor's global solutions team shorten your hiring process since time kills deals, decide within the first part of the interview and spend the rest on the real job needs consider contract to hire instead of direct hire if your process is too slow and you risk losing talent recruiters can act as advisors, not just order takers, by sharing honest feedback even when it is not what the client wants to hear when changing careers, network constantly, practice interviewing, and be honest about your real capabilities instead of overselling use time blocking on your calendar to protect focus for prospecting, research, or certifications so you actually get deep work done ep 249"],["e248-how-to-break-into-tech-solo-show","episode 248 - how to break into tech (solo show)   consider coding bootcamps over self-paced learning for the built-in alumni community and networking access if self-paced, run your job search like a full-time job from eight to five every day spend mornings from eight to noon networking on linkedin and twitter before studying code in the afternoon when messaging someone, lead with genuine admiration, ask a specific question, then request a specific small amount of time like seven minutes build in public by posting what you are learning and building so people can follow your journey and eventually reach out ep 248"],["e247-its-okay-to-stay-somewhere-w-my-dear-friend-stacey-smith-who","episode 247 - it's okay to stay somewhere w/my dear friend stacey smith who is a sr. principal developer stacey smith sr. principal developer staying at one company for years is fine as long as you are still learning and growing ask about tenure in interviews since long employee retention is a strong sign of a healthy team figure out if a frustration is an industry wide issue or something specific to your organization before deciding to quit dont expect your job to fulfill your whole life, build relationships and interests outside of work too practice answering tell me about yourself since confidence there carries into the rest of the interview ep 247"],["e246-recruiting-industry-solo-show","episode 246 - recruiting industry (solo show)   expect chat gpt to assist recruiting work rather than replace the industry since relationships still drive hiring if you are stuck job searching, aim to have one new networking conversation every day treat the job search like a full time job and fill your schedule with networking when unemployed at career fairs collect contacts and connect on linkedin, then follow up with a short call afterward since people won't remember you otherwise keep a short cover letter that highlights your career milestones even though many hiring managers skip reading them ep 246"],["e245-from-engineer-to-product-and-how-to-get-into-tech-w-adam","episode 245 - from engineer to product and how to get into tech w/adam murray who is a sr. product manager at cloudflare adam murray senior product manager at cloudflare try moving from engineering to product by flexing product skills on a side project before making a full switch get involved in open source to practice working with a community the way product managers work with customers do not limit your tech job search to developer roles, consider product, project management, qa, customer success or business analyst paths be careful accepting inflated titles early since you may later have to walk them back in interviews it is fine to admit you do not know something rather than giving a long answer to dodge it ep 245"],["e244-whole-wide-world-out-there-w-alana-kilmartin-who-is-an","episode 244 - whole wide world out there w/ alana kilmartin who is an author, podcaster, and speaker alana kilmartin founder of unserious careers, author, podcaster, and speaker travel abroad after school or college to build problem solving skills and learn how other people live and work a small investment in travel can pay off for years in personal growth and life experience staying stuck in a toxic job erodes your confidence, so create space to see what else is possible before you quit build in activities outside of work that totally absorb you so you can reset instead of only grinding figure out what you dont want in your career, since that clarity moves you closer to what you do want ep 244"],["e243-giving-back-and-helping-everyone-w-my-dear-friend-sydnee","episode 243 - giving back and helping everyone w/my dear friend sydnee sampson sydnee sampson software engineer at accenture and founder of shiptek prints build a community of people who understand your career struggles instead of relying on family who dont get what you do when youre stuck, write down a short list of core beliefs and reread them when you want to give up think ten years ahead and decide who you want to be before chasing a paycheck in a field you dont actually like prioritize cloud certifications and data science or python skills since those areas are in demand right now dont feel pressured to have your whole life figured out young, its okay to make mistakes and still enjoy being young ep 243"],["e242-how-to-find-a-recruiter-solo-show","episode 242 - how to find a recruiter (solo show)   stop mass applying to job posts and instead message recruiters directly on linkedin to build real connections search tech recruiters plus your city on linkedin and use the filters feature to narrow results look for recruiters with at least two years in the industry and two years at their current company when a recruiter cant help you directly ask for a quick favor like resume or linkedin feedback if you find a job posting message a recruiter you know and ask if they can refer you or know someone there ep 242"],["e241-the-shift-to-remote-work-and-the-power-of-content-w-vp-of","episode 241 - the shift to remote work and the power of content w/vp of software engineering, andrew moore andrew moore vp of software engineering at nations lending apply within the first few hours of a job posting since high volume roles fill up fast with applicants skip generic easy apply templates and send a direct message explaining why you fit the role when networking or applying, open with something personal or a shared connection to the work to stand out if you lead remote teams, be intentional about one on ones and check ins since casual hallway contact is gone build your online presence consistently on linkedin since an active profile gets noticed faster than a silent one ep 241"],["e240-live-from-that-conference","episode 240 - live from that conference michael richardson, james q quick, becca panel at that conference: michael richardson (engineering manager, kroger technology, momentum developer conference), james q quick (technical content creator), becca (technical community builder, deepgram, virtual coffee) in-person conversations build trust and authenticity faster than video calls or online profiles treat your external tech community as separate from your employer since companies can let you go but your network stays ask your manager directly for conference budget or career support instead of waiting for it to be offered building trust with developers takes multiple touchpoints like twitter, conferences, and content before they remember or trust you recruiters should introduce themselves as people first and skip generic pitches that ignore someone's actual background or skills ep 240"],["e239-investing-in-your-career-w-amy-dutton-who-is-the-director-of","episode 239 - investing in your career w/amy dutton who is the director of design at zeal amy dutton director of design at zeal build things and do real work because real bugs and problems are what push your skills forward try open source by looking for issues labeled best first issue in well maintained repos on github content you make is for others to discover you, not for you, so do not judge it by your own taste learning how to learn matters more than any single framework since it lets you pick up new tools faster network by genuinely building relationships rather than treating it as a sleazy transactional activity ep 239"],["e238-managing-workload-and-delegation-w-kelly-vaughn-director-of","episode 238 - managing workload and delegation w/kelly vaughn, director of engineering at spotai kelly vaughn director of engineering at spotai pick one must-do task for the week, two backup tasks, and a few small tasks to use as brain breaks put your most intensive deep-thinking work at the beginning of the day when you have the most energy schedule brain breaks and lunch on your calendar so you actually take them instead of skipping them communicate clearly what you are not going to get done so nobody is surprised later delegation starts with trust, so let people do the work their own way instead of dictating your method ep 238"],["e237-working-with-recruiters-solo-show","episode 237 - working with recruiters (solo show)   reach out to agency recruiters first since they work across many clients and often know the market better than corporate recruiters remember agency recruiters only get paid when they place someone, which shapes how responsive they are text or email recruiters instead of calling since most are on calls with other candidates if a recruiter goes quiet, send a direct message asking for honest feedback so you can move on in your search when connecting with recruiters on linkedin always include a personal note instead of a blank request ep 237"],["e236-how-to-survive-an-interview-w-eddie-hinkle-engineering","episode 236 - how to survive an interview w/eddie hinkle, engineering manager at glassdoor eddie hinkle engineering manager at glassdoor treat a technical interview like pairing with a peer instead of performing for a proctor to calm your nerves if youre too nervous to show your skills live, ask to switch to a take home and follow up interview mark an x on a calendar every day you hate your job, and if over half the month is x days start job searching document unusual situations and keep copies of your reviews since managers and companies can change without warning know what you want from a role, like skill building or a big name company, before deciding whether to take a step down to get there ep 236"],["e235-how-to-write-a-job-description-hire-correctly-w-ceo","episode 235 - how to write a job description & hire correctly w/ ceo/cofounder of flightcontrol (yc w22) | creator of blitz.js brandon behr co-founder and ceo of flightcontrol, creator of blitz.js start a job description by getting clear on your company's real identity and core values before writing anything else use a creative job title and photos of the actual team to help candidates picture who they would work with keep required qualifications as short as possible so you do not scare off great candidates, especially underrepresented ones run your job description through a bias checker to catch language that skews too masculine or feminine design your application questions around values and growth mindset so you get a high signal to noise ratio of good fits ep 235"],["e234-we-back-w-clark-sell","episode 234 - we back w/clark sell clark sell  the tech talent shortage is still real even though big tech layoffs dominate headlines look beyond facebook amazon microsoft apple and google since they represent a small slice of the tech job market working for companies outside the us can be some of the best years of a career so consider global opportunities if going out on your own, gather other people's experiences and opinions instead of just asking for generic advice deep in person relationships at conferences and local meetups pay off more than chasing large online followings ep 234"],["e233-guidance-counselor-2-0-w-milecia-mcgregor-career-paths","episode 233 - guidance counselor 2.0 w/milecia mcgregor - career paths outside of software milecia mcgregor software developer and consultant if you hit the three to five year mark and still like coding but want change, shadow other roles like devops or product before jumping ship product managers talk to stakeholders, find pain points, and translate them into user stories for engineers to build if you are burnt out on being told your code is broken, qa or sdet roles let you test and automate instead of just writing new features when starting freelance consulting, take on small clients for a few hours a week and slowly grow your workload before going full time price freelance work by taking your desired yearly salary and dividing it by 2080 to get an hourly rate ep 233"],["e232-recruiter-extravaganza-w-adam-walters-global-head-of-talent","episode 232 - recruiter extravaganza w/adam walters, global head of talent acquisition at polygon.io. adam walters global head of talent acquisition at polygon.io agency recruiting teaches full cycle sourcing while in house recruiting means thinking about the whole business and team growth over time treat onboarding like you are earning candidate trust, not just filling a seat, since candidates are taking a chance on you too set clear expectations on interview process length and stages so candidates do not panic if rounds change give direct specific feedback like naming the exact skill gap instead of a vague rejection, it helps candidates and builds goodwill network with recruiters before you need a job, have a real conversation, stay in touch, and let timing work in your favor later ep 232"],["e231-hodgepodge-episode-solo-show","episode 231 - hodgepodge episode (solo show)   use downtime around the holidays to reflect on your job, listing pros and cons before deciding to make a move crowdsource your job search by creating a shared slack channel with others who have the same skillset or layoff situation bcc multiple recruiters you trust on job leads so at least one of them can act on it always ask recruiters for quick feedback on your resume or linkedin during any call with them practice upward communication with your manager so they stay aware of what you are doing and implementing ep 231"],["e230-you-belong-in-tech-w-anna-mcdougall","episode 230 - you belong in tech w/anna mcdougall anna mcdougall director of product and engineering, author break your career change into three steps: learn the technical skills, build a network, then apply to jobs with research instead of spray and pray use a structure like scrum sprints to set weekly or monthly learning goals if you are teaching yourself to code when networking, ask questions and listen instead of pitching, then jot down quick notes so you remember people at the next event say yes to opportunities and actually show up, since consistency and time pay off within six months to a year in applications use the names numbers quotes approach, naming people at the company, citing your achievements, and adding real recommendations ep 230"],["e229-changing-up-the-way-of-interviewing-w-christopher-swift","episode 229 - changing up the way of interviewing w/christopher swift, senior software engineer at live oak bank in raleigh christopher swift senior software engineer at live oak bank in raleigh ask candidates to walk through specific details of what they built instead of asking basic definition questions listen for depth of experience the same way you would listen to someone who actually lived through a hard situation versus someone who just read about it watch for disengagement in an interview since lack of energy and care is a major red flag value honesty and willingness to admit you dont know something over trying to fake an answer build real connections with peers since those relationships often lead to future job opportunities more than recruiters do ep 229"],["e228-lunch-time-edition-w-vetswhocode-board-member-w-alex-reyes","episode 228 - lunch time edition w/vetswhocode board member w/alex reyes, mba, who is an inclusive recruiting strategist @ meta alex reyes inclusive recruiting strategist at meta be intentional about your next role and highlight your real skills on your resume, linkedin, and in interviews use impact bullets on your resume that show what you made, saved, or achieved at each position if you were laid off be upfront and honest about it, then focus the conversation on your contributions and performance before applying check linkedin for mutual connections and ask if they are comfortable being your referral keep in touch with recruiters over time by engaging with their content or checking in every quarter ep 228"],["e227-obstacles-in-the-job-search-w-jacob-herrington-a-sr-engineer","episode 227 - obstacles in the job search w/jacob herrington a sr. engineer jacob herrington senior software engineer if you lack a degree or connections, take rough early jobs and prove you can learn fast and work hard list short job stints on your resume if you can explain why and what you delivered in that time when a recruiter wont share full details on a role, ask for an unrealistic rate first to gauge real market pay before doing a lengthy take home test, offer your public code history as proof of skill instead build real relationships with people in your field instead of only reaching out when you need something ep 227"],["e225-resume-triage-w-alishah-novin","episode 225 - resume triage w/alishah novin alishah novin resume reviewer on the episode's resume triage segment put your tech stack and technical experience above personal projects so hiring managers see your strongest skills first always include months and years for every job, not just years, or it looks like you are hiding something write resume bullets around accomplishments and impact, not just daily duties, since recruiters already know the basic job description use numbers and comparisons whenever possible, like how your results stack up against an average or goal, not just raw stats alone keep your resume format clean and scannable since hiring managers skim fast and a dense or dark page gets set aside ep 225"],["e224-how-to-find-the-right-fit-w-reid-evans-principal-consultant","episode 224 - how to find the \"right fit' w/reid evans principal consultant and resultstack and co-founder/cto of l3i reid evans principal consultant at resultstack and co-founder/cto of l3i leadership means asking people what they need instead of telling them what to do most jobs during layoffs come through your network, not cold applications, so keep contacts active before hiring someone new, make sure the current team actually wants and needs another person in one-on-ones ask two simple questions, what makes you feel valued and where do you want to grow as teams scale past a small size, regular one-on-ones become essential for keeping people around ep 224"],["e223-fixing-the-interview-process-w-gant-laborde-cio-of-infinite","episode 223 - fixing the interview process w/gant laborde, cio of infinite red a react native first consulting company gant laborde cio of infinite red learning react native is not hard but different, since web assumptions like the dom do not carry over directly job seekers often send one resume everywhere instead of researching and tailoring per company, leading to worst fit not best fit watch out for companies that hire to hit growth metrics rather than because they actually need the role filled when companies over rely on metrics instead of values, people learn to game the system instead of doing good work ask about a company's real values in the interview, since a mission statement that is just a wall plaque means little ep 223"],["e222-from-hourly-wage-at-target-to-beating-out-2k-applicants-w","episode 222 - from hourly wage at target to beating out 2k applicants w/tim stoneberg tim stoneberg steelseries when you spot a job you are genuinely passionate about, skip the easy apply and find someone on the team to reach out to directly being one real person who reaches out beats being one of thousands of applicants in the pile narrow your job search to companies and industries you actually care about so your passion comes through naturally let small personal details like your interests or hobbies show up in your resume, linkedin, or even your email signature to spark real connection if your current job search approach keeps failing, change your strategy instead of just sending more applications the same way ep 222"],["e221-back-to-the-basics-recruiters-solo-show","episode 221 - back to the basics - recruiters (solo show)   know the two types of recruiters agency recruiters work with many companies while corporate recruiters work in house for just one build a list of around ten agency recruiters you like and check in with them monthly instead of waiting on just one use the two and two rule when picking a recruiter to trust look for at least two years in the industry and two years at their current company find recruiters by searching technical recruiter on linkedin filtered by your city then dm them directly text or email recruiters instead of calling since most recruiters are on back to back calls all day and rarely pick up ep 221"],["e220-back-to-the-basics-solo-show","episode 220 - back to the basics (solo show)   treat your resume and linkedin as the building blocks of your job search and be obsessed about getting them right keep linkedin as a short summary with just three or four bullet points per role, not your full work history keep your resume simple, especially for creative roles, so reviewers focus on content instead of design choices google your target job title and pull common buzzwords from job descriptions to add to your resume and linkedin message recruiters and ask for one piece of feedback on your resume or linkedin to improve it for free ep 220"],["e219-meet-the-nerd-team-w-sloane-bartley","episode 219 - meet the nerd team w/sloane bartley sloane bartley recruiter on taylor's team treat recruiters as an extra set of eyes watching the market for you, even if they cannot place you right now when a recruiter has nothing open for your skill set, ask for honesty instead of silence so you can keep searching elsewhere use a simple board to track candidates or job leads by skill set so you can match quickly when something opens up set up regular check ins with your network so you know who is still looking and who has moved on focus less on finding one perfect calling and more on what in your current job brings you joy and lines up with your values ep 219"],["e218-speaking-to-the-pain-not-apologizing-w-jean-leggett-who-is","episode 218 - speaking to the pain & not apologizing w/jean leggett who is an amazing coach/speaker/resume writer jean leggett career coach, speaker, and resume writer build your resume around the hiring manager's pain points by pulling language straight from the job posting group your resume into clear pillars like validation, impact, and people skills instead of a plain chronological list use a cover letter to personalize your pitch since it probably won't win the job but can win you an interview prep for interviews by reading each resume bullet aloud and making sure you can explain the story behind it stop apologizing for your achievements on a resume, this is the one place you're supposed to brag ep 218"],["e217-networking-on-polywork-solo-show","episode 217 - networking on polywork (solo show)   use polywork badges to show what you do so people can find you without a full conversation search a badge like software engineering to see everyone tagged with it and start connecting use the collaborate tab to find people posting podcast, speaking, and live streaming opportunities when messaging someone on polywork, reference the exact collaboration type they listed, like podcast guesting, to skip the guesswork spend about 15 minutes a day on polywork working through badges and the collaborate tool instead of trying to post content there ep 217"],["e216-promoting-a-fun-environment-for-devs-w-my-friend-ray","episode 216 - promoting a fun environment for devs w/my friend ray morrison ray morrison engineer at oc tanner ask for autonomy in your first weeks so you can research and learn the stack without pressure to perform instantly expect to only know about half the tech stack in a new role, so plan time to fill the gaps use a buddy or pair programming on your first few tickets to learn the codebase before working solo be upfront about your story point comfort level so managers do not overload you early on ask if your company will cover a course or certification tied to your bonus or growth goals ep 216"],["e215-the-power-of-a-conference-and-its-community-w-clark-sell","episode 215 - the power of a conference and it's community w/clark sell, founder & ceo of 'that conference\" clark sell founder & ceo of that conference showing up is most of the battle, so register, attend sessions, and put yourself in the room volunteer at a conference, especially registration, because it puts you in front of everyone invest a little money in yourself through courses or conference tickets, it pays off over time after meeting someone at an event, wait a few days before reaching out so you stand out from the first-day flood of messages don't be afraid to sit at a different table or approach strangers, almost everyone there wants to connect too ep 215"],["e214-catching-up-with-a-shopify-recruiter-w-alain-robineau","episode 214 - catching up with a shopify recruiter w/alain robineau alain robineau recruiter at shopify focused on data science when giving rejection feedback, offer candidates a quick call instead of just an email so they understand where they fell short most candidates offered a feedback call will not book it, so dont expect high uptake even when you offer before messaging someone at a company, check their profile so you ask about the right specialty instead of a generic question stand out by sending a pointed message that connects your background to the specific role instead of a generic pitch in a tough market be intentional with outreach, tailor your resume to the job description, and get active in slack or discord communities instead of lurking ep 214"],["e219-meet-the-nerd-team-w-sloane-bartley-2023-01-31","episode 219 - meet the nerd team w/sloane bartley sloane bartley recruiter on taylor's team treat recruiting like helping people through transitions, not just filling roles stay consistent every week since results from your outreach show up two to three weeks later use a tool like trello to organize candidates by skill set and track where they are in the process be upfront with candidates when you have nothing for them instead of ghosting them ask more questions early in a new role instead of pretending to already know everything ep 219"],["e213-english-lit-to-software-developer-w-my-dear-friend-kasey","episode 213 - english lit to software developer w/my dear friend kasey wahl kasey wahl junior developer research bootcamps before committing and check if they interview you first, a real screening process signals they invest in students ask bootcamp graduates on linkedin about their actual experience to get an honest forecast before enrolling when stuck on a concept, write down exactly what you dont understand and ask about it instead of quitting use mornings for job search and networking when your mind is freshest, save coding and content creation for afternoons send recruiters and hiring managers content you created on topics discussed in interviews to show you followed up and learned ep 213"],["e212-navigating-twitter","episode 212 - navigating twitter   set a strong twitter banner and profile picture since they create the first impression for a hiring manager write your title to cover who you are, what you do, and what you tweet about use lists to find and join communities instead of just posting random tweets engage by liking and commenting even if you never tweet, since twitter rewards consistent interaction pin a tweet that shows your mission or who you are to invite follows right away ep 212"],["e211-content-and-the-recruiting-industry-w-joel-lalgee-from","episode 211 - content and the recruiting industry w/joel lalgee from hirewell joel lalgee hirewell incentivize recruiters to create content instead of expecting it for free on top of their regular work get leadership buy in first, since content efforts feel unpredictable and hard to track without support from the top dont wait for perfect production, low quality content that delivers real value beats polished content with no substance internal recruiters who build a personal brand can bring their following with them to any future company they join if youre just starting out, post about your day to day experience and lessons learned instead of trying to act like a thought leader ep 211"],["e210-why-do-people-suck-w-my-dear-friend-andrew-wyborski","episode 210 - why do people suck w/my dear friend andrew wyborski andrew wyborski  most of a career can trace back to just a handful of personal connections so treat every conversation as a potential job lead you can learn a lot about a company by how they source their talent during the hiring process bring solutions instead of problems to your manager and take initiative by proactively giving updates keep a background thread running on side projects or goals so you make steady progress without burning out if you cannot affect change where you are, change your participation there because this is your problem to solve ep 210"],["e209-from-car-salesman-to-software-dev","episode 209 - from car salesman to software dev david software developer, former car salesman message hiring managers directly on linkedin instead of commenting on their posts, since commenting just creates more competition for you if you have experience in an industry like car sales or healthcare, target tech companies serving that same industry for your first dev job build a real support system or mentor outside social media who actually knows you, not just quick calls or viral advice pace your job interviews and give yourself real rest, since burning out on back to back interviews hurts your performance check linkedin for existing connections at a company before applying, since a real referral can speed up the process a lot ep 209"],["e208-the-power-of-self-care-networking-and-community-w-annie-t","episode 208 - the power of self care, networking, and community w/annie t. bellefleur a software engineer at stitch fix annie t. bellefleur software engineer at stitch fix time block your days after bootcamp since the structure you had in class disappears once you graduate split your week into focused chunks like networking days and project or coding days so nothing gets neglected get visible on twitter by engaging with posts and sharing your projects so people start to know like and trust you treat your job search with strategy and intentional conversations instead of just applying to a high volume of jobs schedule real breaks and a weekly day off during your job search so you return with more energy and focus ep 208"],["e207-rest-the-job-search-and-hiring-for-teachable-skills-w-paul","episode 207 - rest, the job search, and hiring for teachable skills w/paul gower who is an engineering team lead at kin insurance and conference organizer for little rock tech fest. paul gower engineering team lead at kin insurance and conference organizer for little rock tech fest treat rest as a skill you actively practice, not just laziness or downtime experiment to find your own rest style since naps and routines work differently for everyone build small recurring team moments like casual icebreakers to create connection and downtime prepare your own thoughtful questions for interviews about culture and practices instead of just answering theirs when hiring, prioritize teachable skills like collaboration and willingness to learn over exact tech stack experience ep 207"],["e206-from-tech-recruiter-to-developer-w-andrew-coleman-who-is-a","episode 206 - from tech recruiter to developer w/andrew coleman who is a react recruiting lead at bitov andrew coleman react recruiting lead at bitovi focus on code, content, and connection instead of mass applying to jobs write about what you build and learn daily, even if no one reads it at first, you still have a record for yourself only a portion of jobs are posted publicly and even fewer get filled through that posting, so networking matters more than applications avoid chasing new languages or frameworks right after a bootcamp, go deep in one skill instead of staying a generalist connect with someone at your target company before you apply, since a referral can get you a first round interview ep 206"],["e205-resume-triage-w-alishah-novin","episode 205 - resume triage w/alishah novin alishah novin hiring manager at microsoft put your technical expertise and summary at the top of your resume instead of burying it under education and certifications write a short summary with specific years of experience and technologies so a hiring manager can place you in seconds quantify your bullet points with real numbers like users, team size, or bugs completed instead of vague statements keep formatting simple and consistent since cluttered layouts, dual columns, and mixed fonts make hiring managers pass even on strong candidates if you lack paid experience, list self-study and side projects with the tech stack and the problem you were solving to show how you think ep 205"],["e204-recap-of-last-few-weeks","episode 204 - recap of last few weeks   talk to recruiters to learn what jobs and salary ranges actually exist outside your current circle go where tech people gather, like discord channels, slack groups, meetups and conferences, to find new opportunities don't let fear stop you from asking for a raise, messaging a stranger, or changing jobs pick something you're passionate about and build a small community around it to grow your network connect with everyone you meet through podcasts, conferences, or online comments to build lasting relationships ep 204"],["e203-compressed-fm-w-james-quick-and-amy-dutton-while-at","episode 203 - compressed.fm w/james quick and amy dutton while at magnoliajs james quick, amy dutton hosts of compressed.fm reach out to people on twitter before a conference and set up a coffee or meetup in advance when meeting a speaker, reference something specific from their talk instead of just saying you're a fan find one or two people to navigate a conference with so you have support in awkward situations engage in discord and slack communities before a conference to build connections ahead of time post consistently on social media so people become familiar with you before and after meeting in person ep 203"],["e202-no-mans-land-out-of-coding-bootcamp-w-drew-robbins-who-is-a","episode 202 - no mans land out of coding bootcamp w/drew robbins who is a recent graduate of a bootcamp that now is at meta drew robbins contractor at meta working on xr/vr tech, recent coding bootcamp graduate expect a no mans land after bootcamp where you have to build your own structure for job search, networking and projects choose a bootcamp with real structure and accountability instead of picking the cheapest option send personalized linkedin connection requests referencing something specific from the persons profile to boost response rates always reply to recruiters even to decline, since responding back is a small effort that builds goodwill and future opportunities track every job you apply to in a spreadsheet so you can revisit rejections and reapply when new openings come up ep 202"],["e201-1-49-gpa-college-dropout-to-staff-engineer-with-will-johns","episode 201 - 1.49 gpa college dropout to staff engineer with will johns, staff software engineer at the butterfly network will johns staff software engineer at the butterfly network pick a framework or language, go deep, get your foot in the door, then look for mentors once you are in check github for open source projects with first time contributor labels, even small bug verification work shows initiative to hiring managers keep your resume updated every few months so you never have to cold recall your experience when you suddenly need it keep a master resume in a doc with a skills section rated by proficiency, then bold the keywords relevant to each job before exporting it as a pdf jot down a quick note each day on what you learned or worked on, then roll it up weekly and monthly to track growth and prep for reviews or interviews ep 201"],["e200-networking-101","episode 200 - networking 101   use polywork badges to find people who share your specific interests instead of filtering generic linkedin searches check the polywork opportunities section for job postings, freelance gigs, and podcast guest requests on linkedin, message someone about a specific interest listed on their profile to get a reply almost every time check a linkedin profile's activity section first, if they have not posted in 30 days consider moving on to someone more active start your job search by networking with people from your own school or community before reaching out further ep 200"],["e199-streaming-shotgunning-twitter","episode 199 - streaming, shotgunning, & twitter drew clemitz front-end engineer when you lose income fast, start building something publicly while you apply, it validates you to employers a single honest tweet about hitting rock bottom can snowball into leads if you already have some network built streaming your coding process in public forces you to think out loud, which builds the same muscle interviews need mix tactics, build and stream during the day, then block out late hours to mass apply and cross post your project find a real community like virtual coffee instead of just lurking, consistent small shows of effort compound over time ep 199"],["e198-from-code-to-coach","episode 198 - from code to coach jonny scharer technical career coach at pathrise treat every job application like a tiered system and spend most of your time on roles you truly want, not easy apply jobs a referral from an engineer on the team carries more weight than a recruiter forwarding your resume find common ground before you message someone, like a shared bootcamp or shared industry interest, to make your outreach feel human before asking for a referral or recommendation, add value first, think of it like a social bank where you invest before you withdraw if software development isn't clicking, stay open to roles like qa testing or low code work since internal promotion is easier than restarting a search ep 198"],["e197-lunch-edition-w-jossie-haines-on-dei","episode 197 - lunch edition w/jossie haines on dei jossie haines executive coach for engineering leaders, former vp of engineering at tile focus on inclusion, not just diversity, or you'll keep losing the talent you hire review job descriptions for gendered words like aggressive or rock star that can turn off women applicants write impact based job descriptions that list 30 60 and 90 day goals instead of a long list of requirements build structured interview scorecards based on specific competencies instead of vague one to four ratings as a job seeker ask about inclusive culture after you get the offer but before you accept it ep 197"],["e196-restaurant-factory-worker-hotel-manager-software-developer","episode 196 - restaurant, factory worker, hotel manager, software developer ray morrison front-end software engineer at oc tanner if you're waiting for the right time to pivot careers, that time will never feel comfortable, just start keep applying even after a great interview, a dead week in your pipeline now means no calls later when networking with distant connections, ask if they know anyone hiring instead of asking for a referral directly instead of asking about company culture, ask who the top performers are and what makes them stand out treat the questions you already answer for people as ready made content instead of straining to invent new posts ep 196"],["e195-from-opera-singer-to-software-developer","episode 195 - from opera singer to software developer anna director of product and engineering, axel springer national media and tech treat your job search like a three step process: learn technical skills, build a community, then apply with tailored materials use a germany style education voucher program as inspiration to research free or subsidized reskilling options in your area post your learning journey publicly, even small topics like the difference between class and id help beginners and build your social proof record a short video cover letter answering the questions employers usually ask, like your tech stack and work authorization status pick one or two polished, complete portfolio projects instead of many small ones so you can show you can handle real world complexity ep 195"],["e194-how-i-would-approach-the-job-search","episode 194 - how i would approach the job search   connect with two to three local recruiters even in a remote job market to get a real pulse on hiring trends ask recruiters direct questions like what are the hot jobs and what companies should i research instead of just applying on job boards take interviews outside your target role as a junior to figure out what you actually enjoy and are good at show up consistently to meetups and conferences even if you stay quiet at first, presence matters more than talking connect with every guest and commenter you interact with online, networking is really just making friends ep 194"],["e193-from-long-nights-at-the-er-to-software-developer","episode 193 - from long nights at the er to software developer emmett software developer at an early stage startup, founder of coder dads build a network by consistently sharing and summarizing content from podcasts you actually listen to on twitter if you cannot attend conferences or take months off due to family or work, use small pockets of time to engage online take every call you get during a job search, even rejections can lead to referrals for other roles if you get laid off, ask for a letter of reference from your ceo or manager explaining it was not about your performance cheer on other people's wins in the job search instead of feeling discouraged by their progress ep 193"],["e192-tips-from-a-former-apple-hiring-manager","episode 192 - tips from a former apple hiring manager tiago ghisi former apple hiring manager, engineering director interview every year even when you are happy in your current job so you are ready for great opportunities ask a connection inside a company to submit you as a referral but write your own recommendation for them to use avoid wasting referral energy on a generic resume and instead write specific points on why you are above the bar for the role prepare a bank of star method stories ahead of time so you are not caught off guard in behavior interviews practice system design interviews with time boxing so you do not get stuck on one section like data design and run out of time ep 192"],["e191-job-search-tips-solo","episode 191 - job search tips (solo)   stop chasing a dream job and instead look for the right job for the season of life you are in build name recognition by consistently commenting and engaging on posts from people and companies you want to work with being present and consistent at events like meetups or conferences counts as networking even without talking to people try messaging people on sites where they have fewer followers since you are more likely to get a response be fearless and message that hiring manager, recruiter, or senior developer you have been hesitant to reach out to ep 191"],["e190-branding-content-and-creating-your-own-luck","episode 190 - branding, content, and creating your own luck christina aldan brand strategy consultant and fractional cmo start networking before you need a job, since your personal brand already exists whether you shape it or not pick one or two content formats and stay consistent instead of trying to be everywhere at once if you can't share work details, write about your own journey, struggles, or lessons instead summarizing a talk or article you learned from counts as content and speakers appreciate it at conferences, pick one or two people to connect with and offer to volunteer or live tweet to attend cheaply ep 190"],["e189-how-to-write-a-resume","episode 189 - how to write a resume   put only your name and a work specific email at the top of your resume, skip phone number and address write a summary section with four or five bullet points on years of experience and top skills so recruiters can decide fast list each job with company, start and end date by month and year, position, location, and a quick summary of what the company does break out professional experience by project with a short overview, bullet points on your tasks, and the technologies used keep your resume simple with no pictures, colors, or double columns so your experience is easy to scan ep 189"],["e188-35-interviews-with-0-applications-w-jordan-kohl-staff","episode 188 - 35 interviews with 0 applications w/jordan kohl, staff software engineer at twilio jordan kohl staff software engineer at twilio turn on linkedin signals that show you're open so recruiters come to you instead of chasing applications build a spreadsheet or trello board of target companies based on remote setup, size, and product you actually care about ask every hiring manager and interviewer what their last vacation was to gauge real work-life balance push interviews toward final stages in parallel so you can use competing offers to negotiate pay up set a clear reply template listing comp, pto, and interests so you can filter out recruiters fast and stay consistent ep 188"],["e187-hanging-with-a-friend-w-cam-goedde-product-marketing-manager","episode 187 - hanging with a friend w/cam goedde, product marketing manager at dell cam goedde product marketing manager at dell when switching into a new role, lean into your actual strengths instead of comparing yourself to specialists around you give yourself a few months to step back from day-to-day tasks and figure out what unique value you bring stop waiting for content to be perfect, post the rough version now and let the idea evolve over time build a tangible hobby or physical habit outside of work so you can see real output and avoid burnout when picking communities or projects to join, look for people and teams that are public and transparent rather than anonymous ep 187"],["e186-i-aint-got-time-how-can-recruiters-help-w-daniel-norton-cto","episode 186 - i ain't got time...how can recruiters help w/daniel norton, cto of neural payments daniel norton cto of neural payments treat recruiting partners like a time-saving resource since most hiring managers cannot spend real hours sourcing candidates themselves agency recruiters talk to huge numbers of engineers every year, giving them a live pulse on market pay and availability that job boards cannot match push back on gotcha-style technical interviews and favor a vetting process candidates actually respect consider contract to hire so the staffing partner handles onboarding paperwork and logistics for you bring in junior developers from coding bootcamps since they arrive with practical skills, strong motivation, and let senior engineers move into bigger roles ep 186"],["e185-building-community","episode 185 - building community   carry something like a tap card or qr code to cut networking friction at conferences and meetups pick about five talks and five people to meet at a conference instead of trying to do everything at local meetups narrow down to around ten people to connect with so you do not burn out start networking before layoffs happen by sending one or two dms a day to people in your field build community around something you are already passionate about and keep the first group small ep 185"],["e184-finding-work-in-the-direction-you-want-to-go-w-michael-chan","episode 184 - finding work in the direction you want to go w/ michael chan michael chan  keep showing your work even when you're happy at your job so you're ready if things change pick a lane for putting your work online instead of trying to match everyone else's content output build a tight network of people who will actually pick up the phone instead of chasing huge numbers summarize or recap someone's talk or post online and tag them, it often gets noticed and shared treat every conference like you paid for it yourself and prioritize meeting people over hanging with coworkers ep 184"],["e183-step-by-step-on-how-to-hire-w-jamon-holmgren-founder-cto-at","episode 183 - step by step on how to hire w/jamon holmgren, founder & cto at infinite red, inc. jamon holmgren founder & cto at infinite red, inc. hire slowly on purpose so your culture and process can absorb new people without strain use a detailed careers application form that lets candidates self-select in or out before you screen them have future teammates do the first review of applications so hiring reflects the people candidates will actually work with test communication skills in slack since remote work lives there and misreads cause real problems give candidates honest specific feedback after rejection because even a short no is better than silence ep 183"],["e182-importance-of-soft-skills-and-hiring-w-jm-huret","episode 182 - importance of soft skills and hiring w/ jm huret jm huret head of software engineering for vaco built show genuine interest in life outside work during interviews since it signals comfort connecting as a real person expect interviews to mirror day to day work instead of grilling candidates with algorithms they will never use prepare notes or a written summary of your projects before talking to recruiters so you can speak clearly under pressure when you lack experience in something, explain how you approached a similar problem rather than giving a flat no pay attention early in your career to whether you actually enjoy the work itself, not just the money it can bring ep 182"],["e181-advice-to-hiring-managers-w-stephen-white","episode 181 - advice to hiring managers w/ stephen white stephen white avp of software engineering at healthtrust performance group focus on missionaries over mercenaries by helping people understand the problem, not just handing them a ticket build culture around shared values and how people actually work day to day, not ping pong tables or free beer let teams decide how they want to work and give them only the tools they need, then get out of the way as a new manager put your organization and team ahead of your own career ambitions and be patient about moving up as a candidate turn interview questions back on the employer and ask how they live out their values day to day ep 181"],["e180-so-you-think-youre-a-senior-engineer-w-andrew-wyborski","episode 180 - so you think you're a senior engineer w/ andrew wyborski andrew wyborski engineering manager at kroger separate the question of skill level from title and salary band since every company labels seniority differently senior engineers impact a wider group of teams, not just their own, by setting direction and standards know why you call yourself senior and back it up with how you adapted, mentored, and solved harder problems, not just years of experience keep a running log of your wins in cause, action, result format so review time and resume updates are easy ask hiring managers why a requirement like a degree exists so you can judge if it is a real need or just a checkbox ep 180"],["e179-locker-room-to-board-room-w-brian-dougherty","episode 179 - locker room to board room w/ brian dougherty brian dougherty chief operating officer slash chief technology officer at jumpstart health investors respect roles on a team and let specialists do their job instead of micromanaging out of your lane good leaders take ownership of failures and let the team own the wins so people can play loose instead of scared use 360 degree reviews and genuinely open door policies to surface honest feedback about leadership in crunch time look to the teammates next to you for motivation instead of just working for the company or the boss when starting out or leading later, volunteer to try other roles outside your usual lane to build context and value ep 179"],["e178-better-ways-for-evaluating-job-seekers-w-josh-cypher","episode 178 - better ways for evaluating job seekers w/ josh cypher josh cypher senior devops engineer at sonos ask hiring managers for a technical screen instead of a blind code challenge if that format doesn't play to your strengths remember you are interviewing the company just as much as they are interviewing you ask tough questions like what is the top reason you shouldn't work here to expose potential red flags when asking about compensation, get a salary range and ask what drives the top and bottom of that band lean into your non-software background and personal experiences since they can set you apart in interviews ep 178"],["e177-productivity-mindset-w-colin-young","episode 177 - productivity mindset w/ colin young colin young exos focus on autonomy, mastery, and purpose when trying to build a new habit that actually sticks start small with stillness practice, even one minute, instead of jumping straight to long meditation sessions treat burnout as a signal to add recovery and restraint, not just an excuse to work more hours create friction against bad habits, like moving your phone to another room or keeping snacks out of reach be authentic in your career instead of copying how other leaders act, and take time to reflect on what you have accomplished ep 177"],["e176-the-power-of-branding-and-networking-w-ken-wheeler","episode 176 - the power of branding and networking w/ ken wheeler ken wheeler developer, open source creator and speaker open source work can boost your name recognition and open networking doors even if the ecosystem is more formalized now than it used to be being authentic online has upsides and downsides, it can build trust but may clash with traditional office politics get involved in communities like discord, github, meetups or conferences because referrals make getting hired far easier than going in cold at conferences leave your group circle open so others can join, and approach speakers after their talks since they want to connect too watch your ratio of consumption versus creation and try to be a net creator since that has the bigger professional payoff ep 176"],["e175-onsite-to-fully-remote","episode 175 - onsite to fully remote sarah head of business development at vault athletics test a four day work week with a trial period and survey employees to see if productivity actually holds up, not just morale build in forced rest between work sprints since burnout drops output just like it does for athletes in training replace office water cooler moments with small structured things like random small group chats or casual team channels for non work talk give people a personal development fund and a remote work stipend so they can choose what actually helps them, instead of generic happy hours let employees choose their schedule and location instead of forcing onsite days, since forced choices breed resistance and autonomy builds buy in ep 175"],["e174-content-creation-101-w-alex-sheridan","episode 174 - content creation 101 w/ alex sheridan alex sheridan founder of impact media marketing start simple by recording yourself on a webcam or smartphone during normal conversations and turn that footage into posts pick two or three content pillars tied to what your audience cares about instead of posting about everything separate personal content from private content so you share real experience without oversharing your life if you are camera shy use text overlays and voiceover instead of talking straight into the camera download tiktok and spend time learning basic recording and auto captions as the easiest way to start making video ep 174"],["e173-networking-101-linkedin-tips-w-me","episode 173 - networking 101 & linkedin tips w/ me :) me :)  use a dot card or qr code at conferences to cut friction instead of relying on traditional business cards before a conference get your linkedin, twitter, or instagram fully set up so people can connect with you right away pick a handful of sessions, speakers, or people you want to meet at a conference instead of trying to do everything on linkedin fix your banner photo, title, and featured section since these are what recruiters and connections see first your linkedin profile is searchable by recruiters using keywords, while your resume usually is not ep 173"],["e172-recruiter-life-w-hunter-mitchem","episode 172 - recruiter life w/ hunter mitchem hunter mitchem agency recruiter focused on software development ask about company financials and funding before accepting a job to gauge stability find a recruiter with tenure who knows the local market and specific tech stacks keep your linkedin profile updated since recruiters rely on it to match you to roles check in with recruiters every week or two so you stay top of mind for new roles give recruiters a salary range and trust their read on where you stack up in the market ep 172"],["e171-leading-globally-in-a-remote-setting-w-shannon-smith","episode 171 - leading globally in a remote setting w/ shannon smith shannon smith vp of engineering at ona keep remote team conversations organized by using dedicated channels for specific squads or groups instead of one big chat build in informal time like a 15 minute coffee chat before standups so remote teammates can bond before diving into work when planning in person meetups, give people advance notice so they can plan around family and other obligations set up a buddy program for new hires so they have a safe person to ask basic questions without feeling judged on your resume or linkedin, dont just list technical skills, explain what makes you uniquely valuable compared to other candidates ep 171"],["e170-content-creation-and-the-job-search-w-me","episode 170 - content creation and the job search w/ me :) me :)  document what you learn every day and post it, even short two or three sentence updates count as content if someone corrects your post, dm them to say thanks and ask for a quick call, this builds your network fast treat being wrong online as a chance to learn, not something to hide or delete post video recaps on tiktok of what you learned that day, even with zero followers you can get real views hiring managers should post about how they lead and what they believe to attract talent instead of relying only on job boards ep 170"],["e169-faang-the-job-search-w-andy-wong","episode 169 - faang & the job search w/ andy wong andy wong senior software engineer at block treat coding bootcamp as only worth it if you put in serious effort, not because of a placement rate promise expect to fail big tech interviews at least once, learn from it, and try again since the process can reopen in six to twelve months lean into your interviewer during fang style interviews, ask questions, and treat it like a conversation instead of a solo test if you dont know an answer in an interview say so and work through it together instead of freezing up in salary talks assume you have negotiating power, know your options, and be willing to walk away ep 169"],["e168-importance-of-conflict-w-liz-kislik","episode 168 - importance of conflict w/ liz kislik liz kislik management consultant and executive coach irrational anger usually signals a boundary violation, not a character flaw calm your body first by noticing your feet, surroundings, or what you can hear to stop a stress spiral rebuild routines gradually, like meeting a colleague for coffee before jumping back into full social schedules talk to yourself in second person to create distance from harsh self judgment look back at what you have already accomplished before assuming you are failing or an imposter ep 168"],["e167-hiring-quickly-and-keeping-the-soul-w-tommy-norman","episode 167 - hiring quickly and keeping the soul w/ tommy norman tommy norman chief agility officer at aquanaevo when scaling a company, know the people who thrived early on may not be the right fit for the next growth stage define core values with specific examples, not buzzwords like integrity, so new hires know how to actually live them let teams try to self correct conflict first through working agreements before escalating to formal coaching or a pip hiring managers lose candidates mainly from lack of communication, so overcommunicate even when there is no new update pay close enough to market rate that money stops being the deciding factor, then win on autonomy, purpose and mastery ep 167"],["e166-navigating-linkedin-w-me","episode 166 - navigating linkedin w/ me :) matthew vp of software architecture and development, vault systems make your linkedin banner and headline explain what you actually do, not just a job title or company name write your profile so someone reading it thinks wow tell me more, same rule applies to resumes add a featured section on linkedin to point people to your github, portfolio, or side projects network inside your own company now, so if a colleague leaves for a new job they may bring you with them when dming someone for advice, give a specific short time ask like six or seven minutes so they know what they are agreeing to ep 166"],["e165-the-power-of-the-dm-w-conner-bush","episode 165 - the power of the dm w/ conner bush conner bush software engineer at netflix rank target companies with a weighted scorecard using prestige pay purpose and people to see what you actually value skip the generic application and dm engineering managers directly but expect a low response rate so send a lot of them check a person's recent linkedin activity before messaging since inactive profiles rarely respond keep outreach short specific and time boxed like asking for eight minutes and tie it to something you both have in common when starting a new remote role spend the first few weeks scheduling one on ones across teams before diving into the work itself ep 165"],["e164-building-tech-culture-from-the-ground-up-w-brian-hogan","episode 164 - building tech culture from the ground up w/ brian hogan brian hogan director of education at temporal push for sponsorship, not just mentorship, by actively opening doors and advocating for junior developers in your network on resumes, show accomplishments with quantifiable results instead of listing responsibilities to stand out from hundreds of applicants good leaders build psychological safety and trust over time, modeling behaviors like taking time off so their team feels safe doing the same research company culture through back channel conversations with current or former employees rather than relying only on interview answers focus on people skills alongside technical skills since much of software work involves communication, feedback, and pitching ideas ep 164"],["e163-wild-a-market-w-jermaine-murray","episode 163 - wild a** market w/ jermaine murray jermaine murray technical recruiter at wealthsimple and career coach ats rejection emails are automated but a real recruiter made the decision to reject you if you keep getting rejected after many applications, revisit your resume and the roles you are targeting instead of just applying more look beyond software development roles like data analyst, business analyst, or project coordinator to break into tech use boolean string searches with your core skills on job sites to find relevant postings even without knowing the exact job title when rejecting candidates after multiple interviews, offer a short feedback call instead of just a rejection email ep 163"],["e162-tis-the-season-of-conferences-w-me","episode 162 - tis the season of conferences w/ me :) me :)  before a conference pick five to ten speakers you actually want to meet instead of trying to see everyone you dont have to attend every session, choose a handful of must see talks and give yourself breaks connect with every person you meet on social media right away since a business card alone usually gets forgotten stick near one or two people you already know at a conference to get introduced into new circles in the hallway track its fine to skip sessions and rest in your hotel room if a conference starts feeling overwhelming ep 162"],["e161-finance-talk-because-we-need-it-w-steve-alverson","episode 161 - finance talk...because we need it w/ steve alverson steve alverson certified financial planner ask if your compensation package includes an opportunity for ownership before diving into details on shares know the difference between restricted shares, which are given to you, and options, which let you buy stock at a set price understand your vesting schedule since most shares vest over three to five years and you forfeit unvested shares if you leave early ask about a company's cash reserves and viability before accepting an offer, especially at smaller or private companies don't let company stock become a huge chunk of your net worth since smaller company share prices can be very volatile ep 161"],["e160-the-power-of-community-w-will-johnson","episode 160 - the power of community w/ will johnson will johnson developer advocate at auth0 document what you are currently learning publicly instead of waiting until you feel like an expert meetups are a way to learn the local industry, find mentors, and meet people who can vouch for you later building relationships before you need them turns a cold application into a warm referral speaking at meetups or conferences makes people come to you instead of you chasing connections approach every interaction by asking how you can help the other person, not what you can get from them ep 160"],["e159-tips-to-hiring-managers-w-luca-cipriani","episode 159 - tips to hiring managers w/ luca cipriani luca cipriani engineering manager at jimdo as a hiring manager, look for candidates through github contributions, twitter, and open source activity, not just resumes job seekers should put themselves out there online since a strong public presence can make up for a weak resume or cover letter the real difference between mid and senior engineers is honest pushback and negotiation, not just technical skill build diverse teams with a mix of junior and senior people since mentoring junior talent helps senior engineers grow too use okrs to align business and engineering, keep objectives few and clear, and communicate them constantly across the company ep 159"],["e158-leading-for-engineers-w-kishau-rogers","episode 158 - leading for engineers w/ kishau rogers kishau rogers ceo at time study define success metrics with your engineering team so everyone aligns on what matters and can work their own way build rest into project planning because burnout shows up as slower, less creative work if you dont create sandbox time so teams can tinker and break things without judgment before building the real thing use transparent hiring steps and prep candidates on what to expect so interviews feel conversational, not an interrogation in job interviews explain the impact of your work, not just the skills, including customer feedback and team context ep 158"],["e157-the-path-to-principal-w-rich-rader","episode 157 - the path to principal w/ rich rader rich rader principal engineer at evacore a principal engineer role is still individual contributor work but carries director level responsibility and trust staff and principal titles are often interchangeable, but senior and lead vary a lot by company so ask about actual responsibilities extreme ownership means removing anything between you and a happy customer and fixing what you see even if it is not technically your job healthy retention comes from rewarding teaching and growth, not rewarding people for working unpaid overtime during big launches building strong remote culture takes intentional effort like regular culture discussions and team activities, not just being in an office ep 157"],["e156-management-road-map-w-eric-yager","episode 156 - management road map w/ eric yager eric yager site tech lead, amazon air science technology organization expect competition for talent not just from other companies but from other teams inside the same large organization offer flexibility on remote work and location since engineers now have many options and recruiters reach out constantly write down team tenants so decisions can still happen smoothly when the manager is not in the room run one on ones every week with direct reports and plan specific topics ahead of time instead of winging it if you lack the exact skills for an entry level tech role, take an adjacent role to get in the door and then work toward the role you want ep 156"],["e155-lay-of-the-land-w-me","episode 155 - lay of the land w/ me :) me :)  stop comparing your career timeline to peers since it only steals your focus and joy ask detailed questions in interviews so you are not surprised by the role six months later treat contract to hire like a working interview but confirm the expected conversion salary upfront expect some pay dip when converting from hourly to salary since hourly often covers benefits and pto building strong culture remotely is possible but it takes real intentionality, not just copying onsite habits ep 155"],["e154-job-hunting-thoughts-w-john-michael-mccasland","episode 154 - job hunting thoughts w/ john-michael mccasland john-michael mccasland front end engineer at upscribe set a clear salary goal before taking calls so you can gauge offers against something concrete use a job specific email plus a trello board to track every touch point with recruiters keep taking calls even when stretched thin because the right fit often shows up late in the process ask interviewers directly if they have concerns about you to quickly learn where you stand be upfront about your stress and interest level with a company if you want them to move fast and be honest back ep 154"],["e153-negotiations-career-progression-ensuring-fairness-w-ingrid","episode 153 - negotiations, career progression, & ensuring fairness w/ ingrid epure ingrid epure senior staff engineer at netlify always negotiate, whether it is initial job offers, day to day role decisions, or career progression discussions build personal rapport in negotiations by asking questions and listening instead of jumping straight to solutions it is okay for your career path to look different over time, lateral moves and team switches are normal and valuable set longer term learning goals tied to industry trends instead of comparing your progress to other people's timelines push for real representation and equal opportunities for minorities instead of just diversity talk or campaigns ep 153"],["e152-how-to-incentivize-developers-w-nick-frederick","episode 152 - how to incentivize developers w/ nick frederick nick frederick ceo of rebar technology share business context and pipeline updates with developers so they feel part of the bigger mission not just their task run regular one on ones even short ones to build rapport and surface honest feedback over time start every new hire with full trust then adjust based on the quality and consistency of their work commission plans for developers sound appealing but are hard to execute since you cant fairly attribute revenue to individual code prioritize cultural fit and the ability to learn over existing technical skills since tools change every few years ep 152"],["e151-content-culture-and-devrel-w-colby-fayock","episode 151 - content, culture, and devrel w/ colby fayock colby fayock senior developer experience engineer at cloudinary writing down what you just learned shows employers you are actively learning and growing creating content reinforces your own learning because explaining a concept forces you to understand it fully set work hours on slack and your calendar so teammates know when not to expect a response networking through a direct message can lead to a job faster than submitting applications through postings turn your camera on during remote calls because seeing faces builds more trust with teammates ep 151"],["e150-finding-your-truth-w-anthony-mays","episode 150 - finding your truth w/ anthony mays anthony mays former software engineer at google, now consultant helping job seekers and companies on hiring underrepresented talent slow down before targeting big tech, understand the mindset and work ethic the interview process actually demands build your network by being genuinely social on linkedin, not just sending connection requests or cold dming your resume practice writing and telling your story regularly so you can clearly frame your value on your resume always negotiate every offer, do your homework by comparing with others, and never let anyone rush your decision hiring managers can support underrepresented candidates by trimming bloated requirements and being transparent about expectations ep 150"],["e149-setting-boundaries-w-shaundai-person","episode 149 - setting boundaries w/ shaundai person shaundai person senior software engineer at netflix cut out streaming apps and social media when you are trying to learn to code or job search on the side pick a few non-negotiables like family and your current job, then build your schedule around protecting those first before saying yes to a new ask, check if it benefits the other person, benefits you, and benefits your company or goals set a hard stop time for work each day and actually shut the computer down to protect your personal time networking through speaking, twitter and genuine conversations can lead directly to job opportunities without ever cold pitching for a role ep 149"],["e148-how-to-be-a-better-manager-w-ashley-hunsberger","episode 148 - how to be a better manager w/ ashley hunsberger ashley hunsberger director of developer advocacy at blackboard (part of anthology) as a hiring manager remember candidates are also deciding if your team and company are a good fit for them share your interview themes and questions ahead of time so candidates can tell real stories instead of reciting a script model self care as a leader by actually taking the time off you tell your team to take if you give regular one on one feedback your quarterly or annual review should just summarize what you already told them ask so many questions in interviews that you could talk yourself out of the offer to really understand the opportunity ep 148"],["e147-things-to-consider-before-leaving-w-me","episode 147 - things to consider before leaving w/ me :) me :)  before jumping ship, check your base salary, bonus, 401k match, hsa contribution and pto policy first benefit monthly costs matter a lot and can make a new offer look worse than your current job ask if your problem is industry wide or specific to your company before assuming a new job fixes it try a direct conversation with leadership before quitting, since many issues can be addressed in a meeting when raising concerns with a manager, frame it as how you feel and bring suggestions instead of attacking ep 147"],["e146-how-to-improve-the-hiring-process-w-nate-taylor","episode 146 - how to improve the hiring process w/ nate taylor nate taylor software engineering director at pluralsight write resume bullets that explain the problem you solved, not just the tech stack or process you used a resume with a few sentences of context can tell a hiring manager more than ten generic bullet points employment gaps are not a dealbreaker if your total experience still shows you can do the job long interview processes with too many touch points can cause good candidates to drop out on their own before hiring, weigh the time to find a perfect fit against the time it takes to ramp up someone less experienced ep 146"],["e145-work-life-balance-w-gabe-greenberg","episode 145 - work life balance w/ gabe greenberg gabe greenberg founder of g2i experiment with a four day or reduced hour work week without cutting pay to boost retention and output write job descriptions that are simple, fun and transparent instead of dry and generic use a short video from the hiring manager to give candidates real context on the role when raising work life balance with a manager, frame it as how you feel rather than what you need watch for workaholism as a reliance on adrenaline, not just long hours, and address the underlying habit ep 145"],["e144-discovering-your-super-power-w-heather-downing","episode 144 - discovering your super power w/ heather downing heather downing senior developer advocate at okta look for contractors who communicate quickly and clearly since patience and clarity matter more than raw skill it is okay to say you dont know something as a contractor and offer to research it if the client pays for that time time box your interview schedule so the job search does not take over every day of your week stay in touch with former coworkers since they often become the ones who refer you to your next job when asking a manager for help, bring what you have already done and what you plan to do next so they can just say yes or no ep 144"],["e143-how-to-break-into-faang-w-genesis-bonds","episode 143 - how to break into faang w/ genesis bonds genesis bonds software engineer at facebook/meta and director of social engagement for text givin treat bootcamps as a two way investment, look for programs that have real skin in the game for your success write out coding problems by hand on paper since real interviews wont let you use an ide or compiler record yourself answering interview questions to catch filler words and awkward pauses before the real thing study the specific company you are interviewing with since interview styles differ, like data structure complexity questions at google dont let one rejection discourage you, a no from one company just means it wasnt the right fit, not a reflection of your worth ep 143"],["e142-from-startups-to-corporate-w-brian-dougherty","episode 142 - from startups to corporate w/ brian dougherty brian dougherty chief operations officer and chief technical officer at jumpstart health investors avoid making big changes in your first 90 days at a new organization so you can see what truly motivates each person get a clear read on team culture before trying to fix anything, then build a new mission and values with the team, not for them watch for self regulating culture, like teammates calling each other out on values, as a sign the culture is actually working be careful when narrowing a strong performer's scope of responsibility since it can feel demoralizing if not handled thoughtfully help each person grow by figuring out if they want to be a generalist, a subject matter expert, or move into management, then assign work that fits ep 142"],["e141-building-good-engineering-culture-w-chris-rathgeb","episode 141 - building good engineering culture w/ chris rathgeb chris rathgeb cto at curemit build engineering culture by defining clear company values first, then hire people who align with them write explicit, detailed definitions of success for each role so people are never blindsided on performance hold weekly one on ones and do deeper quarterly reviews that revisit values and role fit normalize code review from day one by pairing new engineers with a buddy and having them review pull requests early when job hunting, ask how the company actually works day to day and how early engineers get involved in planning ep 141"],["e140-job-search-timing-w-me","episode 140 - job search timing w/ me me  avoid walking around with an inflated title like vp of engineering or cto if you only have a few years of experience, it can spook hiring managers if your experience justifies a senior title, explain and sell that context clearly in your resume and to your recruiter ask recruiters or hiring managers directly if a position is budgetarily approved before investing time in the interview process ask how many other people are currently in the interview process so you understand your competition if you get rejected, ask for specific quantifiable feedback instead of vague comments like culture fit so you know what to improve ep 140"],["e139-salary-negotiations-and-onboarding-w-dan-digangi","episode 139 - salary negotiations and onboarding w/ dan digangi dan digangi engineering manager at docusign ground your salary ask in specific reasons like skills or market gaps, not just a number you want avoid saying a competing offer like a big name company is why you deserve more pay it is fine to counter an offer within reason, just do not throw out wildly unrealistic numbers look at the whole compensation picture including benefits, 401k match, and pto, not just base salary schedule one on ones with teammates early in a new role to build connections and speed up onboarding ep 139"],["e138-observations-with-tay","episode 138 - observations with tay   if your new job feels chaotic or overpromised, it is okay to start looking again even after a few months get raises, title changes, and other promises in writing before you accept a role leave a job over systemic issues like a bad manager or a role that does not match what was promised, not after one bad day work through conflict with a conversation first, then move on if nothing changes after that talk if you want fully remote work, ask directly about remote policy and return to office plans before accepting ep 138"],["e137-distributed-leadership-w-george-livingston","episode 137 - distributed leadership w/ george livingston george livingston leads product and software development at the escape game build a culture of writing since distributed teams lose the easy in person context of a quick tap on the shoulder carve out intentional unstructured time to replace water cooler moments that no longer happen by accident bring distributed teams together in person when possible, even just a monthly lunch, to build real camaraderie try structuring one on ones as a third chit chat, a third work talk, and a third how can i help job seekers should follow up after interviews since strong written communication stands out in a distributed hiring process ep 137"],["e136-helping-job-seekers-and-managers-w-matt-fesler","episode 136 - helping job seekers and managers w/ matt fesler matt fesler engineering manager at navi health when reaching out to a hiring manager, don't hesitate, even if the job feels out of reach you'll gain advice or a connection ask a hiring manager for a short call on what they like to see in junior developer interviews to sharpen your approach keep public code repos accessible and compilable since managers or senior engineers may look through them before an interview be fully honest on your resume and be ready to speak in depth about anything you list, no matter your seniority after an interview, give tactful feedback through your recruiter or in the q&a portion to help improve the process for others ep 136"],["e135-web-3-and-developer-dao-w-isiah-hill","episode 135 - web 3 and developer_dao w/ isiah hill isiah hill works with developer dao, contributing to get coin dao and the radical team treat web3 work as being a facilitator or liaison who can speak to multiple teams rather than trying to be the leader in charge approach web3 as a constant student of the game since the space changes fast and nobody stays an expert for long before joining a dao, get clear on what you are interested in so people can point you toward the right community look for daos matched to your interest, like design focused groups, social token projects, esports, or charity daos before investing in crypto, start with the documentation and do real due diligence instead of jumping in blind ep 135"],["e134-philosophies-on-hiring-and-culture-w-ashley-hunsberger","episode 134 - philosophies on hiring and culture w/ ashley hunsberger ashley hunsberger director of developer advocacy and velocity lab at anthology lead with people first because nobody remembers the project at the end of the day, they remember how you made them feel motivate your team by involving them in setting their own goals instead of leading through fear or pressure try flexible schedules like a four day work week and evaluate weekly if goals are still being met focus one on ones on relationship building and coaching rather than status updates you can already get on slack defrag your calendar around your natural energy so strategic thinking, creative work, and listening happen at the right times of day ep 134"],["e133-developer-advocacy-and-the-job-search-w-ali-spittel","episode 133 - developer advocacy and the job search w/ ali spittel ali spittel leads developer advocacy at aws amplify build a portfolio site instead of relying only on linkedin, since you control the story and can show real projects skip the generic to-do list or restaurant app and build something you would actually use yourself teaching a concept through a blog post, video, or talk proves you understand it and builds communication skills employers want do the job before you get the job by creating content, speaking, or building demos in the space you want to break into keep team morale up by sharing positive user feedback internally, not just flagging what is broken ep 133"],["e132-the-other-side-of-recruiting-w-james-arnold","episode 132 - the other side of recruiting w/ james arnold james arnold recruiter at an autonomous vehicle startup tailor your resume to the job description so the recruiter can match your bullet points to what they need reach out to recruiters before you apply so they can help you decide if it is worth your time when you dm a recruiter, state what you want from the interaction instead of a vague ask for a job the ats is not rejecting you with hidden magic, a human reviews almost every application treat interviews as a chance to learn even if they do not work out, every one teaches you something ep 132"],["e131-the-importance-of-mentoring-w-alishah-novin","episode 131 - the importance of mentoring w/ alishah novin alishah novin director of engineering look for a mentor who has no personal stake in your success so they can give an outside, honest perspective when reaching out to a potential mentor, lead with situation, ask, and background so busy people can respond quickly the mentee should drive the relationship by coming with a clear, specific ask instead of a vague open-ended question use the what else question repeatedly in mentoring conversations to get past surface answers and uncover real issues companies should avoid pairing people with their direct manager as a mentor since that creates bias and limits honest feedback ep 131"],["e130-the-secret-sauce-for-hiring-and-job-seekers-w-david-cabal","episode 130 - the secret sauce for hiring and job seekers w/ david cabal david cabal engineering manager at exodus pay candidates upfront for take home code challenges to show you value their time weigh soft skills and humility as heavily as technical skill since attitude affects how someone takes feedback on a team keep hiring moving fast by reviewing code challenges within 24 to 48 hours and letting candidates pick the next interview slot stay laser focused on the delivery date and push non critical bugs and extra features to the backlog instead of chasing perfection as a candidate come with prepared questions for the interviewer since most people skip this and it instantly sets you apart ep 130"],["e129-how-to-partner-with-a-recruiting-firm-w-ben-henderson","episode 129 - how to partner with a recruiting firm w/ ben henderson ben henderson vp of software engineering at celera health interview your recruiting partner like a hire, make sure they understand what you actually need beyond the job description job descriptions should sell the role and team honestly, including real tech stack and work intensity, not just a bulleted list being upfront about challenges and technical debt during interviews builds trust and reduces early quits mix direct hire, contract, contract-to-hire and part-time talent instead of relying on one hiring model carve out projects that do not require full domain expertise so contractors can ramp up fast without slowing the core team ep 129"],["e128-how-to-negotiate-an-offer","episode 128 - how to negotiate an offer   when you get an offer, keep your mouth shut and let the hiring manager keep talking ask detailed questions about pay schedule, bonus structure and benefits before responding to an offer always ask for time to review an offer instead of deciding on the spot if you counter an offer, always give a clear why backed by research like glassdoor data confirm your salary range is dependent on seeing the full benefits package before locking in a number ep 128"],["e127-networking-becoming-an-independent-and-paypal-w-kent-c-dodds","episode 127 - networking, becoming an independent, and paypal w/ kent c. dodds kent c. dodds remix build a public reputation through speaking and content creation so opportunities come to you instead of chasing applications sharing what you learn at meetups or conferences can lead directly to job offers from people who see your talk demonstrating skill publicly matters as much as having the skill, since nobody hires talent they cant see if an interview process relies on leetcode style questions, ask the recruiter what to expect and prepare with study resources ahead of time being easy to work with matters more than being the smartest or hardest working person in the room ep 127"],["e126-a-tale-of-hiring-devrel-and-various-other-things-w-ted","episode 126 - a tale of hiring, devrel, and various other things w/ ted neward ted neward technologist and author look past checklist hiring and judge candidates by depth and transferable skills, not just exact tech matches a cs degree is not required to succeed in tech, so don't let a job post requiring one stop you from applying build your network and personal brand through local user groups, volunteering, and teaching, not just conferences don't take job rejection personally since most of the time it comes down to fit or circumstances outside your control when hiring juniors, pair them with a strong mentor, build in psychological safety, and invest time before expecting full productivity ep 126"],["e125-the-art-of-freelancing-w-rob-joseph","episode 125 - the art of freelancing w/ rob joseph rob joseph android app developer and host of coffee and coding podcast build a portfolio early since it proves you can do the work before you have real experience to point to save enough money to cover a few months of expenses before going freelance so fear of drying up work doesn't drive bad decisions expect contracting to mean set hours like a job while freelancing means delivering a result by a deadline, however long it takes you referrals from other developers often become your best source of new gigs once you have done good work for someone put your work where people can find it, like an app store listing or simple webpage, so your name backs up what you say you can do ep 125"],["e124-creativity-in-hiring-especially-around-junior-devs-w-andrew","episode 124 - creativity in hiring especially around junior devs w/ andrew wyborski andrew wyborski engineering manager at kroger when hiring junior devs, look past resumes for stories of them automating or improving a tedious part of their old job use coding exams as a conversation starter, not a pass-fail gate, so interviews focus on problem solving not syntax if you cannot find time to teach new hires basic skills, that is a sign you are not investing in your current team either build your network before you need it, join local dev meetups and keep a master resume with every skill and project listed hiring managers should push junior talent into uncomfortable stretch goals since that discomfort is what drives real growth ep 124"],["e123-w-my-dad","episode 123 - w/my dad dave desseyn taylor's father, restaurant industry manager when starting a new job, look at what needs to be done and just start doing it without waiting to be told if you leave a long career to find something new, give yourself time to figure out if you miss the work itself or just the people in interviews, show your character and integrity instead of letting the interviewer do all the talking as a leader, check in on people's lives outside of work, not just their tasks, so they feel a real connection when someone makes a mistake, want ownership and a plan to improve rather than excuses or blame ep 123"],["e122-networking-consulting-and-more-than-a-salary-w-jason-bock","episode 122 - networking, consulting, and more than a salary w/ jason bock jason bock developer advocate at rocket mortgage weigh salary against things like insurance, 401k, time off, and culture when choosing a job independent contracting means more income but you handle your own taxes and insurance, so build a financial buffer working through a consulting firm can offer a middle ground between full freelance and a single full-time role as an introvert, aim to make one or two real connections at an event instead of trying to meet everyone ask for equal time in interviews so you can ask questions and vet the company just as much as they vet you ep 122"],["e121-startups-and-junior-talent-w-haley-zapolski","episode 121 - startups and leveraging junior talent w/ haley zapolski haley zapolski manager of employer engagement at nashville software school ask detailed questions about work from home policy before accepting an offer since flexible can still mean three days in office nail down how much time youll spend coding versus internal meetings versus client calls before taking a job if you cant think of a question in an interview ask one anyway since having none signals you dont care before accepting an offer get on another call with questions and see how willing the company is to invest in you companies struggling to hire senior developers are increasingly building programs to train junior and bootcamp talent instead ep 121"],["e120-self-reflection-and-consulting-w-jeff-ramsey","episode 120 - self reflection and consulting w/ jeff ramsey jeff ramsey leads accounting and finance consulting practice nationally at vaco use consulting projects to test a company's culture before committing to a full time role expect most contract roles to require around 40 hours a week even though pay is often hourly ask any recruiting or consulting firm what your long term career goals are and see if they actually listen use a contract role at a company on the fringes of an industry as a stepping stone into that industry lean on recruiters to learn the market instead of only relying on job boards and easy apply ep 120"],["e119-the-importance-of-partnering-with-a-recruiting-firm-w","episode 119 - the importance of partnering with a recruiting firm w/ jessica kashyap jessica kashyap recruiting leader focused on accounting and finance hiring initiatives at vaco partnering exclusively with one recruiting firm creates accountability since there is no one else to point fingers at if hiring goes wrong mixed messaging across multiple agencies confuses candidates and makes it hard to control the narrative in the marketplace candidates have options and will only commit to two or so opportunities that sound most enticing, so slow processes lose talent speed matters, some hiring cycles moved from friday interview to offer within about five business days a good recruiter will tell you honestly when they are not the right fit instead of forcing a square peg into a round hole ep 119"],["e118-the-importance-of-pto-w-kelly-vaughn","episode 118 - the importance of pto w/ kelly vaughn kelly vaughn business owner, the taproom and gavalo offer unlimited pto with a required minimum, like 20 days a year, so people actually take time off as a leader, model taking time off yourself so your team feels safe doing the same use scheduling tools so messages and emails don't land on people's phones late at night and cause anxiety in interviews, be upfront about what you know and don't know instead of pretending to have experience you lack job seekers should ask hard questions back and talk to current employees to research a company before accepting an offer ep 118"],["e117-2022-hiring-outlook-perfecting-interviewing-w-kyle-allen","episode 117 - 2022 hiring outlook & perfecting interviewing w/ kyle allen kyle allen managing partner, nashville office focus on finding talent instead of just filling a role, know the two or three things that make someone successful in the job spend more interview time selling the opportunity, aim for roughly 20 minutes on the candidate and 40 minutes on the role keep the process to about three rounds within ten business days so you do not lose candidates to slow decisions do technical assessments live as a paired exercise during the interview instead of giving take home tests candidates often undervalue flexibility and autonomy and overvalue a raise once you factor in taxes it is smaller than it looks ep 117"],["e116-w-justin-hoover-how-to-build-out-hiring-processes","episode 116 - w/justin hoover + how to build out hiring processes justin hoover it professional at alliance bernstein skip pure computer science whiteboard challenges since they rarely reflect real day to day work and add undue stress when screening candidates look for three tells: do they relieve stress, do they show perseverance, and do they show a spark of brilliance in solving new problems pair program with candidates on a realistic business problem instead of arbitrary algorithm puzzles to see how you actually collaborate have a small cross section of the team interview together so they can build on each others questions and judge culture fit be transparent with candidates at every step since a cheap or dishonest interview process damages your reputation and scares off good candidates ep 116"],["e115-w-david-khourshid-from-microsoft-to-founder","episode 115 - w/david khourshid + from microsoft to founder david khourshid founder, stately open source contributions can get you noticed by big tech recruiters, even without applying directly big tech interviews often test memorized algorithms that have little to do with the actual job working at a big company can still feel like a startup if you land on the right team early startup hires can skip technical interviews if you already know their work from open source in interviews, focus on the candidate's career goals and interests, not just salary and benefits ep 115"],["e114-failing-fast-and-being-vulnerable-w-cassandra-faris","episode 114 - failing fast and being vulnerable w/ cassandra faris cassandra faris technical community manager at rocket being open about personal struggles with managers and coworkers can build trust instead of damaging your reputation run experiments for a set time then do a real retrospective on what worked and what did not find a couple trusted people you can be vulnerable with instead of expecting the whole company to hold space for you before giving advice ask the other person if they want advice or just want to be heard ask someone if now is a good time to talk before diving into a serious conversation so they can actually listen ep 114"],["e113-recruiter-turned-developer-w-adam-brown","episode 113 - recruiter turned developer w/adam brown adam brown software engineer at twitch, former recruiter focus on one technology first instead of jumping between languages so you actually build depth pick your first technology based on what naturally extends into high demand skills like react after javascript ask multiple recruiters for market insight since they talk to candidates, hiring managers and each other all day pairing a brand new hire with someone just a year into the role can speed up ramp up for both people frame questions you feel you should already know the answer to with self aware humor so you come across confident, not clueless ep 113"],["e112-leveling-up-your-career-w-ken-coleman","episode 112 - leveling up your career w/ken coleman ken coleman broadcaster, author, and career coach don't change jobs just for a bigger paycheck since the good feeling fades in about six to eight weeks find your purpose by matching what you do best with work you love and results that matter to you as a leader your ability to grow is limited by the talent you bring onto your team to retain people, check in on them personally and ask how you can help them win professionally build soft skills like being likable, coachable, reliable, adaptable, and honorable to stand out in tech ep 112"],["e111-w-jacob-herrington-sr-software-engineer","episode 111 - w/jacob herrington, sr. software engineer jacob herrington sr. software engineer rank companies into tiers like a, b, c, and absolutely not to stay organized during a busy search ask companies directly how they ensure pay bands are fair so people doing the same work aren't paid unequally be wary of recruiters who ask for a long call right away since that can signal a long drawn out process put yourself out there on social media and content creation to become discoverable instead of relying only on applications don't be intimidated by job postings since most requirements are a wish list and no candidate checks every box ep 111"],["e110-w-barret-blake-practice-area-manager","episode 110 - w/barret blake, practice area manager barret blake practice area manager networking in college or past jobs often leads directly to future job offers, so build those relationships early long drawn out interview processes with many rounds can signal poor company culture at a prospective employer hiring managers often value communication skills and culture fit as much as or more than raw technical skills keep resumes to a single page with a short bullet list of top skills and brief paragraphs per job practice talking about your accomplishments with family so you can confidently brag about yourself in interviews ep 110"],["e109-w-brittany-garrett-current-nashville-software-school-student","episode 109 - w/brittany garrett, current nashville software school student brittany garrett current student at nashville software school treat an unconventional career path as a unique asset, not something to hide from employers if you are a people person, that skill transfers well into tech roles that need collaboration across teams go into hiring conversations with an open mind and set aside assumptions about someone's background network by reaching out on linkedin, introducing yourself, and following up to ask for a real conversation build a personal vehicle around something you love to consistently meet new people in tech ep 109"],["e108-w-bram-gruneir-software-engineer-at-cockroach-labs","episode 108 - w/ bram gruneir, software engineer at cockroach labs bram gruneir software engineer at cockroach labs if you get a job offer and feel pressured, take time to decide if you have the freedom to do so prep for interviews by practicing algorithms since many companies still expect live coding performance live coding interviews show debugging skills and real problem solving, not just memorized algorithms avoid filtering resumes by big name companies or schools since that bias can cause you to miss good candidates build side projects you are passionate about since that experience and initiative stands out to employers ep 108"],["e107-ama","episode 107 - ama   focus your job search on three basics first a strong resume, a solid linkedin profile, and a good headshot treat your resume as top of funnel and linkedin as bottom of funnel when marketing yourself on polywork use badges and dms to directly message people open to specific topics like hiring hiring managers should keep interviewing through the holidays since competition for talent slows down plan on roughly five to six weeks from first interview to start date when budgeting your hiring timeline ep 107"],["e106-w-ben-henderson-vp-of-engineering","episode 106 - w/ben henderson, vp of engineering ben henderson vp of engineering at celera health understand the real difference between management and leadership: management hits deadlines and kpis, leadership develops people and builds followers fix communication gaps by running regular company-wide updates so everyone knows why priorities are changing avoid judging engineers by lines of code or individual output, instead track whether the team is meeting its commitments and dig into why when it misses to hire fast in a tight market, consider contract-to-hire or part-time contractors to fill urgent gaps while you search for full-time talent job seekers should rely on warm network connections instead of cold outreach or mass job board applications since postings can pull in dozens of applicants overnight ep 106"],["e105-w-jose-mata-regional-talent-manager-at-slalom","episode 105 - w/jose mata, regional talent manager at slalom jose mata regional talent manager at slalom understand the difference between agency and internal recruiters since the relationship with the client changes how much influence they have on your offer if something is on your resume be ready to talk through the details, recruiters will ask about it build your skills on your own through things like github projects or affordable online courses to show you are moving toward the role you want if you worked a contract role, put that context on your resume with a short story explaining why it ended calculate contract pay by adding your benefits and pto into a lump sum and dividing by total yearly hours, not just cutting your salary in half ep 105"],["e104-w-stephon-parker-csm-engineering-manager-at-eventbrite","episode 104 - w/stephon parker, csm & engineering manager at eventbrite stephon parker csm and engineering manager at eventbrite before hiring junior engineers make sure your team has the structure and mentorship to support them or you risk setting them up to fail leaders can build buy in for hiring junior talent by showing it creates loyalty and grows future staff level and leadership talent from within directors and vps should regularly talk directly with individual contributors instead of relying only on managers to relay information ship work more often in smaller pieces and iterate rather than waiting until everything is finished before releasing it a good performance review or termination should never surprise an employee if the manager has been consistently honest about expectations ep 104"],["e103-w-harry-wolff-director-of-engineering-at-mongodb","episode 103 - w/harry wolff, director of engineering at mongodb harry wolff director of engineering at mongodb skill gets you in the door but timing and a little luck often decide if you land the role check how long a job has been posted since freshly opened roles can mean less calibrated expectations and an easier bar hiring managers often default to tools like react as an easy filter because its hard to evaluate soft skills at scale before moving into management expect to learn interpersonal conflict and non-technical skills you were never taught as an engineer expect the job search to take around six months and be ready for the emotional toll since even senior engineers find it draining ep 103"],["e102-w-alishah-novin-director-of-engineering","episode 102 - w/alishah novin, director of engineering alishah novin director of engineering at salero commerce skip take home coding tests and instead ask candidates to walk through a project they actually built in interviews ask what you were trying to build, what went wrong, and what you would change next time resumes still matter so put your programming languages and tools at the top followed by quantifiable experience avoid vague skill ratings like javascript four out of five since reviewers have no idea what your scale means include non tech jobs like customer service or serving on your resume if the skills are relevant to the role ep 102"],["e101-interview-feedback","episode 101 - interview feedback   expect no interview feedback most of the time since over half of interviews end with little to no response from hiring teams wait about 24 to 48 hours before following up with a recruiter and 48 to 72 hours before following up with a hiring manager always work through your recruiter instead of dming the hiring manager directly or you risk getting rejected on the spot email or text for follow ups since those get seen faster than dms or phone calls never bash a previous employer in an interview since getting defensive about feedback can get you rejected on the spot ep 101"],["e100-job-search-mentality","episode 100 - job search mentality   being willing to jump through interview hoops like coding evals or extra rounds sets top candidates apart staying kind and positive with recruiters and hiring managers goes further than being negative or defensive treat rejection as a new connection that could lead to an opportunity down the road flipping your mindset from dreading the job search to being curious and eager changes how interviews go sending a genuine thank you note after an interview, even a bad one, shows gratitude that pays off later ep 100"],["e99-what-to-give-when-remote-is-not-enough","episode 99 - what to give when remote is not enough   set a clear pto policy instead of vague unlimited pto so employees actually know what they get build a defined mentorship program for junior devs instead of leaving them to figure it out alone let employees use part of their time for side hustles or volunteering since it can boost motivation at work give spot raises so people don't have to quit just to get paid fairly consider offering things like a work from home budget, childcare support, or paid parental leave to stand out ep 99"],["e98-dealing-with-rejection","episode 98- dealing with rejection   if you haven't heard back from a recruiter, reach out and simply ask for a yes or no either way recruiters often sit on bad news because they struggle to deliver it, so following up helps both sides rejection usually reflects fit or timing, not your worth, so try not to take it personally keep taking interviews even when you're not actively looking so rejection stops feeling so heavy building real relationships instead of mass applying can cut down how often you face rejection ep 98"],["e97-building-community","episode 97- building community   pick one real passion and build a small group around it instead of trying to network in general start simple with meetup.com, facebook, linkedin, slack, or discord groups to give people a place to gather set a consistent schedule like once a month so your community stays active without burning you out use a shared interest to turn a cold dm into a real relationship instead of a generic networking ask if you are struggling to find a job or make friends, building a community around something you love can open doors ep 97"],["e96-take-home-tests","episode 96 - take home tests   long take home tests hurt retention more than they help, especially when candidates already have jobs and families ask better interview questions instead of leaning on hours of take home code tests to evaluate someone live coding or screen share problem solving together is a fair way to assess skills without a take home burden keep the process tight, aim for around three rounds within two weeks with fast turnaround on scheduling and feedback build experience by volunteering a few hours a week on a real project and adding that work to your resume ep 96"],["e95-q-a-and-state-of-the-market","episode 95 - q&a and state of the market   offer top candidates more than what was submitted to build instant trust and buy-in if you can't offer more money, get creative with personalized gifts like swag or a welcome book candidates are moving fast right now so hiring managers should start hiring well before they actually need someone sift through noise with empathy since both job seekers and hiring managers are overwhelmed right now a shorter, simpler interview process respects candidates already working long hours and juggling family life ep 95"],["e94-w-james-quick-javascript-developer-dev-advocate","episode 94 - w/james quick, javascript developer & dev advocate james quick javascript developer & dev advocate build real projects instead of just memorizing for tests, that is how programming actually clicks programming skills can lead to roles beyond coding, like developer advocacy, if you have interests in speaking or content networking works best when it comes from genuine relationships, not transactional introductions communication skill is what moves developers into senior and architect level roles, not just coding ability start creating content now even if it is rough, consistency over time builds career opportunities and credibility ep 94"],["e93-from-dinosaur-to-the-digital-age","episode 93 - from dinosaur to the digital age alex dale runs the technology practice at vaco build real relationships with recruiters instead of treating hiring as purely transactional a good recruiting partner should push back and explain their reasoning, not just nod along virtual meetings are fine but face to face or video still matters most early in a relationship be honest about expectations and capabilities on both the candidate and client side the job market is moving fast so slow down, think through your plan, and avoid chasing money blindly ep 93"],["e92-the-intangibles-of-the-job-search","episode 92 - the intangibles of the job search   avoid venting or being negative on social media since hiring managers can see your public comments and activity reply to rejection emails with gratitude since it shows your character to the hiring manager being kind after a rejection can put you first in line if the top candidate falls through send a thank you message or linkedin note right after interviewing to stay top of mind focus on building deeper relationships with fewer people instead of spreading yourself thin across many contacts ep 92"],["e91-counselor-2-0-w-danny-thompson-dthompsondev","episode 91 - counselor 2.0 w_danny thompson (@dthompsondev) danny thompson @dthompsondev build your linkedin profile first since hiring managers and recruiters spend their time there, not just on job boards message hiring managers directly with three clear reasons why they should talk to you instead of only relying on applications volunteer for nonprofits or churches to build real portfolio projects since that experience never expires and also builds your network skip generic clone projects like an instagram clone and build something unique you can explain in detail during interviews tailor your resume to match each job posting's key terms since ats software filters out resumes that dont hit enough keywords ep 91"],["e90-youre-fired","episode 90 - you're fired   get your resume and linkedin professionally written before anything else in your job search try to meet or talk with one recruiter a day over coffee or lunch and ask who is hiring look for recruiters with at least two years at their company and two years of recruiting experience when you find a good job posting, bcc a few recruiters you trust and ask if they know the company treat a full time job search as an actual full time job focused on relationships, not just applying ep 90"],["e89-finding-a-mentor","episode 89 - finding a mentor   go after mentorship yourself instead of waiting for a mentor to reach out to you connect with people you admire in your field on twitter and linkedin, then follow, comment, and like their posts before asking for anything when you reach out be specific about what you want to learn instead of just asking someone to mentor you in general offer something of value or help first so you end up in that person's circle before asking for mentorship come prepared with good questions and drive the conversation yourself instead of expecting the mentor to lead it ep 89"],["e88-giving-notice","episode 88 - giving notice   approach giving notice with empathy, thank your employer, then stop talking instead of over explaining be very wary of a counteroffer since it rarely fixes the real reason you want to leave give more notice the higher up or more technical your role is, with four weeks as a common cap offer to document your work or stay on contract part time to help smooth the transition know and be ready to explain your real reasons for leaving, even if you just got a raise or promotion ep 88"],["e87-virtual-interview-etiquette","episode 87 - virtual interview etiquette   set up your interview space with a clean well lit background instead of a messy room get a 4k or 1080p camera a ring light and a basic usb mic to look and sound better on calls position your ring light above you instead of directly in your face for better lighting keep your camera on by default during interviews and work calls to show you are engaged skip fake virtual backgrounds since they tend to look unnatural and distracting ep 87"],["e86-how-to-find-a-recruiter","episode 86 - how to find a recruiter   search linkedin for technical recruiter plus your city to find relevant recruiters fast check a recruiter's connection count and years of experience before reaching out, aim for at least two years in the industry look for personality in a recruiter's profile, not just their title or company when messaging a recruiter use flattery, a specific time ask like seven minutes, and one clear question build relationships with recruiters before you need a job, then set reminders to check back in every few weeks ep 86"],["e85-take-a-breath","episode 85 - take a breath   if you have a current job, narrow your search to a few real-fit opportunities instead of chasing everything at once be upfront with employers if you need more time to decide, asking for a week is reasonable always ask questions in interviews so you know what the job is really like before accepting after interviews send a quick thank you message so you land back at the top of the hiring manager's inbox journal what you think went wrong after each interview so you can track patterns and improve ep 85"],["e84-linkedin-overview","episode 84 - linkedin overview   make your banner photo count since its the first thing viewers see on your linkedin profile at minimum include your email in your banner since linkedin hides contact info behind a click skip putting your resume in the featured section so people dm you directly instead put specific keywords like exact languages or frameworks in your title since recruiters search by title not by scrolling profiles add real numbers and comparisons to your accomplishments instead of vague claims like increased user retention ep 84"],["e83-hitting-the-high-points","episode 83 - hitting the high points   start your resume summary broad then get specific, naming your top years of experience and main tech skills write your resume in project format under each job, naming the project and listing responsibilities as bullet points keep summaries to about five or six bullet points covering your most relevant tech if changing industries, rewrite past jobs in plain terms with numbers so someone outside your field understands your impact build one to three targeted resumes for the specific roles you want instead of one generic version ep 83"],["e82-w-jerome-hardaway-sr-engineer-microsoft-founder-of-vets-who","episode 82 - w_jerome hardaway, sr. engineer @ microsoft & founder of vets who code jerome hardaway sr. engineer at microsoft and founder of vets who code push for remote first if you want to attract top talent, since people are not moving cities just for a job ask companies to back up in office mandates with real data instead of vague feelings about collaboration build community instead of culture on remote teams by keeping conversations public and minimizing private channels add value before you ask for anything, whether raising money, networking, or trying to get hired build and show real projects instead of just a portfolio, since demonstrated work speaks louder than claims ep 82"],["e81-hiring-strategy","episode 81 - hiring strategy   if your hiring process takes longer than two weeks, something in it is broken let recruiters handle scheduling and logistics so interviews move fast, not just sourcing candidates give candidates specific feedback like three bullet points on strengths and gaps instead of vague rejections if a great candidate is not right for the role you interviewed them for, push to find them another fit in the company as a candidate, ask directly at the end of an interview how to follow up and what the next steps are ep 81"],["e80-joe-karlsson-sr-developer-advocate-mongodb","episode 80 - joe karlsson, sr. developer advocate @ mongodb joe karlsson sr. developer advocate at mongodb senior devs often code less because they create bigger value coaching teams and unblocking work than writing every pr themselves leaders should ask senior engineers what they actually want instead of assuming everyone wants to stop coding or start managing promoting someone just because they write great code can create a manager who is weak with people, aim to grow both skill sets when building teams, favor mostly internal promotions with some fresh outside hires to balance stability and new ideas junior engineers get overlooked because companies fear training costs, but a strong onboarding structure lets juniors scale senior output too ep 80"],["e79-job-search-thoughts","episode 79 - job search thoughts   before an interview write down specific accomplishments tied to the exact role so you do not blank when asked treat interviews as a dialogue and ask your questions during the process instead of saving them for after an offer when asked about salary expectations give a straight answer with a no brainer number and a real minimum instead of dodging skip easy apply and quick apply buttons and instead connect directly with engineers, managers, directors and vps on linkedin when messaging a hiring manager ask for a few minutes to learn about the company instead of asking about a specific open role ep 79"],["e78-why-not-hire-junior-developers","episode 78 - why not hire junior developers?   companies default to hiring senior devs because clients ask for senior talent, not because juniors cant do the job if you dont have time to train new hires you are by definition understaffed juniors who get trained and given a chance tend to stay longer than senior devs who get poached constantly hire for talent and long term fit with the organization, not just for one specific role always hire one person ahead of your current need instead of waiting until youre in a crunch ep 78"],["e77-guidance-counselor-2-0-w-eric-yager-software-development","episode 77 - guidance counselor 2.0 w/eric yager, software development manager at amazon eric yager software development manager at amazon put boot camp capstones and side projects on your resume framed as real experience, not hobby work pick side projects that show full backend depth plus some frontend, since full stack work leans mostly backend expect data structures and algorithms questions in big tech interviews even if you rarely use them day to day practice talking about yourself out loud since interview skills and keeping an interviewer engaged take real reps if you cant land your target role at your target company, get in through an adjacent role and transfer internally later ep 77"],["e76-counselor-2-0-w-jason-lengstorf-vp-of-developer-experience","episode 76 - counselor 2.0 w/jason lengstorf, vp of developer experience at netlify jason lengstorf vp of developer experience at netlify schedule company wide days off instead of relying on people to use individual pto during burnout periods compete on lifestyle and work life balance when you cannot match big company salaries give senior engineers real time off since losing them costs you mentoring capacity for junior talent ask story based interview questions like tell me about a conflict with a coworker to spot red flags early batch interview stages close together so you do not lose candidates to faster moving companies ep 76"],["e75-how-to-use-twitter-to-network","episode 75 - how to use twitter to network   set a clear banner photo and profile picture on twitter so people instantly know who you are write a short bio that tells visitors exactly what you post about and why they should follow add an about me style intro video to your profile so people connect with you before they follow use twitter lists and the search feature to quickly find job posts and people worth following engage by liking and commenting on posts since that kind of networking has led to real dm conversations ep 75"],["e74-hiring-for-potential","episode 74 - hiring for potential   be brutally honest with recruiters about what you can and cannot do before entering an interview process practice explaining your thought process out loud since communicating how you solve problems matters more than reaching the right answer get comfortable saying i don't know during interviews instead of pretending to know everything if you are a hiring manager rejecting people for missing a few skills that gap often points to weak training and mentorship in your organization look for companies that let you choose between live coding and take home tests since flexibility helps more candidates succeed ep 74"],["e73-q-a","episode 73 - q&a   never message someone at a company just to ask about a job they posted, it feels disingenuous when reaching out, use admiration, ask for a specific amount of time, and bring one specific question build your brand before you are job searching, not during, so it does not feel forced or desperate how a company treats candidates through the interview process reflects directly on its culture and brand one genuine conversation at a conference or coffee shop can completely change your career trajectory years later ep 73"],["e72-why-finding-your-why-is-important","episode 72 - why finding your why is important   write down your why on pen and paper before accepting any job offer if an offer comes in fast, tell the company you are flattered but still interviewing and ask for a deadline recruiters respect candidates who communicate honestly about continuing their search know what you want to fix in your next job, like pay or promotion track, before you jump few people stay at one company for ten years anymore, so dont put too much pressure on a single decision ep 72"],["e71-hiring-better","episode 71 - hiring better   hire your leadership team first before bringing on a wave of developers at a small company or startup agree on rate expectations with your recruiter upfront so you are not backtracking after an offer goes out do not reject a candidate solely because of resume formatting or typos, talk to them first to see if they have the skills stay open to contract, contract to hire, and direct hire options since the tight market wont always give you the perfect direct hire candidate stick to the interview process you promised a candidate, adding extra rounds damages trust and makes candidates think the company is dragging them along ep 71"],["e70-okay-to-vacay","episode 70 - okay to vacay   track your pto usage and push yourself to actually take the time off you have earned unlimited pto policies can backfire since people often take less time off without clear guidelines if you are a 1099 or w2 contractor build time off into your hourly rate since you have no paid pto a workplace that frowns on taking time off is a sign of poor culture worth moving on from notice when you feel drained or unsettled at work since that is a signal you need time off ep 70"],["e69-back-to-the-basics","episode 69 - back to the basics   update your resume first, focused on project work, before anything else in the job search build out your linkedin presence since recruiters and hiring managers check it to judge your validity use job board sites like dice and indeed and log in daily to trigger recruiter search visibility find recruiters who have been at the same company for at least two years before dming them when dming recruiters use flattery, a specific ask, and a short time request like six minutes ep 69"],["e68-how-to-hire","episode 68 - how to hire   if a company wants a six month guarantee on a hire, push for contract to hire instead of direct hire contract to hire works as a working interview since six months reveals more than a two hour interview ever could use contracting to fill specific skill gaps or projects, use consulting when you need someone to manage an entire project for you sell the opportunity in the first 15 to 20 minutes of an interview before diving into technical questions keep the hiring process to two interviews within two weeks or you risk losing the candidate ep 68"],["e67-what-developers-want","episode 67 - what developers want   offer developers both a leadership track and an individual contributor track so strong coders are not forced into managing people let senior individual contributors own a specific product area and keep advancing without becoming a manager build mentorship opportunities into career growth since many developers want to teach without taking on full leadership give real affirmations and specific guidance instead of vague criticism to help retain your team let leaders step back to an individual contributor role after burning out without treating it as a failure ep 67"],["e66-guidance-counselor-2-0-uf-follow-up","episode 66 - guidance counselor 2.0 + uf follow up   work with a recruiter for your first move from direct hire to contracting so you have someone guiding you through the client relationship ask upfront whether a contract role is set at 40 hours a week or could require more contracting means you get paid for every hour worked, unlike a direct hire salary where extra hours do not add pay when a contract is ending, most of the time there is notice in advance so you can plan your next move the tech job market is strong right now, so if you want to try something new it is a safe time to make a change ep 66"],["e65-how-to-actually-write-a-resume","episode 65 - how to actually write a resume   make your resume summary clear and concise with exact years of experience for each major skill so recruiters know what you do immediately only bold company name, position, location, and year on your resume since heavy bolding distracts more than it helps write professional experience as project based, describing each project at a high level like you would explain it to family list the specific tech used to solve each project problem, then add a technologies used section recapping your full tech stack put a cover letter in your own voice and make it quantitative about projects and results instead of repeating your resume ep 65"],["e64-w-nate-taylor-on-leadership-within-software-development","episode 64 - w/nate taylor on leadership within software development nate taylor director of software development at pluralsight leadership does not require a title, leading an epic or owning a deliverable counts as real leadership experience before promoting someone into management, sit down and ask if they actually want to manage people instead of assuming companies often promote their best coders into leadership without checking if they have the aptitude or desire for it build dual tracks so engineers can grow into deep technical expert roles without being forced into management to break into software development, pick a real problem you care about and build something, then research issues as they come up ep 64"],["e63-w-christopher-choyce-on-scaling-teams","episode 63 - w/christopher choyce on scaling teams christopher choyce former cto of vaynermedia when scaling hiring fast, pick one or two key attributes that predict success and screen hard for those build a full structured onboarding plan so new hires are not just thrown into chaos on day one hire for soft skills like empathy and communication since those are harder to teach than technical tools contract to hire works well as a mutual trial period and avoids the awkward process of letting someone go if you ask candidates to do unpaid take-home tests, consider paying them for their time and effort ep 63"],["e62-contract-to-hire-recruiter-ama","episode 62 - contract to hire & recruiter ama   push your manager for direct feedback instead of waiting for them to bring it up leaders should block time on their calendar specifically to check in with their team if your resume covers years at one company, keep it to two or three pages and highlight renewals and high impact projects cut fluff phrases like great communicator from your resume and let your project history speak for itself job seekers should stay open to contract, contract to hire, and direct hire roles since the right pay and benefits can make any of them worth it ep 62"],["e61-contracting-and-contract-to-hire","episode 61 - contracting and contract to hire   know your full compensation package including base, bonus, pto, and 401k match before comparing it to a contract hourly rate ask recruiters to build pto and two weeks of bench pay into long term contracts since they wont offer it unless you ask build relationships and create content around the team or department you want to move into for internal transfers bring up your performance reviews and market research on pay when negotiating a contract to hire conversion being kind, communicative, and reliable on a contract job greatly increases your odds of getting converted to full time ep 61"],["e60-the-market","episode 60 - the market   if your team is getting recruiter dms every week, sit down and learn what actually motivates each person to stay candidates can now bounce between offers without the reputation hit it used to carry, but acting that way still has long term costs if you commit to an offer for a candidate, dont lowball them later, it just pushes them to keep looking junior devs can build real experience through unpaid intern or volunteer work instead of spending money on bootcamp or coursework hiring managers should stop expecting every skill on the list and instead hire for attitude and train up from there ep 60"],["e59-why-recruiters-do-things","episode 59 - why recruiters do things   ask a recruiter how many people they placed last year to gauge experience and track record ask if the recruiter has a specialty since focusing on one skill set leads to better matches than covering everything ask how a recruiter tracks candidates and whether they will stay in touch even if the current role does not work out if a company is getting a flood of resumes for one role the recruiting firm is not vetting candidates properly recruiters who take time to test and reference check candidates often lose placements to faster firms but it leads to better quality hires ep 59"],["e58-w-josh-carroll-working-for-free","episode 58 - w/josh carroll + working for free josh carroll cio at tag resources build one project from start to finish instead of following a tutorial, so you can explain every decision you made hiring managers sift through hundreds of resumes fast, so a generic bootcamp github project blends in and gets skipped record a short video walking through your side project and post it on linkedin or twitter to stand out putting in a little extra time consistently compounds over months into a much bigger skill gap than you expect skip fancy colored resume templates since they slow hiring managers down and rarely contain better information ep 58"],["e57-hiring-manager-and-job-seeker-strategy-right-now","episode 57 - hiring manager and job seeker strategy right now   job seekers should keep one or two trusted local recruiters plus three or four recruiters in other hot tech cities use short calls of three to ten minutes to quickly figure out which recruiters you actually like working with local recruiters give you eyes and ears in your city, so lean on them for local networking and reputation info hiring managers no longer need to use a recruiting firm in their own city since remote recruiting now works nationally expect candidate lead time of two to four weeks right now, especially for senior technical talent, so start the search early ep 57"],["e56-w-amanda-hughes-from-vaco-technology","episode 56 - w/amanda hughes from vaco technology amanda hughes vaco technology expect a tough first one to two years in recruiting since you hear no far more often than yes burnout happens when you treat the job as task volume instead of building real depth with clients focus on relationship based recruiting over transactional work to build trust and reduce candidate attrition hiring managers should move fast and make quick decisions since the market is highly candidate driven right now pay attention to interview process and speed since candidates are now turning down offers over slow or impersonal interviews ep 56"],["e55-recruiter-ama-cont","episode 55 - recruiter ama cont.   recruiters keep outreach messages vague on purpose so you reply and get pulled into their system toward a metric use a short list of screening questions when a recruiter dms you so you can weed out ones who know nothing about the role ask your recruiter if they have the manager or intake call notes to confirm the role was actually spec'd with the hiring manager if a recruiter is just reading prefixed questions off a script instead of having a conversation, that is a red flag check in with your usual recruiter about local openings even if they have not called you, they may already be working on it ep 55"],["e54-w-bill-isreal-at-built-technologies","episode 54 - w/bill isreal at built technologies bill israel engineering manager at built technologies grow your own seniors by training mid-level devs instead of only hiring senior talent externally aim for a balanced team mix so you are not too top heavy with seniors or too bottom heavy with juniors let senior people own their decisions and use yourself as a sounding board rather than micromanaging for junior devs let them struggle for a bit on their own before stepping in to help, but dont let them stall for days in interviews honesty beats bluffing, say you dont know something and show enthusiasm to learn instead of guessing ep 54"],["e53-emergency-live-about-hiring","episode 53 - emergency live about hiring   if you cant find candidates right now you are probably not offering full remote or not paying enough expect lead times of two to three or even four weeks to fill open developer roles in this market consider paying above typical recruiting fee rates if you want to land top candidates quickly if youre struggling to find senior or principal engineers consider hiring and training up mid level or junior talent instead job seekers should stay organized, avoid being submitted to the same company twice, and check in consistently with recruiters ep 53"],["e52-w-byron-sommardahl-how-to-interview-better","episode 52 - w/byron sommardahl & how to interview better byron sommardahl cto at acorn avenue and hero unit algorithmic tests mostly show what someone recently studied, not real job skill for most developer roles pair programming through a coding kata with test driven development reveals how someone actually thinks and solves problems being able to teach or explain your code during an interview signals strong communication skills developers need emotional intelligence since poor communication can lead to solving the wrong problem for a client if being yourself hurts you in interviews, work on your emotional intelligence instead of just your technical skills ep 52"],["e51-w-alishah-novin-director-of-engineering-celero-commerce","episode 51 - w/alishah novin, director of engineering @ celero commerce alishah novin director of engineering at celero commerce use regular one-on-ones to talk about growth, not just performance, and push for that conversation every other week at worst treat fear of changing jobs as useful information rather than something to ignore, it usually means something needs attention read widely outside your day to day work so you notice what skills and tech are moving on without you tie your growth ask to a realistic timeline like a year or two instead of expecting a big jump in six months get involved in local tech communities and slack groups to see where you stand and get honest feedback on gaps ep 51"],["e50-the-importance-of-recruiting-companies","episode 50 - the importance of recruiting companies kyle allen head of the vaco office a good recruiter does more than find talent, they also push the process to get you to the finish line with offers and onboarding if your hiring process moves too slow in a hot market, candidates and recruiters will move on to someone else recruiters often know about company moves and market shifts before people inside those companies do vet a recruiter by checking their tenure, online presence, and whether you actually click with how they work keep a relationship with one or two local recruiters plus a few more outside your area since remote work is here to stay ep 50"],["e49-recruiter-ama","episode 49 - recruiter ama   when a recruiter dms you, ask them to answer at least half of a short list of screening questions before engaging further only invest time in recruiters who have been at their company and in the industry for at least two years if you dont want more job pitches from a recruiter just stop responding instead of saying no thanks junior devs stand out with a clean consistent resume listing specific tech like react and node instead of vague acronyms before accepting a contract to hire role ask the recruiter if its a true contract to hire and get the conversion salary in writing ep 49"],["e48-dealing-with-recruiters","episode 48 - dealing with recruiters   if a recruiter dms you a role that doesn't fit, send screening questions back or just don't respond and move on recruiters get paid by companies through commission, not by candidates, so never pay a recruiter for help finding a job if there's no job posting for an interview, ask the recruiter for the actual intake notes from the hiring manager practice your elevator pitch out loud and record yourself to catch habits and improve how you sound in interviews to find fair pay, research ranges and talk openly with trusted peers in your field instead of relying on vague titles like junior or senior ep 48"],["e47-w-aaron-hurt-from-amazon-on-interviewing","episode 47 w/aaron hurt from amazon on interviewing aaron hurt systems development engineering manager at amazon amazon's interview loop can run five hours total and may be split across multiple days instead of one sitting each loop interviewer focuses on specific leadership principles or competencies, so expect behavioral questions tied to past actions amazon aims to respond within two business days after initial contact and within five business days after loop interviews match your resume language to the job description, since missing keywords like a specific os or tool can keep you from getting a phone screen avoid vague words like expert or massive on your resume or in interviews, be specific and ready to back up any claim you make ep 47"],["e46-marketing-the-job-search","episode 46 - marketing & the job search   treat your job search like marketing by staying top of mind through consistent daily posting or engagement post one piece of content regularly to document your job search or career instead of staying silent comment and engage on other people's posts daily, it builds relationships that lead to referrals and job leads skip putting your resume directly on your linkedin profile so people have to message you and start a conversation write or blog about a topic you care about, even a niche one, since small posts can reach a surprisingly large audience over time ep 46"],["e45-review-week","episode 45 - review week   run regular performance reviews because they build trust and give people a safe place to raise issues if a team feels disconnected, check how long it has been since the last review be direct in reviews instead of softening feedback, people respect clear honest communication more stay consistent with check ins and reviews over time, results take years to show but they do show as an employee, come to reviews with energy, bring your wins, your struggles, and your direction for the future ep 45"],["e44-stop-messing-up-interviews","episode 44 - stop. messing. up. interviews.   research the company before an interview, not knowing basic facts about them is a red flag ask questions about the role, including why it is open and who you would be working with try to interview directly with the person you would report to, not just their team members write down your accomplishments and relevant project examples ahead of time so you do not blank under pressure bring a notepad to jot down thoughts during the interview instead of interrupting the interviewer ep 44"],["e43-taking-free-work","episode 43 - taking free work   volunteering your time an hour a day can build real skills when you lack job experience treat volunteer work as professional experience and list it on your resume and linkedin reach out to nonprofits and churches since they often need web or digital help get comfortable asking people directly if you can help them with a project for free offering a small amount of free value upfront can help people see your worth and lead to paid work ep 43"],["e42-w-john-fly-director-of-se-at-smiledirectclub","episode 42 w/john fly, director of se at smiledirectclub john fly director of se at smiledirectclub define your company culture early, waiting until you feel the need for it usually means you are already in trouble avoid silos by making sure every new hire understands the company mission, not just their job role when hiring a highly skilled person, be clear in the interview about who the company is so they do not pull culture off track build a standardized interview process so every candidate is evaluated through the same steps and skills criteria skip the canned job description and think through why the role exists so you can speak to it honestly in the interview ep 42"],["e41-recruiting-ama","episode 41 - recruiting ama   ask a recruiter for contract vs direct hire, remote status, and salary range upfront instead of waiting for them to volunteer it ask a recruiter if the client is an ats or vms and whether they have direct contact with the hiring manager before investing time keep your resume to two or three pages and load it with the specific tech stack for the job you want use the correct technical terminology on your resume even if the job description has it wrong, since you are the professional to reach someone at a company you want to join, dm an employee directly and ask for a few minutes of their time instead of cold emailing ep 41"],["e40-recruiter-processes-and-testing","episode 40 - recruiter processes and testing   expect recruiters to test you and ask for references before submitting you to a client always ask your recruiter to share the actual test results with you, not just a pass or fail if you bomb a skills test, ask the recruiter to let you retake it rather than giving up stick with a small handful of recruiters you trust instead of testing with every recruiter you meet while in school or bootcamp, do free work for your network to gain real experience for your resume ep 40"],["e39-come-with-questions","episode 39 - come with questions   show up engaged to every call, chat, or video interview since each one is a unique interaction always come with questions instead of saying you have none when asked ask a recruiter how many people they placed last year to gauge how active they are ask a recruiter what their process looks like so you know what steps come next end every call by asking if they can introduce you to another recruiter or contact ep 39"],["e38-lets-hang","episode 38 - let's hang   instead of applying through a company website, find people you know on the company's linkedin page and dm them directly when you dm someone at a target company, ask for a quick virtual coffee to learn about the role rather than asking for a job build a small mock-up or proof of concept using the company's own product or api before reaching out use that mock-up as an excuse to message a developer advocate or senior engineer at the company for feedback creating something real for a hiring manager gets more attention than offering to work for free or sending a generic greeting ep 38"],["e37-w-joel-lalgee-content-creation-personal-branding-and","episode 37 w/joel lalgee & content creation, personal branding, and clubhouse joel lalgee human headhunter, helps recruitment agencies with personal branding on linkedin focus on quality over quantity in job search, a few deep connections beat mass applying every time commenting on other people's linkedin posts counts as content and can help you build real connections you lose out on jobs mostly to internal referrals, so network your way into becoming one tie personal stories into business lessons on linkedin, overcoming a struggle resonates more than a resume treat clubhouse as a way to start conversations, but always move the relationship to a real one on one call ep 37"],["e36-w-mustafa-khundmiri-navigating-twitter","episode 36 w/mustafa khundmiri & navigating twitter mustafa khundmiri entrepreneur and freelance writer treat cold emailing as a prospecting game, not a numbers game, to get better response rates focus on researching the right people instead of blasting the same message to thousands of contacts stop selling in your first outreach message and focus on starting a real relationship instead use twitter to showcase your skills publicly since people can start reading your work from day one follow and engage key hiring people on twitter for months before dming them about a job ep 36"],["e35-w-austin-schlessinger-austinschless-twitter-to-network","episode 35 w/austin schlessinger (austinschless) & leveraging twitter to network austin schlessinger strategy consultant and twitter content creator use twitter threads instead of single tweets so people can engage at any point in the story instead of waiting until the end build a content habit by writing short essays or threads consistently to develop both a writing skill and a visible portfolio of ideas when starting out on twitter, define your core principles, share examples of people doing it well, then tell stories around those ideas use twitter mainly to test ideas and network, since small but engaged niche communities matter more than follower count use linkedin for job searching but turn to twitter if you want to learn content creation, join startups, or build an online audience ep 35"],["e35-navigating-contract-to-hire-pt-2","episode 35 - navigating contract to hire pt. 2   ask a recruiter how long a contract role has been open since months of vacancy is a red flag always ask why the position is open, since turnover could be unrelated to the project or performance ask if the hiring manager has budget to convert contractors to full time and if they have done it before ask about conversion salary ranges so you are not lowballed when a contract converts to permanent if you lack experience, offer free or volunteer hours to build a portfolio and get references ep 35"],["e34-navigating-contract-to-hire-pt-1","episode 34 - navigating contract to hire pt. 1   ask recruiters how many people they have placed with this specific manager before taking a contract role ask if the contract position has a history of converting to full time or has that opportunity work with a recruiting firm that has been around for at least two years since turnover creates bad handoffs confirm you are a w2 employee of the recruiting firm, not a 1099 freelancer, when contracting ask if the recruiting firm offers benefits or can point you to other places to find them ep 34"],["e33-w-seth-alexander-the-state-of-candidates-in-a-remote-culture","episode 33 w/seth alexander & the state of candidates in a remote culture seth alexander software engineer at evidence care expect recruiter outreach to come through every channel, even emails not listed on your public profiles or linkedin set a personal filter for how much more pay would justify the risk of switching jobs before engaging with offers ask about compensation even when happy in your role so you know your market value for future raises or promotions location based pay is shifting as companies pay for the value an engineer provides, not where they live share market outreach data with your leadership so the company stays aware of retention risk and can respond with transparency ep 33"],["e32-how-to-find-a-recruiter","episode 32 - how to find a recruiter   use the linkedin search bar with terms like recruiter or technical recruiter plus your skill set, then filter by city consider skipping recruiters with under two years experience since it often takes about two years for a recruiter to find their footing in a market check a recruiter's linkedin profile first and reach out to ones who show personality rather than a boring profile always customize your connection note every time you message a recruiter on linkedin ask a recruiter you already trust for referrals to other good recruiters in your market or city ep 32"],["e31-about-me","episode 31 - about me!   treat your network like capital, posting consistently on linkedin can unexpectedly reach thousands of people starting in high volume cold calling teaches resilience even if you have no idea what you are selling at first specializing in one niche, like software development recruiting, helps you build real credibility over generalist recruiting speaking at local user groups and code camps is a low pressure way to start building a public reputation remember that behind every job search is a real person who is scared, not just a number to move fast past ep 31"],["e30-finding-your-why-in-the-job-search","episode 30 - finding your why in the job search   figure out your actual why before starting a job search instead of applying just to see what happens its fine if your why is simply chasing more money, just be honest about it and own that reason write down and rank your top priorities like money, title, work life balance, and commute before you search know your full compensation package including benefits and 401k costs before you start looking, not after ask about benefits earlier in the process instead of waiting until the offer stage to find out they are bad ep 30"],["e29-chat-w-webflows-quality-engineer-manager-josh-cypher-on","episode 29 - chat w/webflow's quality engineer manager - josh cypher - on hiring josh cypher quality engineering manager at webflow global remote hiring means you compete on pay with candidates everywhere, not just your local market location based pay tiers can lose you good candidates once remote work becomes common instead of rare before planning a return to office ask if the job truly requires a body in a seat every day think about succession planning for hires who may only stay a short time during uncertain job markets in interviews explain your reasoning out loud since communicating how you think matters as much as the right answer ep 29"],["e28-gone-are-the-days-of-staying-at-one-company-forever","episode 28 - gone are the days of staying at one company forever   its okay to job hop, gone are the days of staying at one company your whole career if you have a short job stint, still put it on your resume and be ready to explain why it ended in interviews, tell a clear story about why you left a job instead of just saying it wasnt good the average job duration people see is around a year and a half to two years staying at one company for many years is fine, but you need to keep innovating and show your title changes and growth on your resume and linkedin ep 28"],["e27-communication-is-the-key-to-culture","episode 27 - communication is the key to culture   set expectations with your manager early about time off so there are no surprises later ask new employers what their expectations are for after hours communication before you accept over communicate during the workday so your team always knows where you are pick up the phone or hop on video instead of letting tension build over slack or dm keep your camera on during calls to build rapport and trust with your team ep 27"],["e26-recruiter-specialization-w-jonah-dimeo","episode 26 - recruiter specialization w/jonah dimeo jonah dimeo cyber security recruiter at vaco (morgan franklin), charlotte ask a recruiter directly if they specialize in your field before working with them on your search interview the recruiter as much as they interview you to see if they actually understand your tech stack check a recruiter's linkedin connections to see if most are in your field, that signals real specialization if you're a junior developer, ask recruiters what skills show up on placed candidates' resumes that you're missing aim to work with a recruiter who has been at the same company and in your local market for at least two years ep 26"],["e25-why-do-recruiters-ghost","episode 25 - why do recruiters ghost   recruiters often ghost because they are juggling hundreds of candidates and heavy admin work, not because they do not care follow up with your recruiter yourself, do not assume they will always reach out first check in once or twice a week for the first few weeks after meeting a recruiter, then ease off text or email recruiters instead of calling since most are in meetings all day and will not pick up remember the hiring company pays the recruiter, not the job seeker, so communication is not always guaranteed ep 25"],["e24-stop-messing-up-interviews","episode 24 - stop messing up interviews   research the company before any interview so you can actually answer what they do take phone interviews in a quiet place and have your resume in front of you for talking points keep pen and paper handy during interviews to jot down questions so you dont forget them write down key accomplishments tied to the specific role before you go into the interview skip the thank you letter and instead send a personalized connection note on linkedin after the interview ep 24"],["e23-networking-101","episode 23 - networking 101   when you dm someone new, ask for a specific short amount of time and name the exact topic you want to discuss stop trying to sell yourself at networking events and instead get genuinely curious about the other person at conferences, pick a handful of people you actually want to connect with and leave once you have met them treat networking as making friends, not making a pitch, and keep outreach messages short and real if you want to get serious about using linkedin for networking, cut distracting apps and put your focus there instead ep 23"],["e22-chatting-with-a-vp-of-engineering-on-offers","episode 22 - chatting with a vp of engineering on offers jack evans vice president of engineering at evidence care give a specific salary number instead of a vague range so hiring managers know where you actually stand know your full compensation picture upfront, including benefits, bonus and 401k match, before entering negotiations if a candidate asks for much more than expected, treat it as a signal to review your team's pay, not a red flag on the candidate come prepared with research and clear expectations, since that preparation signals how you will perform on the job once you accept an offer, don't renege if something better comes along, since that damages trust with the employer ep 22"],["e21-how-to-navigate-job-postings","episode 21 - how to navigate job postings   before submitting your resume to a job posting, try bcc'ing recruiter contacts and asking if they have connections or are working that role if you lack contacts at a company, go ahead and submit your resume but also reach out to the hr manager or recruiter to ask about next steps remote policies keep shifting, so apply to onsite roles in a city you want to move to and use a remote contract period to slowly research and relocate job descriptions are often stuffed with keywords by hr, so only apply within your actual years of experience and skill level instead of chasing every listed requirement spending an hour messaging people at companies with open roles beats spending that same hour mass submitting resumes around town ep 21"],["e20-lessons-leading-in-a-pandemic-w-a-vaco-friend","episode 20 - lessons leading in a pandemic w/a vaco friend taylor roberts leads a team in raleigh doing sales and client delivery for technology pay attention to what people say with their hands and their hearts, not just their words start meetings by checking in on the person before jumping into tasks let go of controlling every detail and trust your team at a deeper level notice when team members start proactively checking in on each other and build that into a habit as a leader, talk to your people about where they want to go and align that with business strategy instead of just dictating direction ep 20"],["e19-how-to-find-a-job-with-a-job","episode 19 - how to find a job with a job   find recruiters on linkedin by searching the word recruiter and favor those with two years at their company and two years recruiting once you find a few good recruiters bcc them together on job leads so they all know what you are seeing ask every recruiter you talk to for one or two more recruiter contacts to keep expanding your network log into job board profiles like indeed and dice every single day since recent activity pushes you to the top of recruiter searches spend at least 15 to 30 minutes a day networking on linkedin by posting, commenting, and engaging to stay visible to your community ep 19"],["e18-never-take-a-counteroffer","episode 18 - never take a counteroffer   write down why you are job searching before you talk to your manager so counteroffers don't shake your resolve once your employer knows you are looking, they often plan to replace you at a lower cost later treat a new job offer like it matches your current salary unless money is truly the reason you are leaving research your salary band and talk to peers so you know what to counter with before negotiating one round of asking for more after an offer is fine, but asking repeatedly risks losing the offer ep 18"],["e17-what-makes-a-good-motivational-talk","episode 17 - what makes a good motivational talk   bring real energy to a new year pep talk or your team will feel flat and uninspired share personal stories about your own struggles so employees relate to you as a leader balance honesty about hard times with genuine positivity instead of dwelling on the past year keep new year talks clear and concise since long rambling meetings lose people's attention practice radical candor by being direct with specific numbers instead of vague statements ep 17"],["e16-how-not-to-be-creepy-in-the-dms","episode 16 - how not to be creepy (in the dm's)   before dming someone, comment on and engage with their posts for a while so you are not a stranger when you do dm, reference something specific the person did or posted instead of just saying hi or whats up use the jab jab jab right hook approach, give value through comments and likes before you ask for anything when someone changes their job status on linkedin, comment and dm them asking how they found that job to start a conversation dont open a recruiting or sales dm with claims about being different or promising huge results, just be yourself and stay low key ep 16"],["e15-walking-in-confidence-in-2021","episode 15 - walking in confidence in 2021   dont carry last years setbacks into this year as an excuse for a slow start confidence comes from knowing what you provide, not from feeling ready starting small, like recording yourself on your phone, can build toward doing something bigger and scarier later let others see your work instead of hiding it out of self doubt treat challenges as part of the process instead of using them as a reason to stay stuck ep 15"],["e14-time-management-discussion","episode 14 - time management discussion   clean out recurring calendar invites regularly to keep your schedule clutter free block off an hour or so each day in your calendar just to work your to-do list instead of letting it sit untouched try dragging emails straight into outlook to-do to turn messages into actionable tasks use voice memos to respond faster to long emails like resume critiques instead of typing everything out use color coded flags on emails to mark what needs a response, a follow up, or can wait ep 14"],["e13-what-is-culture","episode 13 - what is 'culture'   build culture around we not me, make team success the shared win instead of individual stats run short daily huddles, five to ten minutes, to get everyone aligned before the work starts create a safe space where people can be open and vulnerable without fear of judgment when someone brings you a problem, listen and affirm first before jumping to solve it promote an open door policy so your team feels comfortable bringing issues directly to you ep 13"],["e12-practical-tips-you-can-implement-to-find-a-job","episode 12 live - practical tips you can implement to find a job   before applying, bcc a few trusted recruiters on one email asking if they are working the role or know someone who is keep a separate job search email so personal messages never get mixed up with hiring managers track every application in a simple tool or spreadsheet with company, stage, and who submitted you always know which recruiter submitted you where so you are not duplicated or left in the dark use a scheduling link and ask for a short call like five minutes to make it easy for busy people to say yes ep 12"],["e11-working-with-a-recruiter-part-2","episode 11 live - working with a recruiter part 2   remember the company pays the recruiter, not the job seeker, so expectations should be set accordingly recruiters juggle many candidates at once, so check in regularly instead of assuming you are being ignored have grace with recruiters since a lot of paperwork and coordination happens behind the scenes after every meeting research your current pay, benefits and market rate before negotiating so you can talk real numbers with a recruiter be firm on your worth but stay flexible, since being too rigid on rate can cost you an otherwise good opportunity ep 11"],["e10-how-to-work-with-a-recruiter","episode 10 live - how to work with a recruiter   look for recruiters who hit the two and two rule, two years at their company and two years in your local market insist on a zoom call instead of just a phone call, since you would not trust a financial advisor you never saw face to face find one good recruiter, then ask them who else in the city does good work and get introduced bcc five recruiters you trust on a job posting you find and ask if they are working that role or have leads on it keep checking in if a recruiter goes quiet, since recruiters are busy and ghosting is usually not personal ep 10"],["e9-if-you-are-in-a-position-of-hiring-listen","episode 9 live - if you are in a position of hiring...listen.   trust your recruiting firm enough to move fast once they send you a candidate instead of running three more rounds of interviews recruiters typically charge around 20 percent of first year salary for average talent and 25 to 30 percent for top talent consider contract to hire as a six month working interview so both sides can see if its a fit before committing companies requiring full time on site work in tech right now will lose people to fully remote offers elsewhere if you insist on in office work for your team ask whether that reflects a lack of trust in your leadership ep 9"],["e2020-12-02-austin-cumberlander-live-finding-a-job-as-a-developer-by","austin cumberlander live + finding a job as a developer by putting out content austin cumberlander junior software developer post real projects with a short description of what you built and what you learned to start building visibility sharing your honest job search journey and feelings often resonates more than polished project updates end posts with a question to invite comments, that engagement signals and conversation help posts travel further reply to comments and engage with other people's posts or your own audience will stop engaging back stay consistent with posting and don't take it too seriously, focus on your goal instead of fear of judgment dec 2, 2020"],["e2020-11-17-3-things-to-do-when-you-are-laid-off","linkedin live episode 8 - 3 things to do when you are laid off   update your resume first and tell a story with project details instead of a long list of bullet points make sure your linkedin has an updated title, banner, and photo since it exists for recruiters and hiring managers to find you collect recommendations on linkedin to help you stand out from other applicants work with recruiters before applying elsewhere since they can do a lot of the heavy lifting for you get your resume uploaded to job boards and consider ones like dice and indeed for tech roles nov 17, 2020"],["e2020-11-16-importance-of-workplace-culture","linkedin live episode 7 - importance of workplace culture   workplace culture used to mean free beer and ping pong but that disappears once everyone works from home so culture has to come from somewhere else regular reviews are a sign of good culture so if you have not had one in years that is a red flag worth questioning treat one on ones as real conversations and not just strategy talks since sometimes people just want to connect explain the why behind new initiatives at work because skipping that breeds distrust instead of buy in when interviewing ask to speak with other team members about culture and request a second interview if you still are not sure nov 16, 2020"],["e2020-11-16-how-to-network-during-a-pandemic","linkedin live episode 6 - how to network during a pandemic   like and comment on posts in your field consistently since that is the new way to network stay on someone's radar by engaging with their posts long before you ever ask them for a favor post on linkedin daily and aim for two or three posts a day if you can if writing feels fake or salesy you can still network by liking commenting and dming people follow accounts in your field on instagram and use it strategically for your career, not just friends nov 16, 2020"],["e2020-11-13-tips-and-tricks-on-how-to-write-an-effective-resume","linkedin live episode 5 - tips and tricks on how to write an effective resume   early career resumes should include more detail, not less, so hiring managers see everything you have done tailor your resume to the specific job you want to get called for instead of leaving in unrelated old experience replace vague bullet points with specific project details and quantitative results like team size or timeline give comparative stats when switching industries so hiring managers can see drive and performance in a new context use clean spacing and reasonable font size instead of bolding everything so your resume is easy to read in seconds nov 13, 2020"],["e2020-11-12-rich-rader-principal-engineer-becoming-an-effective-leader","rich rader principal engineer + becoming an effective leader rich rader principal engineer influencing people matters more than being technically right, so learn to understand what others actually want before pushing your point read how to win friends and influence people and the 21 laws of leadership to build real influence instead of just telling people they are wrong give team members slightly more responsibility than they would ask for themselves, framed as growth and backed by support, not added stress protecting your team sometimes costs you personally, so decide early whether you will defend your people even if it affects how you look to bosses pair juniors and seniors as mentors for each other so growth keeps moving up and down the ladder, not just top down nov 12, 2020"],["e2020-11-04-opening-doors-with-public-speaking","linkedin live episode 4 - opening doors with public speaking michael richardson senior software developer at kroger submit your first conference talk even if you expect it to get rejected, just to start the process treat public speaking as a long game, expect rejection and keep improving your submissions each time when picking a topic, think about whether it fits the audience and not just what you personally love talks generally fall into technical deep dives, high level overviews for managers and beginners, or people skills topics speaking and organizing conferences builds a network of organizers and speakers that opens up new opportunities nov 4, 2020"],["e2020-10-30-the-power-of-linkedin","linkedin live episode 3 - the power of linkedin   post on linkedin consistently since organic reach can expose your content to a much wider audience than your follower count suggests use hashtags strategically by thinking of them like google searches for the audience you want to reach challenge yourself to post daily, but even twice a week is far better than not posting at all share authentic, specific stories about your work instead of generic self help or get rich quick content stay patient since it may take months of consistent posting before a post takes off and builds your following oct 30, 2020"],["e2020-10-29-chris-marler-engagement-editor-for-saturdays-down-south","chris marler engagement editor for saturdays down south + building brand and being vulnerable over social media chris marler engagement editor for saturdays down south building a brand starts with knowing who you are and owning both the positives and the negatives expect public pushback when you put opinions online and learn to take the losses without quitting figure out your own lane instead of copying someone else's style, since there is room for different approaches being open about personal struggles and vulnerability helps people connect with your story more than a polished image stop comparing your timeline or salary to others, progress happens on its own schedule if you keep putting in the work oct 29, 2020"],["e2020-10-28-putting-yourself-out-there-w-drew-dawson","linkedin live episode 2 - putting yourself out there w/drew dawson drew dawson recruiter posting one short video about your background and interests can replace weeks of applying to jobs one by one a simple two minute video recorded on a phone with no editing or lighting can get strong response from recruiters and hiring managers if video feels uncomfortable try live blogging on linkedin or recording audio instead to put yourself out there watching yourself on video helps you clean up filler words and become a clearer communicator for real interviews a short intro video that covers background and strengths can double as practice for the common tell me about yourself interview question oct 28, 2020"],["e2020-10-26-putting-yourself-out-there","linkedin live episode 1 - putting yourself out there   document your job search process publicly instead of keeping it separate from your personal life post about a challenge you faced at work and how you overcame it to build engagement tag people in your posts so their followers see it too, expanding your reach keep videos short, aim for 90 seconds or less and no more than two minutes stay consistent posting and commenting for 90 days instead of quitting after a week oct 26, 2020"],["e2020-10-22-lessons-learned-in-leadership-during-a-pandemic","lessons learned in leadership during a pandemic kyle allen managing partner, nashville office ask people how they are doing and actually slow down to listen instead of rushing to fix problems stop over engineering check ins, sometimes a short unstructured one on one beats another scheduled group call own your mistakes publicly and apologize directly when you get something wrong with a team member give feedback by sharing both sides of a situation so the other person does not go on the defense redefine what matters during a crisis instead of excusing bad results or sticking to old metrics oct 22, 2020"],["e2020-10-15-blake-lower-the-hustle-of-freelancing","blake lower + the hustle of freelancing blake lower freelance web developer and founder of a freelance company treat goal setting as an honest conversation with yourself about what you actually want, not generic promises use facebook groups and meetup.com to find clients, introduce yourself directly and get on a call fast offer a smaller discounted scope first to build trust instead of overpromising on a big budget document your journey publicly on twitter or blogging to build a following and bring in freelance leads expect rejection in outreach, keep sending messages and follow ups, and move on quickly when someone goes quiet oct 15, 2020"],["e2020-10-08-recruiter-ama-w-glenn-stovall-and-production-ready","recruiter ama w/glenn stovall and production ready glenn stovall production ready follow up with your recruiter almost every day since they are juggling many candidates and job openings at once dont load your resume with skills you only dabbled in since recruiters can misread a side project as deep experience instead of mass applying, find the company on linkedin and message people on the actual team to get connected recruiters are paid a percentage of salary or a bill rate, so a strong relationship with one can protect your pay post one thoughtful piece of content on linkedin or twitter regularly since hiring managers and recruiters are watching those feeds oct 8, 2020"],["e2020-10-01-hacking-linkedin","hacking linkedin   use your linkedin cover photo to share contact info or a quick summary of what you do pick a profile picture with good lighting and a pop of color so you stand out in a sea of tiny thumbnails make your title specific with the exact tech or skill you use since recruiters search by keyword and skip vague titles like software developer keep your about section to two or three lines since linkedin only previews a short snippet before cutting it off spend time every day liking and commenting on posts since engagement through shared connections can get your profile and posts seen by hiring managers oct 1, 2020"],["e2020-09-24-alishah-novin-building-a-culture-around-vulnerability","alishah novin + building a culture around vulnerability alishah novin director of engineering at salatoro commerce a good manager helps with pay and growth while a bad manager undermines both, so invest in people before they consider leaving ask unconventional interview questions to see how candidates handle a weird situation, not to find one right answer code reviews feel vulnerable because you are laying your thought process out to be judged, which fuels imposter syndrome build trust by showing you understand people's life circumstances, then back off and avoid constant check-ins so they can focus during a tough job search stay true to your actual role and skills instead of taking an unrelated job out of desperation sep 24, 2020"],["e2020-09-17-laura-harvey-twitter-for-networking","laura harvey + leveraging twitter for networking laura harvey  start small on twitter by congratulating people on new jobs or birthdays to build real connections use the hundred days of code hashtag to stay accountable and share your coding journey daily keep hashtags relevant and minimal on twitter, unlike instagram where more hashtags can help don't compare your early posts to others since most people only share their highlight reel bring genuine enthusiasm to interviews, since attitude can matter as much as resume or portfolio sep 17, 2020"],["e2020-09-10-reid-evans-critical-thinking-and-how-to-separate-yourself-on","reid evans + critical thinking and how to separate yourself on the job search reid evans principal consultant at results stack position yourself as someone who solves problems instead of chasing a specific job title or w2 role in interviews, focus on communicating clearly and asking good questions rather than proving you are the best coder in the room recruiters should ask what projects a company has going on instead of just asking about open reqs for a specific tech stack a resume should tell a hiring manager what you do in 15 seconds, skip fancy fonts, columns, and headshots your most recent job or two should have real detail since that is what hiring managers focus on most sep 10, 2020"],["e2020-09-03-calvin-curry-how-to-navigate-the-job-search-as-a-junior","calvin curry + how to navigate the job search as a junior developer calvin curry  treat your capstone project as the bare minimum, not the finish line, when job hunting as a junior dev take on free work for nonprofits or small businesses to build a real portfolio while job searching list projects on your resume like real jobs, with a summary, tools used, and technologies for each stop mass applying and instead message alumni or employees at target companies to get warm introductions after any networking call always ask for a next step, like an intro to a manager or another developer sep 3, 2020"],["e2020-07-16-brandon-arvay-landscape-of-marketing-right-now","brandon arvay + landscape of marketing right now brandon arvay marketing agency owner, branded78, lexington kentucky post content without worrying about production value, especially when starting out on social media focus your limited time and budget on the one or two social channels where your actual audience spends time respond to every comment and message because community management is an overlooked part of marketing find businesses with a similar customer base and collaborate so both sides gain exposure to new people during tight budgets, try text message outreach since open rates run far higher than email jul 16, 2020"],["e2020-07-09-nashville-technology-council-panel-part-2","nashville technology council panel-part 2   know your one to three non-negotiables before you start a search so you can judge if an offer is worth taking target two networking meetings every single week to keep your pipeline full like a sales funnel leave past bad job experiences at the door in interviews since coming across bitter is never a good look tailor your resume to each posting but stay focused on one to two target roles instead of scattering across many for junior developers a few personal or free projects can separate you from others who only have bootcamp demo work jul 9, 2020"],["e2020-06-18-nashville-technology-council-panel-part-1","nashville technology council panel-part 1   use the two and two rule to vet recruiters, checking if they have been at their firm and in the market for at least two years send recruiters a list of ten screening questions, like whether a contract role has approved budget to convert to full time when you are unemployed, work with up to four recruiters at once since each one has their own book of business keep your resume detailed and results focused, while your linkedin should read as a shorter summary of that same story treat your job search like a full time networking job and commit to at least one intentional conversation every day jun 18, 2020"],["e2020-06-04-lunch-it-virtual-networking-group-may-edition","lunch it virtual networking group-may edition   never stop learning after a bootcamp, picking up projects keeps opportunities and growth coming never stop meeting people, most roles come through a personal connection rather than cold applying keep a blog or code pen of your work, even small posts can unlock career opportunities years later build personal projects or do free work to show initiative, the journey matters more than the finished product when networking on linkedin, check in on someone genuinely instead of pitching yourself, people respond better to that jun 4, 2020"],["e2020-05-21-how-to-hire-and-navigate-your-business-during-covid-with","how to hire and navigate your business during covid with alex belew alex belew chef and owner of dalts and jane in murfreesboro look for people with integrity, self-awareness, and a genuine desire to learn over raw talent or experience give candidates a trial shift so both sides can see if the fit and culture actually work handle performance or culture problems with a calm, private conversation instead of public conflict be honest and transparent with your community and staff about business struggles instead of hiding them stay creative and keep pivoting your offerings when your usual business model stops working may 21, 2020"],["e2020-05-14-what-does-recruiting-look-like-post-covid-19","what does recruiting look like post covid-19 chase tinkham business development at vaco, virginia beach office treat recruiting like a profession and position yourself as a market labor expert instead of apologizing for being a recruiter a video call already builds more connection than a phone call so use that as your starting advantage keep a running list of everyone you talk to and send a regular check in email so people know you did not forget them post one thoughtful piece of content a day so you stay top of mind when candidates are ready to move be upfront when you have nothing to offer but still help with resume or job search advice so the relationship outlasts any single role may 14, 2020"],["e2020-04-15-dont-know-what-to-do-in-this-market-neither-do-we","don't know what to do in this market? neither do we. jake and dan recruiters keep your resume updated now so you are ready when hiring picks back up check in with recruiters periodically but do not text every single day treat linkedin like a networking event, post thoughtful content and record a quick video about yourself stay patient and avoid pressing too hard, since desperation can hurt you in interviews consider free or discounted work to build your skill set and portfolio while the market is slow apr 15, 2020"],["e2020-04-03-personal-branding-with-jake-and-dan","personal branding with jake and dan jake and dan recruiters, vaco start building your personal brand now instead of waiting, like planting a tree today instead of years ago document your work and share real wins or lessons instead of trying to create perfect content be yourself online, don't copy other influencers, your own voice and passions build trust faster use social media to extend your reach if you're introverted so you don't burn out from constant networking find your own way to stand out, whether it's meetups, community boards, or something as simple as a signature style apr 3, 2020"],["e2020-03-27-job-search-tips-with-jake-and-dan","job search tips with jake and dan jake shepard and dan thompson senior director at vaco (raleigh) and vaco technology practice lead (tampa) update your resume now and tailor it with specific technical details, not vague soft skills claims use any downtime to learn new skills or certifications so you stand out when hiring picks back up skip the generic skills section and instead show real projects or github work to prove what you can actually do work with more than one recruiter and favor ones who have stayed at their company for several years know your own priorities in a job search, whether that's pay, team size, technology, or company culture, before you start interviewing mar 27, 2020"],["e2020-01-20-hot-jobs-for-january-20-24th","hot jobs for january 20-24th   16 dot net openings are active this week across the city for mid to architect level roles two true remote mid-level dot net contract-to-hire jobs pay around 48 an hour converting near 85k senior javascript architect roles are open at 140k, mostly remote, working in react node and aws south of nashville a fully remote senior dot net react direct hire role pays 120k and is open to candidates across the southeast a principal architect role needing php skills pays 160k if you have that specific background jan 20, 2020"],["e2019-12-13-interview-with-mary-haynes","interview with mary haynes mary haynes recruiter at vaco mary haynes moved from the music industry into tech recruiting with zero tech background and learned the terminology on the job when networking at events, aim to make one or two real connections instead of sticking with people you already know use the holiday season to prep your resume and research companies rather than waiting until january to start your search recruiters can help you avoid getting lost in automated application systems that companies use to screen resumes posting about your work or passions on linkedin can lead to unexpected opportunities since you never know who is paying attention dec 13, 2019"],["e2019-12-06-little-rock-tech-fest-2019","little rock tech fest 2019   partner with two or three recruiters and interview them using specific questions before trusting them with your job search stop submitting resumes directly to job postings since it disqualifies recruiters from working on your behalf use the two and two rule: look for recruiters who have stayed at their company two years and worked in the local market two years format your resume with a high level summary, specific project bullet points, and a full technology stack recruiters can search keep linkedin brief with three sentences per role covering the application, technologies used, and full tech stack dec 6, 2019"],["e2019-09-30-interview-with-chris-spintzyk","interview with chris spintzyk chris spintzyk direct placement recruiter, vaco before job hunting, figure out exactly what you want to do instead of just taking anything for a paycheck build a list of target companies, then work backwards to find a way in instead of applying online look for a recruiter who has been with the same firm and local market for at least two years work with two to three recruiters at most to get a well rounded view of the market without creating chaos stay in touch with your recruiter and update them on changes since the relationship works best as a two way street sep 30, 2019"],["e2019-09-12-music-city-code-2019","music city code 2019   screen recruiters with the two and two rule, two years in the market and two years at their current company work with only two or three recruiters at a time so they stay locked in step with you and don't price gouge you ask a recruiter how many people they have placed with a specific team before trusting their contract to hire pitch quantify your resume with real numbers and keep it to two or three pages instead of forcing everything onto one page structure your linkedin in three sentences, what you do in plain terms, your main technologies, then your full tech stack sep 12, 2019"],["e2019-08-12-code-on-the-beach-2019","code on the beach 2019   ask every recruiter or hr contact if a sign-on bonus or reload bonus is available before assuming there isn't one build your resume with a broad summary up top, specific bullet points, and a technical skills recap at the end rather than vague titles keep your resume a living document and update it to highlight the type of work you want more of, like ui projects if you're moving that direction work with no more than two or three recruiters at once and vet them using pointed questions about the role before trusting their job orders update your linkedin, dice, and indeed profiles with specific keyword-rich tech skills and remove old unprofessional content recruiters might find aug 12, 2019"],["e2019-07-18-devspace-2018","devspace 2018   work with a recruiter who has been at the same company at least two years and in the local market at least a year check a recruiter's linkedin for references from people who can speak to their technical ability ask a recruiter who their main clients are right now, even if they have no open job for you, to see if they really work your market check in with your recruiter every week since lack of communication is the biggest complaint, not lack of effort keep your linkedin open to opportunities, list your tech stack clearly, and consider building a presence on twitter to network with developers jul 18, 2019"],["e2018-09-11-scenic-city-summit-2018","scenic city summit 2018   look for recruiters who have worked at the same company for at least two years since frequent job hopping often signals poor performance ask any recruiter how they screen candidates and avoid working with ones who refuse to meet face to face or hop on a video call quantify your resume with real numbers and comparisons instead of vague phrases like good communication skills keep your linkedin and resume simple by listing only your main front end, mid tier and back end technologies rather than every tool you have touched stick to working with about two recruiters at a time and avoid mass submitting your resume to job postings since recruiters cannot represent you once you have applied directly sep 11, 2018"],["e2018-07-18-raleigh-net-user-group-meetup","raleigh .net user group meetup   avoid saying you crushed an interview, recruiters see that as a red flag that it actually went poorly make your resume specific and quantify results instead of vague lines like works well with others plan on a resume of three to five pages if you have real experience, not just one page only work with two or three recruiters at a time and pick ones who have stayed at the same company for several years start your job search before you get laid off so you have time to prep your resume and skills jul 18, 2018"],["e2018-06-05-music-city-code-2018","music city code 2018   meet your recruiter in person or at least on video before trusting them with your job search a good recruiter knows the basics of tech and stays active in the local developer community only work with two or three recruiters at a time so your resume does not get overexposed in the market look for recruiters who have stayed at the same company and in the same local market for at least two years build your network on twitter and linkedin and add side projects to your resume to stand out as a junior developer jun 5, 2018"],["e2018-05-21-hiring-for-success-from-a-tedx-speaker","hiring for success from a tedx speaker! john rouda it director and part-time professor at winthrop university when hiring, weigh personality and team fit as much as technical skill since skill can be taught find talent at local meetups by watching who speaks and who stays after to talk, not just who shows up expect anyone in a senior role to mentor others, and ask candidates how they have mentored before build a portfolio and a personal website with your own domain to stand out with no work experience yet make your resume font and design slightly different from generic templates so it catches a hiring manager's eye may 21, 2018"],["e2018-05-02-hiring-off-eq","hiring off eq brad miller and andrew may brad miller, director of operations and product ownership, and andrew may, ceo and lead architect, code tank labs for junior hires, watch how they react when stuck, saying i need help is a better sign than silently struggling for days build in a tough or unsolvable problem during interviews to see how candidates handle failure, not just success for senior and architect roles, let recruiters vet the technical skills first, then focus the in person interview on leadership ask about hobbies and life outside work, leading a kids sports team or community group shows leadership that is hard to fake if you are an introvert who codes alone, join a team or attend meetups regularly to build the people skills hiring managers look for may 2, 2018"],["e2018-03-13-the-madness-of-finding-a-job-for-a-junior-developer","the madness of finding a job for a junior developer thomas fitzgerald junior .net developer at nava health in nashville freelance web development experience alone can be a tough sell when employers see it as less credible than full-time work taking a non-developer role that still uses your design or coding skills can be a smart way into the industry code school fundamentals like github, command line, and agile matter more once you are on the job than they seem to in class getting dumped by a recruiter after sending your resume out everywhere can hurt your search, so stick with one or two reputable recruiters who follow through a warm introduction or mutual connection to a recruiter can lead to a much better experience than cold outreach mar 13, 2018"],["e2018-03-07-nashmicro-meetup","nashmicro meetup   partner with no more than two to three recruiters at a time instead of relying on just one check how long a recruiter has stayed at their current company, longer tenure usually means better market knowledge ask recruiters direct questions like who they know at the company and how long the role has been open give recruiters a clear rate or salary range instead of a single number so they can negotiate on your behalf be upfront and honest with recruiters about your interest level since lying makes it harder for them to place you well mar 7, 2018"],["e2018-02-16-contracting-vs-direct-hire","contracting vs. direct hire daniel oliver software developer at radio systems corporation expect recruiters to ask about contracting early since contract to hire is now a normal part of the process going independent cuts out the middleman but means handling your own taxes, benefits and paid time off w2 through a staffing company is usually easier since they handle taxes and paperwork for you being a career contractor is not a red flag by itself, but talking about your next move during an interview is no job is fully guaranteed so dont avoid a contract role if its the kind of work you actually want to do feb 16, 2018"],["e2018-02-01-relocation-and-social-media","relocation and social media kyle welch engineer at eventbrite when relocating for a job, find a recruiter who knows the local market and can advocate for your best interest beef up your online resume profiles since you wont have the benefit of local recognition when applying from out of state build genuine relationships at meetups and conferences so companies eventually reach out to you instead of only applying cold stay active on twitter and github so hiring managers can see your passion and side projects outside of work hours being open to feedback and able to disagree gracefully with other developers is a quality that helps you grow in your career feb 1, 2018"],["e2018-01-05-someone-other-than-me","someone other than me! david green software developer at netapp skip generic job boards like indeed, dice, and monster since leads rarely turn into real interviews and resumes get filtered by keyword systems look beyond typical meetups for networking, try reverse job fairs and talking directly with internal recruiters ask for a salary range early in the process so you avoid finding out you're 30 to 40k apart after a final interview invest in a professional resume and linkedin rewrite so recruiters start reaching out to you instead of you chasing every lead prep for interviews by researching the company and interviewer, practicing whiteboarding on sites like interview cake, and putting full effort into take-home assignments jan 5, 2018"],["e2017-12-07-why-meet-a-recruiter","why meet a recruiter   meet in person with a recruiter instead of relying only on phone calls when job searching partner with one or two recruiters you trust rather than spreading yourself across many visit the recruiter's office so you can also meet the team who may handle your opportunities know who is representing you by meeting both the recruiter and the sales side of the team dress business professional when meeting a recruiter, even though a full suit is not required dec 7, 2017"],["e2017-10-13-recap-of-past-episodes","recap of past episodes   use font size 10 for your resume and keep it one page if you are a recent grad or have a year of experience expand resume length as experience grows, two pages for three to four years and up to five pages max for very senior candidates post your resume on dice if you work in it, even if you are not a developer, since it does not require a dice profile to be technical keep your linkedin updated with a professional photo and two or three sentences per position summarizing your work pick a recruiter who has stayed at the same company more than two years and has at least one to two years of recruiting experience oct 13, 2017"],["e2017-08-08-recruiters-who-needs-them","recruiters. who needs them?   after posting your resume to job boards, the real next step is learning how to work with a recruiter look for a recruiter who has worked in your local market for at least one to two years avoid recruiters who job hop every year or two just to chase a salary bump favor recruiters who have stayed at the same company for an extended time, ideally two or more years a recruiter with both local market tenure and company tenure is worth a closer look aug 8, 2017"],["e2017-08-08-resume-writing-then-what","resume writing. then what?   if you work in it, put your resume on dice since only it professionals use it, which cuts down on noise know that recruiters search by last activity, so logging into dice to edit your resume can trigger calls even if you are not job hunting use career builder if you are outside it since it has a broad reach and is easy to use expect more recruiter contact the more job boards your resume sits on, so post selectively if you want fewer calls on linkedin use a professional photo and describe your job in one simple sentence plus a second sentence listing your technologies aug 8, 2017"],["e2017-07-18-youre-fired","you're fired.   when you're unexpectedly job hunting, start by rebuilding your resume since most people haven't touched it since college skip the one page resume rule if you have real experience, longer resumes can work for people with more years in the field make sure your resume has enough detail tied to the job description or employers will pass on it cut soft skills language like works well with others and replace it with quantitative data about your results use specific numbers like sales figures or revenue generated to show impact instead of vague job duties jul 18, 2017"],["e2017-07-01-about-me","about me.   recruiters get a bad reputation, so look for ones who actually know the tech they recruit for specializing in one space, like software development, helps a recruiter give better advice and connections getting involved in local developer meetups and conferences builds real relationships, not just job leads a recruiter who shifts from cold calling to in person relationships tends to understand candidates better don't just dismiss recruiters, learn how to use them well since they can actually help your search jul 1, 2017"],["e2017-06-30-goals-for-this-podcast","goals for this podcast   expect practical job search content instead of slides or scripted decks episode content grows from live q&a sessions taylor has run at tech conferences upcoming topics include how to write a strong resume learn how to work with recruiters from someone who does it for a living goal of the show is to make job transitions easier for people in the search process jun 30, 2017"]];
